        StaticBook("* Note *", "Othello", "A wouldnt suggest ignoring me again, sir. Your locks were broken, and your valuable stolen. Sorry.\n\n[!] A smiley face is drawn here in a dark black dust, probably that of coal [!]\n\n-Othy.", ""),
        StaticBook("*A letter*", "Clover Orvir", "[!] The note is neatly folded inside the envelope\nyou notice the formality of the cursive and begin to read the letter.\nDue to recent actions in my life, i must resign my duties as a Sillumir.\nI have learned much, and have had many trials and errors during my time. I hope one day i can return with nothing to hold me back.                             -Clover Orvir\n[!] As you finish reading you spot a few odd stains at the botom of the page, they look like small water marks", ""),
        StaticBook("-=Love Poems=-", "Kiveck Irelia", "~-=Love Poems=-~\nIf you said you were cold, I would wrap my arms around you.\nIf you said you were thirsty, I would give you the ocean blue.\nI would give you anything: the moon, the stars, the sunest too. This heart in my hands I hold out to you.\nImagine me and you, I think about you day and night, it's only right to think about the girl you love and hold her tight. So happy, together.\nA million stars up in the sky, one shines brighter I can't deny. A love so precious, a love so true, a love that comes from me to you.\nTrue love is measured by how deep you fall. And judged by how low you are willing to crawl just to save it and make it last. It is determined by how willing you are to open up to offer trust.\nThere's a light inside my heart that always lights the way; a glimmer of hope that gets me through the day; A little ray of happiness, that sometimes gets me through the harsh and sad times.\nThat little light, is you.\nI stumbled upon these small poems in a very secret place. I believed that I should share them with others now.", ""),
        StaticBook("-=Promise=-", "Klaudia Crast", "-------------------\n\n     ---=()()()=---\n\n          Promise\n\nBy Lady Klaudia Crast\n\n-------------------\nHis steps pounded the pavement with a steady beat; a paradox of the quickened rhythm of his heart thrumming against the walls of his chest like a jackhammer.\n\nSix long years had passed since he last laid eyes on her and their home.\nThey had become nothing short of a dream that both haunted and soothed his soul, wishing with every desperate breath he took for his dream to become a reality. \n\nIt may have taken six years, but his wish was finally granted, and the elation he felt -\ncouldn’t overpower the anxiety and uncertainty that raided his heart and mind. It threatened to reduce him to the memory of what put him in this situation to begin with, and although he tried not to think the worst, he'd witnessed more than any twenty-\neight year old man should. Yet, despite his seemingly positive aura, doubt shadowed his presence. \n\n He carried on, taking a moment to revel in the memories he had once created with her, even in their short time together.\nIt had been the last night before his departure. He’d lain there, his body entwined with hers, inhaling the scent that clung to the warmth of her skin, the scent of the lavender fields that she’d lain in just hours before he claimed every inch of her.\n“I’m going to miss this,” he whispered, kissing the top of his wife’s head. She snuggled in closer, tracing the pad of her finger along the contours of his chest and stomach.\n\n\n[!] A drawing of the lady and man resting would be here in great detail.\n“I wish you didn’t have to leave.” Her breath tickled the hair on his chest. “Just promise me you’ll return to me?” Lifting her head up to meet his eyes, she quickly added,\n\n“I know that’s a silly request, and I shouldn’t have asked that of you, -\nit’s just…I can’t bear the thought of anything happening to you.”\n\n“Nothing is going to happen to me,” he’d reassured her. “I wont be over there for very long. Before you know it, I’ll be back, and we can start the family we’ve always wanted.”\n“Oh?” She questioned, her eyebrows raising in amusement. Dragging her bottom lip between her teeth, she said, “If we have a boy, I want to name him Michael.”\n\n“My middle name?” He questioned, taking a mental note of how soft her skin felt against his -\nand the fullness of her lips that beckoned his to caress. Her smile was his answer, and he couldn’t find it within himself to argue. He would give her anything she desired.\n\n“Then, we name our baby Adalind if it’s a girl.”\n“No,” his wife protested. “You can’t do that.”\n\n\n“Do what? Use your middle name if it’s girl, just like how you want to use my middle name if it’s a boy? A little hypocritical, don’t you think?”\nHe watched in amusement as his wife’s jaw dropped, words caught on the tip of her tongue, unable to form a rebuttal.\n\n\n\nSo beautiful....\n“It’s such a strange name, David.”\n\nHe laughed. “Since when did strange phase you? You’ve never been one to settle for ordinary.\n\nBesides, I love your name. I love everything about you.\nAnd when we do have a baby, that baby will be a part of you, and I love the thought of naming her after the one woman who stole my heart the day she purposely dumped her tea on me.\"\n\nHis wife looked up into his eyes before snorting in laughter,\nburying her face into his chest. “You deserved it! You shouldn’t have been such a pompous arse.\"\n\nBrushing her golden waves aside and tucking them behind her ear, David took a deep breath in, revelling in his wife’s perfection.\n“This pompous ass got what he wanted. You’re mine, Quinn. Always and forever.”\n\n“Promise?”\n\n“Promise.” He meant it, with every breath he took, every beat of his heart, and within that moment as he covered her body with his-\nhe had never felt so sure of anything in his life.\n\n------------------- \nShall the tale end here? \n\nShall it move forth and elaborate upon their future? \n\nFind out soon! Promise 2 COMING SOON!", ""),
        StaticBook("-Clover Orvir", "Clover Orvir", "[!] The letter is in bueatiful Cursive handwriting with a small blue flower pressed against the pages. You begin to read...\n\"To Elodie,\nIs has been a while and seen as my husband has gone missing i would have a request. As small as it seems i am wondering if i could borrow a flint and steel or some rashons of food. As much as i regret asking i need... to feed my daughter\nand my current two jobs will not cover.\nVan'ayla.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n-Clover Orvir", ""),
        StaticBook("100 Mina Questio", "Ikur Seregon", "100 Mina Question\n\n--\n\n\"thilln ito nae'leh?\"\nAre you pure?\n||\n\"thilln ito kae'leh.\"\nI am pure.", ""),
        StaticBook("2 III III I", "Oracle", "A Short Story\n((Written by Evelyn for the Asulon Novella Competition))\n\nRetold by Leyu'Maehr\n\nOracles Library\n\n4th of Malin's Welcome -1443\nThe wind rattled the window panes, screaming to be let in. But Chirstina’s mother drowned out the howls of nature. Pacing up and down outside the door to the bedroom,\nChirstina wondered if her mother was going to survive giving birth to her little sister and frowned at the thought of how her father would handle it. The minutes slowly turned into hours as Christina waited, almost falling asleep before the doors shook\nwith the final scream of pain from her mother. Concerned and slightly curious, Christina cracked the door open slightly to peer inside. The sight that greeted her was of her mother collapsed in the bed, with her father and the doctor both whispering\nover the top of her. The doctor stepped back saying “I’m sorry, but she is beyond me. I don’t know any that could bring her back from the ‘deep sleep’. All that can be done for her is to leave her and hope for the best.” Christina’s dad spun around as\nhe heard the door opening slightly but lets out a sighs as he sees his older daughter. Taking the bundle at the end of the bed he hands it to Christina. “Look after your sister Evelyn, we must see to your mother” he said in a rush before slamming the\ndoor again. Looking down at her new sister, Christina whispers to the baby “Evelyn… well looks like I have to look after you till mother is feeling better”.\nAs the days went by their mother never truly recovered, leaving Evelyn in the care most of the\ntime of her big sister. Being away from home so often on business in other nations, Evelyn’s father wasn’t the one to teach her to feed her, to teach her to walk, to write but rather it was Christina who mothered her as she grew up. Time went\nby with little change until Evelyn started to grow more inquisitive about why her sister was looking after her more than her parents. Whenever Evelyn mustered enough courage to ask Christina ‘Why does Ma stay in bed?’ or ‘why isn’t Da\nhome?’ Christina would ignore Evelyn’s questions and take her out to the woods nearby to take their minds off of their family. Every time they were to enter the forest, Christina would stop Evelyn on the outskirts of the trees and say “Now you know\nthat you are never to come here without me. It is dangerous for little girls, definitely ones as pretty as you.”\nThis would bring Evelyn’s customary sigh and rolling her eyes as she always responded “Yes ‘Mother’ I know” before skipping\noff into the wood while she stuck her tongue out at her big sister. Christina feared the words in a way, how they seemed to hide secrets within their twisting branches. But, on the other hand she feared her inquisitive little sister’s questions about\ntheir family and knew that their adventures through the forest were the only things that kept her mind occupied. But as Evelyn’s mind grew sharper it became harder for Christina to keep her mind focused away from their family with simple games in the\nwoods. But after every time Evelyn asked the gap between the next time grew, until after several weeks Christina let out a small sigh of relief hoping Evelyn had just accepted that their parents couldn’t be there for them.\nSitting around the fireplace together on a moonless night reading Christina’s love poems, Evelyn piped up “Hey Christina, we should show these to Ma”. Closing her book slowly, Christina got up slowly “Eve, we can’t bother Mother she is tired. How\nabout we go out to the forest in the morning and-“. Evelyn stood up, slamming her own book shut with a snap that silenced Christina. “NO! I’m not stupid you know sister I know why we always go to the woods. Why can’t we just wake Ma up!” Evelyn shouted\nat Christina with pleading eyes. Christina slowly slid her book onto the shelf back into its place before saying “we can’t wake her Eve, just pack the basket and we can have a picnic tomorrow”. Her words fell on deaf ears though as Evelyn\nwas already half way out the door into the night, crying as she ran for the forest. Turning to notice the door creak shut, Christina ran outside with a torch screaming desperately for her little sister to come back and away from the dark\nwoods.\nCrashing through the first line of shrubs a few seconds ahead of her sister, Evelyn ran on through the night for a couple of minutes before hiding behind a rather large oak just at the bottom of a slope. With her heart thumping Evelyn waited\nbreathlessly for the telltale rustle and light of her sister as she ran past and over the hill calling out to Evelyn to come back. Smiling to herself in the dark Evelyn sat down to wait for her sister to come back the same way after noticing she had\nlost Evelyn in the dark before jumping out to scare her. Just as she settled down to wait; the heart stopping scream of Christina wrenched the air from across the other side of the bank of dirt. Evelyn jumped up with fright and scrabbled up\nthe slope to look down at her a scene lit by Christina’s torch lying on the ground.\nCrawling across the grass was her sister, drawing a bloody smear across the green stems from a gruesome gash across her thigh, the wound going all so deep that Evelyn\ncould see the stark white of her sister’s bones. Frozen stiff with shock and fright, Evelyn stayed there at the top of the mound on all fours. Unable to move as she watched the pigman enter the light, dragging a very ancient, blood soaked blade behind\nit as it approached Christina. Turning onto her back, Christina whimpered and pleaded for the for the pigman to stop and leave her alone but was cut short as it wrenched one of her arms wide with its free hand and deftly sliced her arm off just above\nthe elbow.\nEvelyn was petrified, unable to look away as the monster utters an indistinguishable grunt as it flings the lifeless limb away from it, spraying the small clearing in a fine mist of red before stabbing its blade through\nChristina’s ribs trying to silence her new screams. The gurgling now coming from Christina seemed to attract another pigman as it enters the light from the other side, grunting to its companion in a similar way before stomping onto Christina’s\nstill attached wrist, bringing forth a sickening crack as the bones snap like a twig under the weight of the monster causing Christina to scream louder in pain. Both the pigmen seemed to worker faster the more anguish that she showed, either\nout of frustration and annoyance or some sort of delight as they drew her closer to death.\nThey hacked and stabbed with their gleaming blades never truly scoring a fatal blow but sending blood and flesh all across the grass, her severed arm\nnow hung from a low branch dripping blood onto the first pigman’s head. Seeming to both have become bored or irritated by the screaming and gurgling, they grunted at each other as if talking about what to do next. After a\nshort moment one of the pigmen took a hold of her leg, grunting at the other as it wrapped its fingers around Christina’s still screaming head, before both tugging violently. The trees still seemed to hold the scream for Evelyn’s ears even after her\nsisters neck had stretched so far that no noise would ever leave it. Opening her mouth Evelyn tried to scream, but no noise came out as she looked down at the two monsters standing dumbly in what remained of her sister.\nThe next few\nseconds seem to just stand still for Evelyn, before she took one step back, crushing a pile of very crisp leaves. The little beady eyes of the two pigmen turned and stared at Evelyn, their flesh clinging to their faces, Evelyn staring at the pigmen,\nthe pigmen staring at Evelyn. It went on, nothing happening till one of the monsters let out a sheik setting everyone into motion. The hulking monsters charged up the hill at Evelyn as she turned and pelted for the edge of the woods, not even noticing\nthe branches wiping at her body as she ran or the tears streaming across both cheeks.\nShe kept running even after the crashing of the pigmen through the undergrowth had long died away into the distance. She ran straight out of the forest, covered in\nlittle scratches, she kept running past her house, past her neighbors, she didn’t stop till she saw the light of the local tavern and had burst through the door to find a group of Rangers and Wardens sitting around a table drinking, singing\nand rolling dice. One of the men turned to see Evelyn before remarking “My, look what we have here. Isn’t it a little past your bed time Mis?” bringing a burst of laughter from his comrades.\nCatching her breathe and wiping away the remnants of her\ntears Evelyn quickly blurts out what happened to her sister in the woods pleading them to help her. “You expect us to bother going out there at this time of night? Ah it would be nothing but a waste of time” grumbled one of the men as he turns back to\nthe dice on the table. The man sitting next to him slaps him on the shoulder “Hey maybe you should go help her, you know those piggy men usually have a nice amount of minas on them and by the looks of the dice you could do with whatever you can get”\ncausing the rest of the men to burst into laughter and return to the game at hand. One of the men casually says over his shoulder to Evelyn “People constantly go missing in that forest, it’s a waste of our time you both shouldn’t have been in there in\nthe first place”.\nTrying desperately to gain their attention again Evelyn starts to shriek at the men causing one of them to pick her up and throw her out the door. Lying there in the mud as the rain slowly started to set in over then night sky, Evelyn\ncried until not a single tear would come to her. A few hours later found Evelyn standing in her mother’s doorway, dripping water all over the wooden floor as she watched her mother sleep peacefully. Running her hand slowly through her hair\nEvelyn whispered to herself “I’m sorry Mother. I’m so sorry. I… I just wanted us all to be happy together.” She turned away almost breaking into tears as she starts to walks out, walks past her room, past Christina’s room, past their books,\npast the families fence, past the local tavern, past the Laurelin gates, past everything she knew and into the waiting night.\nHer mind was blank as she walked, one foot in front of the other, plodding along completely dead to the world around\nher. It wasn’t until the dawn broke and the first rays of sunlight hit Evelyn’s eye blinding her for several seconds as she tried to wipe the sleep and tears from her eyes. Looking around at her surroundings she found herself on a small trail through\ndense undergrowth but not so far from a main road that she figured was most likely Kings Road by the amount of activity she could hear. Covering her ears slightly from the clamor coming through the trees Evelyn stumbled off to find a place to\nrest. As the sun rose to its peak and started to fall, Evelyn finally surrendered to her weariness and collapsed between the roots of an oak tree.\nShe found no peace in her sleep though, the last seconds of Christina’s life\nplaying over and over again as her body started to shiver from the chilled night. So lost in her nightmares Evelyn didn’t notice the two knights until one wrenched her around to peer at her in the torchlight. Looking at Evelyn’s frightened\nexpression with a grin the knight holding her called out to his companion “Aye looks like we found our relief from fightin’ those damn green skins and fairies” causing the second knight to laugh as he helped his friend rip Evelyn from her resting\nplace and send her sprawling onto the grass.\nToo frightened to think about anything other than getting away from the two men, Evelyn started frantically crawling away on all fours before one of the men stamped down on her leg causing\nher to scream out in pain. Taking his armor off slowly as he walked up beside Evelyn, the other man looked down upon her and chuckled “Oh please don’t leave us little fairy, we are terribly weary from battlin’ up and down Kings Road and I could use a", ""),
        StaticBook("7 II III I", "Oracle", "The Theory of Magical Engineering\n\nRetold by Leyu'Maehr\n\nOracles Library\n\n20th of Snows Maiden -1443\nFirstly, you will need a suitable conduit for the Magical Imbue. Any piece of equipment should suffice, although wood lacks the endurance for a imbue. I suggest using Iron - rough, yet strong and dependable.\n(Much like dwarves, harhar!) Then, you will need to create the Magical Imbuement to be used on the empty shell (Catalyst). This is the tricky part! You will need to lay magical runes onto the Catalyst, runes that- summarise what the object's use will\nbe. For Instance, a crossbow created to pierce metal should be imbued with runes of Breaking, Unbinding, Smashing ectetera. Hopefully, this will help you create magically imbued item of your own! If you want to know more, you should consult my\nother guides on Artificing. Thanks for Reading! PS. This book has been imbued with runes of self-destruction. Ha Ha, Gotcha!", ""),
        StaticBook("<> Our Love <>", "Kale", "<><><>.Our Love.<><><>\n\nWritten By: Dr. Kale\n\n   Finally, here we are on the balcony with the most delightful sunset.\n  There he was standing in front of me leaning in for the kiss, the all of a sudden my nightmare appeared.\n\"Awh look at that, how cute. Let me guess you're going to kiss her now?\" he said laughing. \n  My eyes narrowed. Who is this bigget that ruined my long awaited moment?!\n  He succeded in upsetting Zeliek, for he drew his sword to challenge him.\nThe other man simply laughed and disappeared so Zeliek and I went back to what was supposed to happen. Okay, finally here it goes. \"Pff, lad you sure do take a long time to plant one.\"\n\nThat was enough for Zeliek, he jumped down ready to fight this man.\n\"What's your name, you pompous fool?\" Zeliek asked, furry in his eyes. \"If you'd like to know, it's Mason!\" he shouts with a rather large laugh. Once Zeliek saw his state he decided to simply part ways... Never to hear from him again.\n\nAbout two months\npass, and nothing from Zeliek. As I was learning to stitch there he was. \"Where's your other half?\" he'd ask. Once I heard the familiar voice I grew livid! \n\"You ruined everything!\" I shouted as my heart beat pounded. His reaction at first seemed rather\nhostile, but then became soft.\n\n  \"Apologies Miss?\" I didn't like this man. Nor did I have intentions to speak to him any further. \"Ah, Mason just the man I was hopin' to see!\" My teacher shouted quite happily before he scolded me.\n\"Masie, don't be so rude.\"\n\n  After that day, it seemed that Mason came almost everyday to that particular spot just to see me. Our hatred turned into a light friendship. Until one night in a lively tavern.\nHe looked off, and stared at the table after glancing me in the eyes. \"Masie?\" he asked with a embarrassed look upon his face. \"Hmm\" I said taking a sip of rich wine.\n\n\"W- would you care to have dinner sometime?\"\n*Here would be a drawing of a wine goblet hitting the cold stone ground*\n\n\"Wh- what, are you serious?\" I asked in complete shock. \"How do I know you're not makin' fun?\" I then crossed my arms defensivly. He laughed with rosey cheeks.\n\"I erm.. I promise?\" For a moment I actually considered it fully. But seeing the wine hit the ground, just tore me inside.\n\n\"Only if there's wine.\" I'd state firmly with a smile. He seemed glad to hear this.\nWe saw each other almost everyday. Some say we were the 'Fools Lovers' with all the joy that filled our hearts. Years went by, but then everything changed on the voyage to Vailor. Rain poured down harder as the lightning struck. He stood before me, not-\nlooking into my eyes. \"Masie.. whatever we had leave it in Athera. We move from each other for now.\" Tears fell from my eyes. My heart ripped into small pieces.. \"Mas-\" I barely got out before he left me there alone, cold in this horrid dark rain.\nThere was nothing left but a hopeless love. As the years grew longer and more lonesome, I became very knowledgeable with medicine. Even got a clinic in the Caliphate as a head doctor.\n\nIt never seemed complete without him, for he held a piece\nof my torn heart. That night standing on my balcony breathing in the clear air, a dove dropped a letter that reads:\n\"My dearest Masie, after all these years I can't help but think of you. I think it's time you knew the truth. May we meet?\" - M\nHolding back the tears was hard. For they weren't tears of joy but of dark sorrow and pain.\n\nHe continued to write me until the Moon grew a size larger, then I decided that I'd meet with him.\n<><><><><><><><><><><>\n\n  Walking into that garden and seeing him. After all the years, he hasn't changed one bit. \n\n\"Masie, I haven't much time but you must know. I did what I did on the boat to protect you.\"\nHis words were soft but hushed. I stared into his eyes, his beautiful eyes.. \"Why did you? What are you protecting me from?\" I couldn't help but ask.\n\n  \"I can't tell you, or else I won't be able to protect you from them.\" His gaze turned to the sky.\n\"Ju- just know that we will be able to be together one day, but for now I remain in hiding.\" He then turned to me with a moment of bliss in his eyes as he grabbed my face and kissed me.\n\n \"We will be together soon, I promise.\n\"I love you, Masie.\" A tear fell from his eye as we stood there face to face. Not another word was spoken for he left quickly afterwards and I was chocked. One thing was for certain.\nOur fated, happy, disfuctional, but unique love was not at an end. For this was our beginning. Our love.", ""),
        StaticBook("A Better Elder.", "Edgar Bobins", "A Greater Elder Than I.\n=--=--=--=--=--=--=By Fatty (Edgar) Bobins.\n\n           ({*})\n\n\n\n24th of The First Seed, 1593\nI was not born to be a leader, as most wee-folk are not. I was made for smoking, drinking, farming and the such and it just so happens that I eventually ended up an appointed elder of Reedsborough. The current year is 1593 and I often catch myself\nimagining what a better leader than I would do. So I decided to write them down so that I may look to improve upon my mistakes or so that future leaders may strive to be better with the help of my ideas.\n1. An Elder Should be Friendly.\n=--=--=--=--=--=--=It is the job of an Elder to be kind and introduce new wee-folk to the village and make it appear to be an enticing place to stay. Elders should also do their best not to make enemies with their fellow\nvillagers, for this can divide the village into choosing sides. This is of paramount importance because the attitudes of elders often decide how new villagers will feel about the village, and the approval of new villagers is very important to the growth\nof our culture. If our culture doesn't last then we will inevietably end up like the biggun nations an cultures. We will become violent, snobbish and rude.\nSo it is up to the Elder to set an example of how to act!\n2. An Elder Should Party Often!\n=--=--=--=--=--=--=It is my vision that there would be as many parties as the villagers see fit to have but more importantly that the village itself would throw grand festivals once a year and all people big and small would\nbe invited. Consistency would be key to success because with consistency more people would be aware when festivals would occur and so more people would attend. Festivals encourage pride in the village and discourage bigguns from their naturally violent\ntendencies. Festivals are great fun and promote friendship among villagers! Festivals also encourage villagers to work, the chefs will cook, the brewers will brew and the tailors will sew in preparation for the grand events and thus this will promote a\nhappy village who will be active throughout the year in anticipation for the festival!\n3. Life Under a Hill\n=--=--=--=--=--=--=I think that it is very important that all villagers are content with their homes and all villagers should be given the oppurtunity to build themselves a home if they so desire (so long as it is a proper burrow)\n4. A Department\n=--=--=--=--=--=--=As the current Sherrif of Reedsborough I take great pride in the department and the deputies who work to protect and serve the village. It is essential to upholding the laws of proper and to remind villagers how\nfriendly\nvillage officials are. It is very important to chose responsible and respectable deputies so that they do not poorly represent the village or our traditions, so in turn it is important to be grateful for our beloved deputies and all they do for us.\n5. The Voice of Villagers.\n=--=--=--=--=--=--=I think that it's be a nice idea if villagers could have their voices heard all the time, whether the best way to execute that idea is by holding village meeting where the Elders listen to the ideas of the\nvillagers or if the villagers should elect an official every year. Regardless I would like to see some change in this regard.\n6. Health and Safety\n=--=--=--=--=--=--=I would like to see an apothecary, medics and sugeons around the village to ensure that our lovely villagers are healthy (even if we don't fight as much as bigguns). Currently there is nothing of the sort in\nReedsborough but I think that needs to change very soon.\n7. Shogging\n=--=--=--=--=--=--=I think that we have many talented shoggers currently and I want them to enjoy our beloved game while still in their youth. I would like to see a shogging league established so that a competitive and friendly environment can\ngrow for our shoggers. Shogging keeps our villagers entertained, healthy and friendly and it is a much safer hobby than the orcish ways of fist fighting for sport. Though shogging is great I do wish other sports like hog riding, log splitting and drinking\ngames would become more popularized so as to give the villagers more ways to create friendships with each other.\n8. Discovery\n\n=--=--=--=--=--=--=I think it would be a nice sentiment if we encouraged our great tinkerers and inventors would invent new things and discover. Now I'm no expert on these kinds of things, for I'm more inclined to spend time in the field\nthan hunched over a table but I did recently travel to an elven city with one of my deputy's brother and some biggun had a wand that clicked and released a loud noise and a magical bolt. After seeing this I do fear that one day our distance from biggun\nculture will leave us stranded in the past while the rest of the world progresses with new tools and machines.\n9. Literature\n=--=--=--=--=--=--=Given this is my first book I have ever seriously written I think that we need to encourage our villagers to write and read so that we have a functioning library for curious villagers, specifically the children. As a\nchild I spent much time at the Library of Draguur to learn the secrets their book held and I was often exposed to the existence of an improper halfling who likely could have been a very bad influence on me. So if you're reading this and you've read this\nmuch make sure to write a book of your own so that our ideas can last longer and spread farther.\n10. The Laws of Propper.\n=--=--=--=--=--=--=As of current we are still functioning under the same set of laws that we've had since 1522 (established by some random man named Andwise Peregrin and our beloved villager Larry Shortoak) and to my\nexperience\nthey have served the village well. They stress the value of trust and friendship and emphasize important laws like no shoes or locks. It is a strong foundation for our culture that we've had for centuries but as of recent many villagers have seeked change\nbecause of biggun thieves. Maybe they are just new to following our ways but all of our ancestors dealt with these issues and never had many issues. It's simple that you leave expendable items like hay and bottles in chests around your home but you hide\nitems you wouldn't trust around bigguns, like iron and family recipes. Though if a neighbor asks to borrow some of these things then you should be forthcoming and allow them to borrow the items if they are trustworthy and you are not in dire need of the\nitem. Though I cannot force the villagers to see this way and nobody else can I'm sure that it will be clear to them as they mature.\n11. Shovels\n=--=--=--=--=--=--=We wee-folk are not very aggressive and as stated in the rules of proper we do not wield weapons. Thoguh if we need to defend ourselves we would take up our tools, and that's what we've done for centuries. Though as of\nrecent we have not had a village smith and it has become very troubling because our tools are not as great as they should be and it leads me to fear for our villagers safety. A better elder than I would take up the craft themselves to provide good tools\nto the villagers..\n12. Of Farming and other Crafts.\n=--=--=--=--=--=--=It is imerative that farms, livestock, docks and workstations are kept up to a safe and welcoming standard that make working very easy to access for any villagers who would like to make a name for\nthemselves and even take part in trade with other people. Currently in Reedsborough we have no mine and a dusty anvil and this troubles me and I would like to see change soon.. but that's for an Elder better than I.\n13. Coins\n=--=--=--=--=--=--=The main form of trade amongst bigguns. Coins are well known to encourage greed and materialism (which we as dwarven descendants are already susceptible to) are forbidden by the laws of propper.\nWe do not need to\nuse coins\nand I think that it is better to trade with our neighbors because this promotes conversation and working amongst the village. Admittedly coins could promote trade and friendliness amongst bigguns... Though this does also mean that this would encourage\nvillagers to interact with bigguns and stand between them and something they desire. This can be very very dangerous and I do not think coins are a good idea at all. I think that this would put our villagers in danger and that it would be a massive goof.\n14. Shoes\n=--=--=--=--=--=--=Shoes are the bane of my existence, I despise them and I personally do not believe any person who wears them is a true halfling. Shoes rub on the hairs atop our feet and rip them out from the stem and also give us terrible\nrashes. Shoes also tear at our naturally thick soles and give us terrible blisters which make any form of walking a new form of torture that orcs couldn't enjoy. Shoes also stub our toe nails. Think of it, at the end of every step your foot slides forward\nand your long nails slam into the front of the shoe and the back end of the nail tries to break away from the flesh attatching to it. Shoes are disgusting to even think of and oddly many newuns still wander into the village with them. It is important to\ninstantly burn these monstrosities so that all villagers can know how evil they are and for the safety of the wee-folk who wears the shoes. I think I have done a very great job as an elder to make the evil of shoes apparent, for instance ridiculing\nimpropers who wear them and calling them tiny pink goblins (because they obviously weren't a halfling).\n15. Conclusion\n=--=--=--=--=--=--=So my book is running out of pages and I'd like to leave a few pages for readers to make notes or draw an image in my likeness or something of the sort. In conclusion I'd like to say that it is important to make time to\nlisten to older folk who have lived in times long forgotten. Also make the villagers know they are appreciated for their friendly attitudes. Stick to the rules of proper, for they have guided our kind for many centuries and have never led us too far\nastray. Make sure to have your own fun and make fun for the villagers, thus your life will be full of friends and merry times.", ""),
        StaticBook("A Black Bounty", "Barrowlord Kozilek", "Locate the Wight named Kozilek and say unto him, \"hallowed pearls.\"\n\nOnly there does your opportunity lie.", ""),
        StaticBook("A bloodied tome", "Jonathan Dunharrow", "Malghourn's mother was not a nice lady. She doesn't like blood magic.\nThis is, your knowledge of blood magic.", ""),
        StaticBook("A burnt note", "Yhorm Aldyhr", ". . . four months, five days, and a few hours. It had been so long we existed without a looming axe seeking to sever our head from our shoulders. Yalia had done well bringing us so far from the taint we once called home. Yet as is with all good\npestilences, it clung true, and followed us to the ends of the earth. . .", ""),
        StaticBook("A Gift", "Cristen Irl", "I had a great and fantasic time...i haven't been this happy in a long time. I thank you for the trip and thank you for spending time with me.\n\nJust before we went i painted this photo. I hope you like it.\n\n-Cristen Irl", ""),
        StaticBook("A GUIDE: Hijabs", "Zahrah", "A GUIDE: HIJABS\n\n\nTen hijab tips and secrets.\n\nFor those who wear one, who may wear one, or are generally interested.\n1. There are so many ways to tie a hijab.\n\n\nTie around the back of your head? More volume? There are so many choices!\n2. We actually have really nice hair!\n\nJust because it's hidden doesn't mean it doesn't exist. \nSometimes, it's even pretty.\n3. Running is a no-no.\n\nTry running or doing anything that makes you warm. We heat up a lot faster than the rest of you.\n4. We get asked a lot of silly questions.\n\n\"Are you actually bald?\"\n5. Most days is bad Hijab day.\n\nJust like bad hair days, sometimes you can put on your hijab and look like you haven't slept in weeks.\n6. Wearing new clothes can't be done until you have a hijab to match.\n\nReally. Do not try and wear a blue dress with a lime green dress. Think about it!\n7. We own so many, but have none to wear.\n\nIt's like going to look for food when the pantry is full. It's there but it's not really /there/.\n8. Hijabs can block sound too.\n\nSo we're not ignoring you, we actually can't hear you.\n9. If you drop your food, it ends up in your hijab and not on the floor.\n\nNo more wasting food that you accidentally drop! Who could ask for more?\n10. But finally, we're really proud of our choice.\n\nWe may be looked at funny, asked why we cover up. Some races don't understand. But. . \n\n'When I wear my headscarf, I feel I have faith, and identity. . '\n'And that's also beauty.'\n\nhttp://tinyurl.com/pxohds6", ""),
        StaticBook("A Holy Text", "Beltran", "[!]In lettering resembling the ornate holy books of oren is written:\n'Forsooth doth the caverans maze, of brother frair cannot be saved, lest the advice of the sage, of marble stone and treated clay.'\n\n'Disrespect' my guide does sow, seedlings thoughts here and fro, for lest the speaker speaks it so, the farmer-\nstrikes so with his hoe'\n\n'Reap the wheat' the farmer thinks, of times with father upon the brink, nostalgia doth not sink it's roots, for father's name he has it moot.'\n'Of father's name, give me rest, for I will guide you to those who best, enlighten so with thou mantle else, thy head will rest upon farmer's mantle.'", ""),
        StaticBook("A Horse Guide", "Klaudia Crast Warnek", "[!]\n\nOn the page before you would be a series\nof horses that roamed\nthe lands free.\n\n-------------------\n\n[!] Here would be a drawing of a saddle made from the finest of leather, thick and heavy on the horse-\nback as a man sat atop straddling the animal.\n-------------------[!] Reigns used to pull at the horses maw that almost were whip like shaped in the form of silver circlets around the mouthing area of it.\n-------------------\n[!] Various types of armor in the form of iron, diamond, even gold placed upon a royal steed was drawn here in color.\n\n-------------------[!] Thick set ropes used as leads for the animal, wrapped in circlettes upon the grassy plain.\n-------------------\n\n[!] A stick like figure,\nalmost resembling a gardening hoe is here and in the drawing shown to be used to tame the animal.\n\n-------------------\n\nAs a guide to remember to care for your animal by taking\n- very good care of it. Feeding it the proper foods of apples and carrots. \n\nEnsuring it has enough room to roam and clean water to drink. \n\nTaking it out on rides daily at the very least and keeping it cared for and clean.\nAs a tool for instruction this guide is written to you by the Lady Klaudia Crast Warnek, for you use.\n\n-------------------\n[!] Here would be a drawing of a woman riding a horse down a pathway with intense speed.", ""),
        StaticBook("A Hundred Years", "Deven", "A hundred years is a very long time!\n\nHo,yes,ho!\n\nA hundred years is a very long time!\n\nA hundred years ago!\n\nThey used to think that pigs could fly!\nHo,yes,ho!\n\nI don't believe it, no, not I!\n\nA hundred years ago!\n\nThey thought the moon was made of cheese!\n\nHo,yes,ho!\n\nYou can believe it if\nyou please!\n\nA hundred years ago!\n\nThey thought the stars were set a-light!\n\nHo,yes,ho!\n\nBy some good Angle every night!\n\nA hundred years ago!\nThey hung a man for making steam, they cast his body in the stream!\n\nA hundred years ago!\n\nA hundred years is a very long time!\n\nHo,yes,ho!\n\nA hundred years is a\nvery long time!\n\nA hundred years ago!", ""),
        StaticBook("A Late Note", "Lavinia Aureon", "I love you, my little doveling, and I hope you and your husband are filled to the brim with happiness forevermore.\n\n-Granmama", ""),
        StaticBook("A Letter", "Renna Talraen", "Glad to see your shop is doing well. Living my dream, eh? Contact me if you're interested in expanding your trade to the Holy Empire as well.\n\nRenna", ""),
        StaticBook("A letter", "Griff28", "Dear White Rose\n  It's been awhile I'm not sure what going on but, something's wrong,  as I walked in the first time, I felt like something else was there not just Bohra, and Dragons. As it is I've been trying to get the Marked position but Temp told me\nto talk to Peter\n\nDay 2: Fuck this, Fuck this, the Witch woods is terrible, trolls, and spiders, but something remains, a door, I must get it open, but those  DAMN trolls.\n\nDay 3: Things are l;ooking bad for Malinor soldiers of the\nGlurtzfolock have been seen kidnapping, and killing people.\n\nDay 4: We are preparing to take the fort at Storms Crossing, the men are ready, a win is expected.\nDay 5: Vodnik seem to be dangerous, they infect rivers, the White Rose found the cure, I have it in me.\n\nDay 6: The Exodus happend, I'm all alone now.\nDay 7: Castle Greywyn has been corrupted, the war for Anthos has begun. \n\nThis is it, were all going to die, Setherien forces raid settlements, killing elves, who will fight.", ""),
        StaticBook("A Letter", "Ikarus Trea Delviorigh", "Dear Wandering \nArcanist\n\nI over-heard you talking earlier within the cloud temple docks, saying that you use arcane evocation and arcane sheilding. I was hoping that you'd be looking for an apprentice.. possibly someone who wants to learn sheilding..\nI understand if you don't want any students, such as many mages. But if you're looking for one, I'd be more then happy to take that position. I wish to learn arcane sheilding simply because I wish to protect the people I love, and possibly allow them to\nlive a happy lifestyle.\nI already know some of the basic's about magic, and the arcane. Such as all arcane magic such as evocation and sheilding is drawn forth from the void. The void being a plane of existance, that mages once connected to, can use to\nsummon forth an element or arcane power. Then shape that energy in our world. I'll need to learn a bit more and I know that. I'm hoping you'll take the time to teach me and allow me to learn a magic..\n-Sincerely \nIkarus Trea Dalvoirigh", ""),
        StaticBook("A Letter", "Miu", "Thanks for everything\n\n\n\nGood luck  on journey c;", ""),
        StaticBook("A letter", "Nemir", "Dear Elves of Haelun'or,\n\n   I feel that I should do what's right in my life and open my eyes.  My path has been set and I walk down it feeling free, spiritually clean, and truly pure.  I thank those who understood me, though I shall not say names for-\ntheir safety.  I thank you guys.\n   I am writing this letter because I am leaving Haelun'or, forever, I am not running to Oren, that would be a too much of an obvious place to do to.  I am not going to the Dwarven kingdom, you might look there too. -\nI am going to a place where I feel safe.\n\n\n\n   -Farewell\n\n  ~ Nemir.", ""),
        StaticBook("A Letter (1/?)", "TehLulu", "[!] The book seems to contain the transcription of one part of a series of letters. They all seem to be written by the same hand, implying perhaps that one side of the exchange wrote copies of the letters, for some reason...\nWe know little of the one called Dworkin and all that we do know is steeped in mystery, clouded by the forgetfulness of the past. I can say for certain the man was a mage; A sorcerer of great renown in his time.\nHis specialty of skill is lost, but what I have managed to infer from the tome found in a makeshift tomb in the tower far to the North implies he was not a master of merely one magic, but of several. Indeed, perhaps even all of them, for there\nseems to be much implied of both his seeming-omniscience and omnipotence. (Though you and I both know such a thing is impossible in practice..no?)\n\nAside from being a master mage, it is known he was\nunpredictable, at best. Rather than sit in a dreary room pouring over tomes as you or I might, he opted instead for practice in the field. He lead a band of adventurers, each and every one a master mage in their own right. Though there is no\nrecorded name for his fellowship, it may very well have been the foundings of our very own Guild, seeing as so much of our own history was lost. They were known for their work against the Fallen One, and I can connect their efforts to several\nas-of-yet-unexplained incidents from that era, references to lost and forgotten cities and places. They are refenced in the Tale of Nine Cities, in locating the lost relic of the cult of Sondar, in the rescue of Queen Yi'naethia, and in some ancient\nwritings concerning their failure to save something referred to only as \"The Jewel of the South\" before it was lost.\n\nThere is so much we do not know of Dworkin, but so much potential for us to gain should\nwe endeavour to rediscover what was lost. The Guild's archives in Malinor could be of significant use in this matter and it would serve the Guild's purpose if you permitted me to send one of my graduated apprentices to investigate this\nmatter on my behalf, as I of course can not depart from my post.\n\nKind Regards,", ""),
        StaticBook("A Letter (2/?)", "TehLulu", "[!] The book seems to contain the transcription of one part of a series of letters. They all seem to be written by the same hand, implying that one side of the exchange wrote copies of the letters, for some reason...\nYou are well aware that the archives in Malinor are locked and that I am not authorized to grant you access to them. Access to the Archives requires the permission of a majority of Archmages as well as the express\npermission of the Arcane. Indeed, the Archives cannot even be opened without his presence and assistance.\n\nIt is insulting you presume I would be naive enough to believe your claim that this serves\nthe Guild's purpose, for it would be obvious even to the most unlearned of apprentices that this serves no one's purpose but your own. I do doubt you truly wish for the Archives to be open, for to do so would require informing the other\nArchmages of your newfound habit of pillaging and defiling graves. Graves the inhabitants of which you do not and could not truly know.\n\nYou are chasing nothing more than a fantasy and I urge you to\nabandon this quest of yours, llir. I would not use such abusive language had you heeded my words when last we spoke of this at the gathering. Your words are lost on this one, as I will not assist you in this vanity.\nYour request is denied, for I cannot grant it.\n\n\nWith respect,", ""),
        StaticBook("A Letter (3/?)", "TehLulu", "[!] The book seems to contain the transcription of one part of a series of letters. They all seem to be written by the same hand, implying perhaps that one side of the exchange wrote copies of the letters, for some reason...\nIf you think it has gone past my attention that the only Guildhouse the Arcane ever bothers himself with anymore is yours, you are indeed as naive as you claim not to be, if not more so. It would not in the least suprise me, nay, any of the\nGuildmasters, if he had provided you some alternate means of accessing the Archives. The very definition of naivety is assuming that any of the Archmages could be bothered to rise from their nigh-constant slumbering of ninnies in order to host a\nvote!\n\nNor should I be worried for their approval, for so long in their posts they've been and I in mine, I could tell you precisely what could be offered to each in turn in exchange for their approval. Even the Arcane dare not\nrefuse with all of them aligned against him, and refuse I know he would, for you have his ear. \n\nThe Guild's purpose is manifold, and you seem insistent upon denying at least one facet of the life we have sworn ourselves to. The discovering and\ndissemination of knowledge between Guild members is akin to a holy duty, one which you seem intent upon refusing with you maternal protection of the Archives. By refusing me my right to knowledge you dishonor the Guild and do no credit to your illustious\nposition, one which you clearly do not deserve.", ""),
        StaticBook("A Letter (4/?)", "TehLulu", "[!] The book seems to contain the transcription of one part of a series of letters. They all seem to be written by the same hand, implying perhaps that one side of the exchange wrote copies of the letters, for some reason...\nYou are correct in your analysis of the Guild's founding principles and so I must congratulate you on answering an unasked question which any initiated apprentice is required to know. Sulian, llir.\nOf course it was likely possible for you to answer as you did without touching upon soure wounds. While you answered that elementary question correctly, I am afraid you are quite incorrect about nigh everything else. To start, no.\nThe Arcane does not visit frequently and if we must be open and honest, as good peers should be, I will admit I have not seen him in quite some time, and likely I saw him last the same place you did; The gathering at the Tower.\nHe has not provided me with any alternate method of accessing the Archives without his presence, and even had he, I would most certainly not make it available to one such as yourself. I know where your politics align you.\nAs said, I am not naive. And as such I will admit to my knowledge, and share it with you, in accordance with the Guild's purpose.\n\nI know you are the reason the gathering was held, and the cause of the ever-growing schism which forms\na great ravine 'twixt the Guild. The subject of it is on the tongue of every apprentice as far west as Jyl'hied, and as far east as Rynak. It is a thing impossible to quiet, even in whispers, and it brings forth ill will towards the Guild, to have so many\nmurmuring of such a sensitive subject. It has reached such intensities that an apprentice in Rynak was killed by one of the native Uruks, simply for the fact that he overheard the apprentice murmur the name.\nYou and yours tread upon a sensitive subject, one which the Arcane had hoped to put to rest with the gathering. \n\nOne which should have been put to rest.\nIf we are going to touch further upon the ideals of the Guild, especially the very same of the mutual sharing of research and knowledge, then perhaps we should discuss your offences against these principles?\nNeed I remind you that all you have discovered of this Dworkin, including his supposed tomb and your subsequent robbing of it, you have failed to shared with the rest of the Guild? That book you stole belongs in the Archives you so dearly\nwish to access.\n\nPerhaps if you agree to put it in it's rightful place, the Arcane may be willing to grant you access.", ""),
        StaticBook("A Letter (5/?)", "TehLulu", "[!] The books seems to contain the transcription of one part of a series of letters. They all seem to be written by the same hand, implying perhaps that one side of the exchange wrote copies of the letters, for some reason...\nYour glib does you no credit. To dare compare one such as myself to an uneducated apprentice is an insult of the highest order, and if you do not wish for me to touch upon 'sore wounds' as you call them, perhaps you ought to tame your quill and cork your\ninkpot, lest you waste it all on such nonsense. \n\nWas the gathering my fault? To claim it was a fault at all to finally have the others awakened from their slumber and forced to pay some attention, however small, to the world and matters at\nhand, is obscene. I will proudly take responsibility as the so-called 'fault' of the gathering. Be me the cause, I will gladly be a herald of the winds of change. Perhaps such a cyclone has to turn to dust the dry old bones of those who are too dated and\nout of touch as to be no longer relevant. What an upset that would be, to have a new set of leaders, perhaps those who care for note just the dry, dusty past, but the future of the Guild as well?! You and I know the current set of hands behind the Guild\nare content to do nothing. They would still their hands for the entirety of time, if they could, but alas, even for the children of Malin time is not infinite. He will return, sooner or later. Yet still you deny my solution. \n\nMalinor will burn if we\ndo not prepare, along with you, your inept mate, and your Archives, if we do not act.\n\nBut perhaps it is no longer a matter of \"we\", but of \"I\". I will give you your credit where it is due, yes, to have noted how far my influence and my\nsubject of concern has spread. Congratulations and adulations to you and your apprentices, and their ever watchful eyes and ever listening ears. But the lion's share of credit is my own, for influencing such a great many people.\nIf I put the call out, who do you think would heed it? Who would come, if I were to call a gathering of my own? I doubt there would be so many excuses as per attendance as there were with the Arcane's. No, it was never likely many would attend the\ngathering of one with a name and no face. \n\nIf the Guild refuses to provide me the resources and assistance I require for the furthering of our purpose and for the protection of our people, then I will simply have to find my own solution, with my\nown resources.\n\nTo lecture me on my research habits is foolish when you know well how secretive the upper echelon is. Either secretive or slothful, no matter. I have not seen a report or any new research from any of the Archmages or\nGuildmasters in quite some time. And you know better than I how secretive the Arcane is in his own private projects. Or perhaps, considering how close you are, you do not. Perhaps the very idea he could keep a secret is ludicrous to you. I would not be\nsuprised. If our leaders will not follow the same standards they put forth, I certainly feel no obligations to do so. You are the only on falling for their idealised vision for the Guild. None of the other Guildmasters will submit their research to anyone\nany longer either.\n\nIf the Guild cannot help me, then I will forge my own coalition. I offer you one opportunity, out of mutual respect. The echelon has forgotten our purpose and abandoned our cause. Join me and let us begin a project to\nbring the Guild back to its roots.", ""),
        StaticBook("A Letter (6/?)", "TehLulu", "[!] The book seems to contain the transcription of one part of a series of letters. They all seem to be written by the same hand, implying perhaps that one side of the exchange wrote copies of the letters, for some reason...\nWhatever the Archmages do, if you wish to save the Guild from what you foresee as an inevitable failure, you would do well not to act as they do. Yes, many of the mages and apprentices find the current leadership...wanting.\nBut what can we hope to change, when we act the same as they do? By setting a proper example for those learning, we can define what the Guild should be. Titles are nothing but frivolity, and we need not be the Arcane ourselves to influence the minds\nof the young, as you yourself have unfortunately demonstrated clearly.\n\nI know you wish for the Guild to support your endeavours. But we do not have the funding for the type of expedition you would like to mount,\nnor for obvious reasons can anyone grant you access to what knowledge pertaining to your subject of obsession, if any, we have.\n\nIt would be a massive undertaking to go searching for remnant of whatever tale Dworkin may\nhave left behind, both in terms of finance and manpower. Neither you nor the Guild have the ability to finance such a thing.\n\nIt is foolish to approach the delicate subject of the Guild's state in such a brazen manner, as if plotting\nsome sort of rebellion or upheaval. This is not the appropriate manner in which to bring about your desired change, nor is this schism an adequate reason for upheaval.\n\nAs for my joining you,\nI will reiterate my stance as it was during the gathering. No matter where the Archmages stands, no matter what the Arcane does, no matter what external situation the Guild is in, I stand my ground.\nMy opinion on the matter is the same as it ever was. I will not join you, and if you choose to continue on this path I will be forced to bring the matter of your behavior to our superiors.\nI have not done so yet purely out of mutual respect.\n\nOne I like to think we both share.", ""),
        StaticBook("A Letter (7/7)", "TehLulu", "[!] The book seems to contain the transcription of one part of a series of letters. They all seem to be written by the same hand, implying perhaps that one side of the exchange wrote copies of the letters, for some reason...\nThen you are a fool, and when the time comes and I find what I seek, it certainly will not be you whom ever sees it, save for perhaps in practice. Unlike yourself and your superiors, I have not wasted my time, effort and finances. Unlike your family, I\nstill hold significant assets, in nearly every corner of the world. I own significant land to the west of Malinor, a majority share in a mining company in the eastern mountain range, several logging camps here, in the Northern forests, and have invested\nheavily in a successful caravan of merchants that travels the roads, indeed trading in such successful settlements as far flung as Rynak. \n\nAs for manpower, every whisper your apprentices have heard of? Every tale\nof mages bringing the subject of debate up in their classes? All of them owe allegiance to me, and believe in my cause. For as many eyes as you have, I have triple. For every set of ears, the same. \n\nIf the echelon will not support me, they are no\nsuperiors of mine. If indeed I have no superiors, then why is it I would tolerate such an insult to my person as to be assigned to such a far-flung assignment as the frozen north, while you are showered with praise and granted such illustrious positions\nas you have been? You are not my better by any means, and while once you were my equal, if you bend the knee to those inferior to I then surely you are no longer. You are just the same as they. Content to sit and rot with the same thing, day in and day\nout, until the end of time. Content with mediocrity, you would allow the Guild to rot around you. And when it does, what will you and they do then? Sit in the skeleton of its remains, likely, and complain of the draft. \n\nBut I? I will not sit idle. I will\nbe the winds of change, even if I have to use every last resource at my command to usher it in. While you fade into obscurity, I will save not only the Guild, but our people. \n\nAnd you shall pray that I succeed, for my purpose is righteous\nand only in my cause can be found a defense against the threat, a light against the shadow which looms over us and has since the time of Dworkin, the time history has forgot.", ""),
        StaticBook("A Letter to Kale", "Lorena Faeliel", "*Upon the parchment would be neatly written lettering.*\n\nMiss Kale,\n\nI have encountered an issue. It would seem that the copy of your medical book I had has been stolen and as such, I am no longer\nable to sell it. If possible, would I be able to acquire a copy of it from you that we could sell?\n\nI also hope your royalties found you well. \n\nIf you have any concerns, please let me know.\nSigned, \n\n\n          -Lorena", ""),
        StaticBook("A moldy journal", "Beltran", "[!]The script below is written in a shaky, but experienced hand. As you read through the journal, the letters slowly turn red as ink is replaced with blood.\n\"It grips me, this madness. It coils through my bones, the very sickness in my flesh. The holes have started to appear, symptoms of this unholy disease. I fear our time is at an end.\n\nBrother Grimsby has advised us to pray to our lord,\nso that the lord's water may sweep us away to peace, but what peace shall I know if these waters are the very root of our sickness? Even now, I ponder upon the legitimacy of our god.\"\n\n\n1570 Brother Hosei\n\"Brother Lackmin was the first to fall out of our congregation. We buried his screaming corpse in our first grave. The other's are no better. Even now, I see the rotted specks of black upon their eyelids, hint of the corruption within them.\nThe great serpent has made it's visit again. It seems to detest our diseased bodies. Though I write this entry by candlelight, I still remember it's yellow eyes.\"\n\n\n\n1570 - Brother Hosei\n\"Brother Grimsby was the last who fell, except for me. Else the hint of rot on my body, I do not seem to be sucumbing to the rot as my fellows. I call it blessed, but Grimsby named me foul. 'God's water does not wash you away to his embrace' he chortles-\nas he reanimates. I had no choice but to smash his skull open, less he does the same to me.\"\n\n\n\n\n1570 -Brother Hosei\n[!]The script below appears in a chaotic, loopy script, a noticeable difference from the previous pages.\n\n\"It is me. I am chosen. I kneel at the alter and he speaks to me. The crimson waters flow from my flesh but yet he demands more.\n'a sacrafice, a sacrafice' they whisper in my ears. My Brother's voices pester for life, but even stab after stab, slice after slice, they do not abide.\"\n\n\n\n\n1571- Brother Hosei\n[!]A picture of a snake fills the entire page, printed in crimson are the words 'It Watches, my time has come.'", ""),
        StaticBook("A Note", "Lorena Faeliel", "*A note would be wedged into a crate of winebottles. The contents of each a viscious black liquid. The note reads:*\n\n\"Love, your 'friend' left this crate of. . what ever here for you. She spoke about a 'Loss of Independance'\nand how they would stall that. She also said, and I quote\n'Good luck with that whole payoff thing.'.\n\nI do not know what she is talking of and quite honestly, it is concerning.\n\n-Lorena.\"", ""),
        StaticBook("A Note...", "Vladimir Tahorran", "Dear friends,\n\nWith the departure of my dear husband-to-be, I feel as though I can no longer remain in this city. The area holds too many memories for me.\n\nAs such, I have chosen to depart in search of a new home.\nI take only a small handful of food and a tent under which to sleep to aid in my journey.\n\nI bid you all farewell.\n\nSincerely,\nVladimir Tahorran", ""),
        StaticBook("A Poem ((1))", "Sofetios", "The Cobbler\n                    and\n                The Crow\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n                         -By Sofetios\nI was working in the night,\nAs I bent over a shoe,\nI was working very hard,\nto finish before the morning dew.\nI was just about finished,\nWhen what was this I hear?\nA tapping, slight tapping,\nAnd it seemed to be so near.\nI turned to the window,\nAnd what did I see?\nA crow tapping at my glass,\nStaring at me.\n\"Go away.\" I said,\nSternly as I did stand.\n\"Don't you know I have work to do?\"\nThough I know it didn't understand.\nBut the crow kept tapping at the glass,\nAnd I began to get mad,\nThis crow just would not stop,\nNot even a tad.\nI turned to my unfinished shoe,\nTo work as long as I could bear,\nBut then I noticed something missing,\nThat would stop me from completing the pair.\nThe golden lace that I needed,\nFor this order to be complete,\nI couldn't find it anywhere,\nAs the glass the crow did beat.\nI checked my table, checked my pockets,\nAnd then to my surprise,\nThere was a hole in one,\nThat was just the right size.\nI would never finish this shoe,\nBy the morning light,\nNow things were dire,\nand my situation was quite tight.\n\"I must have dropped it,\nOn the way back from the store!\"\nBut it was too far I thought,\nAs my confidence tore.\nBut then I realized \"What's this?\"\n\"Where was the tapping I couldn't bare?\"\nI looked to the window,\nAnd the crow wasn't there.\nI went to the window,\nAnd what was in its place?\nIt was simple and divine,\nThe golden lace.\nI opened the window,\nAnd took it with care,\nBecause of who gave this gift,\nI was certainly aware.\nI went to my table,\nAnd finished the shoe.\nBut then thought of the crow,\nIf I only knew!\nI then went to bed,\nWith not much I could say,\nI only did hope,\nThe crow would come back some day.\n\nThe End", ""),
        StaticBook("A Sealed Scroll", "Caliph Faiz Kharadeen", "Dearest David Campos or any Salvian official,\n\nI hope all is well within the nation of Salvus, may prosperity continue to reign over your lands! I bring you news over the terms of allianceship between our two humble nations.\nThe council meeting of advisors has just finished, and I write this letter fresh after the meeting. \n\nThe council unfortunately have come to the decision of declining any sort of allianceships/defensive pacts\nat this time. 9 against the idea of a defensive pact while 3 in favor. It is a unanimous decision, I am afraid. \n\nEach one has brought up convincing arguments agianst a defensive pact, such as how both our militaries are not prepared\nfor warfare, and the many disadvantages to going to war if it comes to it. We spoke long and plenty on it, and I have to agree with my advisors on this. I am no leader if I desregard what my people want. I am sorry.\n{\"text\":\"\"}\nMay peace be with you and your people, perhaps in the future things can turn in a different direction.\n\nSigned by,\nCaliph Faiz Kharadeen", ""),
        StaticBook("A Short Walk", "Rilkor Niwarsay", "Book One of The Locksmith of Dwedmal Isle\n-\n((Written by Arctic_Guard))\n-\nThere once lived a man who had a simple job by the name of Haladries. He\nenjoyed his life as his work was indeed something he admired. It wasn’t a difficult job either, except for the slight inconveniences that come with the task. In his workshop he worked all day and night making the fruits of his labor. He didn’t receive\nmany visitors at his workshop besides the occasional customer who would arrive to request he made them a mechanism that had a convenient way of keeping things safe. He rarely turned away business as the coinage is what he strode for.\nIt was what he\nlived for as he was just like every other Alradian. he dreamt of a day where he would have others working for him. He wasn’t far from his dream as he already had employed another gentleman who showed up to help around his workshop during the second\nsunlight hours. His paid assistant went by the name of Larahel. Haladries thought of him as a peculiar fellow as Larahel rarely spoke, but rather did what he was told. Haladries would sometimes try to conversate with Larahel, but\nLarahel never replied.\nOn night Haladries was returning to his workshop from the blacksmith down the road. He had a small grenum box under his right arm as he strode lightly down the cobble pathway of the quiet port town. His loose\nflimsy shirt rustled in the breeze. Haladries was only a few buildings away from his workshop as he squinted to see the sign hanging outside with the candle light gleaming off of it’s beveled letters. He smiled as he thought of getting out of the dark,\ncold night and into his warm, illuminated workshop. His smile quickly faded away though as the wind mysteriously stopped blowing as if by sudden impulse.\nHe slowed his walk speed to a stop on the side of the road as he felt the nervous feeling\nthat there was an abnormal presence on the last stretch of the road to his workshop. It was as if something were different and that he was being watched. Haladries clenched his chest as he found it difficult to breathe. His knees were failing him\nand he was slowly falling to his knees. He dropped the grenum box as it fell to the craggy stone road. The sound as it hit the ground upon which he was falling to hurt his ears as it’s metallic thud became so loud he couldn’t think.\nAfter the ringing\ndied in his ears all he could hear was a loud thudding. It couldn’t be his heart as his hand didn’t feel any beating. His knees hit the road in front of him and the pain that coincided with it was unlike any other he had felt before. His eyes were\nstrained to a point that anything bright pierced his eyes with a burning feeling of anguish. His gaze shifted from the cobble road to his destination before him. The lights were so bright he could hardly see.\nThen, out of the blinding light he\nalmost thought he could see the light shift as if the door to his workshop had opened. A tear crept it’s way out of one of his burning eyes. It’s journey was halfway down his weathered face as a voice came from before him. It was a voice he heard only\nearlier that day.\n“Haladries,” they said as they strode down the stoney path, “You’re late.”\nHaladries closed his eyes and a few moments later he opened them. His vision had regained its strength and he no longer felt the cobble road’s\nrough edges on his knees. He was startled slightly as he blinked several times, amazed he looked down to see that he was standing. He still had the box under his right arm. He looked over to the person approaching him. It was the customer who\nasked for the internal lock made for the chest he carried from the blacksmith earlier that day.\n“I came to check on your progress and I see you have yet to start,” said his customer. “You do know that it is very important that you\ndeliver this lock box to me as soon as possible, yes?”\n“I- I do, yes,” he replied in the common tongue of the Alradian to his customer. He struggled to utter a few more words, but his parched throat was demotivated as the hooded\nominous figure for a customer stopped only a few steps away from him and began to speak.\n“I don’t think you understand. This is very important for you to finish this, I came all across Alradia to find the best locksmith to secure one of the\nmost sought after relics in all of Upper Harath. If it is stolen, because I can’t have it secured in an indestructible box under lock and key, I will make sure the First Light doesn’t favor your deathbound essence,” hissed the disgruntled and\nfurious toned customer.\nHaladries stumbled backward a few steps as the fire of the man’s stern words licked his face with more fury than that of the first sun. “Of course,” he subtly spoke as if a mouse were to pacify a colossal\nhumgern. “I- I already have the lock and- and key prepared!” he added with fear in his voice. “All I need to-”\n“Just finish the job!” shouted the customer with malicious grievance in his input.\n“Alright sir!” shouted Haladries as he\nquickly hurried off to his workshop’s door. His hand shaking, he reached into his pocket in search for the key to his locked workshop door. He felt the tip of his fingers touch a metal object and then his hand jolted forth to grip it in whole and fish\nit from the pond that was his pocket. He pushed the key into the lock twisting it until the door gave in and he pushed it open with his free hand and hurriedly closed it behind him.\nHe anxiously sat the grenum box down with a metallic thud onto his\nworkbench. It fell with such weighted force the mechanism on the other end jumped at the impulse. Haladries, his hand trembling he moved over to the window at the front of his workshop as he looked back at where his customer approached him.\nHaladries was surprised as there was no one there. He looked out into the darkness as he thought for the moment. His eyes widened as his head pulled back from the window.\nHaladries’ gaze shifted to his door which was locked.\nHe was shocked as he remembered seeing his customer exit from his locked workshop. He stumbled forth pushing past a chair to the door as he gripped the handle and relocked the door. He let out a deep breath as he stepped back. He\nfelt almost safe again and he strode back to his workbench where the metal container sat with a mechanism ready to be put inside.\nThrough the rest of the short night until dawn he equipped the metal lockbox with a functioning internal lock and key.\nRight as he finished inputting the last bolt he heard knocking at the front of his workshop’s door. He picked up the lockbox and moved toward the doorway to the front of his workshop. He was admiring the shine of the grenum container with key in lock as\nhe walked into the front of his shop. He noticed something out of the corner of his eye.\nHe glanced over to it for a split second as he walked to the door he then looked back to what he carried. With his realization he looked over to what he had\nglimpsed at and it was his customer walking toward him. Haladries looked very confused as the hooded person took the box from his hands. The customer then flipped a token into the air, it spun and as it did, the light bounced off of it’s very green\nsheen. Haladries held out the palm of his hand as it fell into it.\nHe looked down to it, brows furrowed as he squinted to the strange payment. It had an unfamiliar language written around the face. It followed the irregular curvature. When\nHaladries looked up from it to ask the customer what it was, they were gone. Haladries looked back down to the token as he inspected it, admiring its unique form. It was made of a metal unfamiliar to Haladries and he had no idea what it was.\nNonetheless, he shoved it into his pocket and carried off tirelessly back to work.\nOn the next night he was busy at work in his workshop when he heard another knock at the front of his door. He looked up from his workbench and walked toward\nthe front of his establishment to answer the door. As he paced through the front room, he saw a bright flash of light at the doorknob of the locked door. Suddenly the door practically exploded into his building into millions of pieces.\nHaladries\nturned away covering his face as he stumbled back to the doorway into the rest of his workshop. Without question he began to run to the back and up the stairs. His footwear thudded on the wooden stairs as he skipped as many as he could in as\nshort of a time as possible. His breathing was intense and he quickly swallowed the salty air from the sea as his dry throat burned. He heard another explosion from underneath and he held himself against the wall to his left as his\nworkshop began folding inward on itself.\n“Oh no, please!” he shouted in a scared tone. The makeshift establishment gave in to the unknown force dragging the walls to the ground below. The wall before him caved inward as it was ripped from it’s\nplacement and it smashed into the wooden flooring of the second floor. The rest of the walls fell suit and Haladries ducked out of the way of the debris just in time. A monstrous echoing roar came from below unlike anything natural over the cracking\nand splintering of wood.\nHaladries scurried to his feet as he made way for the now open wall where a window sued to be at the front of his workshop’s second floor. He held his hands over his head, running as the roof fell apart above\nhim. Running and running he kept moving, so close to freedom into the abyss of nightfall. He was almost there and the roof finally collapsed. Running, running, jumping. He lept out of the falling building and he fell to the street below.", ""),
        StaticBook("A small note", "Stephanos", "\"This bouquet is from me, for you with love.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nA secret admirer of yours.", ""),
        StaticBook("A Snelf's Greed", "Jacob 'sharpblade' Jackstorm", "Once upon a time there was a snow elf prince. He wasn't a bit of a cunt; he was a massive cunt. As thus, he eyed Shreck's Swamp with some pretty cheeky greed, this is where the cunt crossed lines into being a bloody well idjit. You see, Shreck's might\nknows no ends, he's fucking tougher then me mum and she'll fucking bite your ear off for talking shit about her aweful onion pie. Anyways, Shreck found the fuck out, ork's connections are bloody everywhere, found one in me soup once. Tasted nice.\nSo, upon his majestic steed he set out from his swamp in the orkish desert until he got to his shoddy little wall. With a single leap he was over the walls. Killing the entire camp he moved to fucking RKO him, breaking his face, after a backflip drop\nkick. Then Shreck killed the snow elf prince and everyone lived okay. Except the snelves; snelves were fucking dead.\n\nAnd that, kids, is why you stay out of the blue cunt's fucking swamp.", ""),
        StaticBook("A Theif v1:book1", "homijoe", "Clerics\n-Theifs guide\n<vol. 1\n\nLenfarthing farms,\nacross to the homes and over the two bridges, one made from Oak and thinned branches, another made from a tree root itself..\nA third, leading past..\nThe Dark forest, spiders amidst and mushrooms and webs of great, holds the trail of which to follow. Mages live here, up the stone hilltop to \ntheir home. Sheep's wool, a farm, and a gatehouse with streaming fountains of crisp water.\nThe second gate, may not always be open, be weary of guards and mages about. The stove rom, on the right... two store cuboards guard chests with simple single padlocks, a trapdoor leading to the great storeroom..\nGuarded by many a locks of steel...\nTo the left, a homely passage with a golden chest without any locks atall... an easy find.\nStraight through past the shrine room, a chest stands in the center... Locked but within holds armor of gleaming iron infused with light abound. Staffs and Axes too\nLastly, the barracks... homes of guards and tennants mustered about carelessly. Doors unlocked and chests unstowed, and easy pick of litter when it comes to fine linen or apparel worth stealing. The masters room is guarded by doors and padlocks.\nDifficult\nOn your travels....\nbe stealthy, speak to noone, if perchance you are caught....\n\"I wish to speak to the master of this establishment on a merchantry business offer...\"\n\"Perhaps I may stay longer?\"\n-Spriggar Stormhammer\n\nCompany Owner and servant of his Kingdom of the Dwarves\n\nUntil we stand, we forever hold ourselves as fools, frightened, weak..\nUntil we fight we will not be free.  *1445", ""),
        StaticBook("A Tualan Tale", "Kareem Ibn-Yrdam", "Once, long, long ago, there was a lonely man. \nHe woke up alone, with no recollection of his identity or his origin. He only knew that he was alone in the wilderness, and he urgently needed to get shelter, so he could more easily recover. Examining his\nsurroundings, the man, whom we shall give the name 'Steve', noticed a chest nearby, with torches placed around it. Tentatively, he opened the container, and to his surprise, the contents were not malicious. Inside were a couple of apples, a crude stone\naxe, and a bundle of sticks. Ecstatic, Steve would extract the tools and materials from the chest. Afterwards, he snuffed the torches, and tucked them into the hem of his trousers. He hacked the chest into pieces to be used later, and he began his trek.\nMost of a day later, Steve arrived  in a small village near the edge of the forest. The people were friendly, and highly willing to trade and harbor Steve for a time. Steve recovered in the tiny hamlet for a month's time. During this period, he made\nfriends with many of the villagers.  When the time came to leave, both the residents and Steve felt reluctant to say goodbye- and so they did not.  With the help of his new friends, Steve built himself a home at the top of a hill just outside the hamlet.\nSteve began his own farm, and even began producing iron tools from a mine he established nearby. Soon enough, Steve was the most prosperous member of the community. He had married a village girl, and had children. The people had even voted him to be the\nChief. Life was going well, until one day, a child came screaming from the meadows beyond the forest. She managed to stutter out that there were strange men coming, with large swords and axes. Steve quickly rallied the village  militia and sortied out to\nmeet the foreigners. The ensuing battle was bloody, but resulted in the invaders being driven away, though at the cost of many fighters from both sides. Afterwards, Steve and his closest retainers went off on a trip to all the nearby settlements, to rally\nall the men,  as word had come that the invaders were strong, and weretaking land in the south. The result of these talks with neighboring settlements was Steve taking command of a large mixed army, and the absolute rout of the invaders at the decisive\nbattle that would win the war. Upon his return, the people cheered for him, and he was so loved that he was installed upon the throne. He brought all of the villages together into one kingdom, a nation which he dubbed, 'The Kingdom of Tuala.' He ushered\nin a new age of prosperity, bringing about  the Golden Age of Tuala.  He and his people lived happily ever after, and to this day, Steve's descendants rule Tuala in a far away continent.\nFIN.", ""),
        StaticBook("A worn diary", "Sillice Nightingale", "Day 1 of the journal.\n\nI met a small elf named Leyun. She saved me from a room I was locked in. She helped me remember Aeras who I promised I try and remember. She then told me he visits this other circle. I still dont understand two circles. Or druids\nbut she led me here where I met a druid named Izzy. She had white hair and yellow eyes and was very nice to me. This person named Art was here too but he seemed to sad. My name is Sillice from what the others tell me and I forget things. I just hope this\ndiary helps me..\n\n5th, First Seed 1493\n\nI found Aeras again and remembered him. It took me a while but I did it. I...still cant remember anything in my past though. A strange human came into the grove. he frightened me then\nattacked this weird creatured that wandered it. It was made of mud and looked like a woman. but the mud didnt attacked back and actually looked scared. I do not know what to think.\nDays have passed..months..now years..only memories I hold lie within this diary of mine. Even now, I cannot even remember why I hold onto it so. The name 'Aeras' in my mind yet like an echo, its been slowly fading away. I wondered back to what looked like\na grove, but it was charred and the air hurt my lungs. I started crying, but I didn't know why. So I left and kept walking.\n1st Malin's welcome 1498\n\nIt's nice here. So many beautiful things in this small grove. There is another further down the hill. Even the elf city looks nice. I might look around some more to see if I find what I am searching for. Aeras....who is he?\nI found a small village. Oakhearth is the name. Its so nice and peaceful here. As if I were in a dream. I felt like I was being watched then a high elf appeared from the trees. She was so beautiful and elegant. The Owl Druid is what she called herself.\nDruid....it sounds familiar. She was very quiet then gave me one of the houses here for me to live in. I didn't want to impose on her for a place yet she dissappeared once more. I felt being watched again yet somehow I wasn't afraid. Like protected.\nIt's summer. The little farm within the village is so beautiful with the wheat growing. I was trying to harvest some when a male elf started talking with me. Adorellan..that was his name. He looked familiar yet I didn't know why. Like a friend long lost\nto me. He knew me and of my past. He then told me my real name.\n'Winteer'\nThat name...it scares me, but I do not know why. He took me to the druid grove and then I remembered, he took me before and hurt me. Forcing me to remember something I did, but I\ncouldn't remember. Those eyes still scare me. Haunt my dreams. He said his sorry to me and wanted to help me get my memory back. He went to the druids up on the tree. The tree was so pretty and beautiful, almost like a dream. Lillith, that was her name.\nShe was angry and was going to hurt him. She was frightening me. I thought druids were suppose to be friendly...Adorellan lead me away from her and we left the tree.\nDeep Cold, 1499\n\nI guess I haven't written in here in awhile. I have such interesting adventures it would seem yet I can only remember on paper. I am currently tending a farm in the dwarven lands....I think? I am unsure. It is quite cold here\nbut I do not mind so much. It's peaceful. The word 'Druid' again. It appears I am always finding them. My memory is slightly returning. I remember the villege I live at and I visit there often. A merchant just moved in there. I hope he likes the place as\nmuch as I do.\n13th, First Seed, 1503\n\nI recrieved a letter today from that high elf that helped me. I am starting to remember people some. I was asked to bring some crops to this cleric named Ramza at the druid grove. He was friendly.\nThis place is so dark...a voice. I followed it here. A voice of distress, echoing from this cave. getting louder as I drew near. The voice I could make out more. The sound of a female in distressed. I called back but all went silent for a moment before\nshe started calling again. The cave was dark but I hestiently went. I want to help. It was so long and deep then I tumbled and fell and here I am...where? I don't know. like an old library of sorts. A cave-in it appears happened here. No one has been here\nin a long time. I dont think I can get back up the rumble, but that voice...its soothing and soft. echoing in this room...calming me, but I can't find her. Perhaps i will look around more when I recover some more from the fall. I just hope my bread last\nuntil I am able to climb out of here or someone is able to find me...", ""),
        StaticBook("ABC with M. Mali", "Gallidron Elindrow", "A B C\n       with     Martin\n            Mali\n~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~\n\nThis book is intended to help the young elf or other individuals who would learn the beautiful language of the Children of Malin.\n[!]\n\nIn the book one would find a page for every character in the Elvish alphabet, of which there are many. Each letter has a colorful drawing of a thing who's name starts with that character.", ""),
        StaticBook("Adam's Bravery", "Vannessa Longridge", "Chapter 1:The Sighting\n\nThey stood proudly atop the hill, the wind raging around them, their hair fighting against the tide. Swords in their sheathes, bows on their backs and arrows in their quiver, they made their way down, the 2 brothers.\n\"Are you sure that is where he lives?\"Asked Ibraheem. Adam merely nodded. They made their way to the base of Marbrand and started scaling the walls. \n\nChapter 2: Jory\nHis face was weathered, sculpted out of pure hate.\nHe stole and murdered for joy. The 2 brothers were here for just that. Steal back what was rightfully theirs, their father's sword. They creeped into Jory's bedroom skilfully when Ibraheem stepped on a loose plank. CREAK. Swift as an arrow, Jory swung his\nsword towards Ibraheem, but Adam skillfully parried the sword, bringing his dagger down on Jory's chest. In one swift movement, Adam had saved his brother's life and ended the cruel lord's life. They exited with the sword in their hand and proud of\ntheir deeds.", ""),
        StaticBook("Adunian Poems", "Hamish", "Open your eyes and watch the sky,\nto see the blue and the white,\nand view the stars that roam up above\nand watch the birds in flight.\nTo see the seasons change with time\nand the people grow and age,\nto see the kindness in their hearts\nand their dark eyes that hold their rage.\nOpen your eyes to notice the difference\nbetween the rumours and praise,\nfrom the people who honestly care\nto those that could not be more blasé.\nTo notice the change from black to grey\nfrom purple to blue, orange to red,\nand see green turn to yellow,\nthe rich comforted and the hungry get fed.\nOpen your eyes to watch the pain\nthat others hide from the rest.\nThey might be the man on the street\nor the masquerade, best dressed. \nNotice the seasons wash the air new\nto make room for their colours, \nand make the people change their clothes\nas well as watch out for others.\nOpen your eyes to their lives going by,\nto their hopes flickering and fading,\nthe things they want most leaving\nand their dreams gone from trading.\nWatch out for a puddle of tears\nnear a gravestone of the loved,\nand see those who longed to be touched\nas they keep their fingers gloved.\nOpen your eyes to see the compassion\nthat seems to flow in this realm,\nfrom the caring and the kindness\nto the comfort that can overwhelm.\nBut also take note of the depression\nthat sits at the bottoms of the lakes, \nthat rots on the tree branches\nand in the corners of the crates.\nOpen your eyes to see what I see, \nthe final realisation;\nthat with every new begining\ncomes yet another civilisation.\nBut just rest and take it all in,\nwith your eyes wide open\nand see this shattered realm like me,\nwith my heart completely broken.\n{\"text\":\"\"}\nShattered,\nThe fact that I am broken,\nBroken,\nEven with what I said;\nTattered, \nMy heart is thankful for use,\nSmashed,\nEven though all out my heart bled.\nHalting,\nMy world, but seems to keep spinning,\nCrushed,\nAs my mind searches for support;\nImperfect, \nHe is gone forever as they said,\nUneven, \nAnd I did not have the energy to retort.\nDisturbed,\nThey told me, when I asked what happened,\nSnapped,\nThey said, when he fell with my heart,\nDefeated,\nHe was, as he was carried outside,\nCracked,\nThey described he was not that smart.\nStopped, \nas I cannot hear it anymore,\nDestoryed,\nmy heart to the very core,\nAltered, \nas I think its beat has changed,\nDefective,\nand it has made me beyond poor. \nIs,\nwhat a time that this has been,\nWhat,\nI have been through is too much,\nI,\nonly wanted to see him again,\nFeel,\nhis heart and his touch.\nMy,\nThree, dear Three, bless him,\nElton,\nfor he was my net under the ledge,\nIs,\nhe really, truly with you now?\nDead,\nand gone, straight over the edge?\n{\"text\":\"\"}\nHis green eyes never leave my gaze\nand his beard will always be brown,\nhis kilt never lost its colour\nas he kept not a trace of a frown.\nHis high stature has yet to be forgotten\nwhat with his boom laughter and grin,\nand his highspirits not only with his family\nbut also in the tavern with his gin.\nHis comforting words kept me going\nas his form took shape in my mind,\nfor it was I who kepts his memories,\nit was I that he never left behind.\nI cannot say I enjoyed all of it\nfor that man saw too many wars,\nbut he also had his moments of triumph,\nand those he loved dearly of course.\nHe kept his mind straight and level\neven under the most pressure,\nhe always knew where his place was\nand respected those who were lesser.\nSometimes he forgot right from wrong,\nbut then again, we all to at some point,\nand yet he knew how to keep people motivatied\neven though he was hard to dissapoint.§\nAnd though I miss him still to this day,\nwith years gone since he passed,\nI still wish Hanrahan was here with me,\nbut for now his memories will stay glassed.\n{\"text\":\"\"}\nI tried to paint you with the finest paints,\nto bring your green eyes and brown hair,\nbut the picture simply slipped from my mind,\nand on the canvas you were not there.\nI had the colours perfectly mixed,\nmy brush poised and ready,\nbut when it came to shaping your face,\nI could not keep my hand steady.\nI ended up painting a landscape;\nit was one that I knew all too well,\nit had trees in the back and buildings around,\nbut little did I know that I fell.\nI watched my hand with curiosity,\nnot knowing what it was doing, \nand suddenly I forgot where I was,\nand even the tea I had brewing.\nI forgot my life and all my memories,\nI forgot who I had lost,\nI only knew about the place at hand,\nand I could not see the lines I crossed.\nI backed into my own painting\nof the trees and of the buildings,\nand backed right into the old Clinic of ours\nand I found the stash of old kindlings.\nI relit the fire just behind the counter,\nand leaned on it as I looked towards the door,\nI had washed the floors and that silver bell,\nand waited for your arrival once more.\nAs I stood in the Clinic of Memories\nI seemed to trick myself,\nbut at the time I could not see you\nas I restocked the shelf.\nYou walked in quietly as you always did,\nand I hung my coat on a hook.\nBut I could tell that I was not alone\nas you stood in that corner and looked.\nI turned around to see your expression,\nthose green eyes sunked into your face,\nand when you told me simple words that calmed me,\nthey helped me find my place.\nYou stepped closer and hugged me,\nyour words were soft and kind,\n\"You will be all right, I promise,\" you whispered,\nand suddenly it was I who was hard to find.\n\"Can't I just go back to the Clinic?\"\nI almost cried as you came near,\n\"Yes, anything you want, I promise,\nbut for now, Arial, you must stay here.\"\nSomething happened just then, \t\nwhen you promised I would be okay.\nI knew that the person I was then\nwould not be the person I am today.\nFor now I know that Arial\nwas the one who you wanted to protect,\nso she took her things and kept them safe,\nleast she be the one I forget.\nAnd now your dreams can come true,\nfor your daughter now walks these lands,\nand I can assure that Arial will be all right, \nbecause she is in your own hands.\nShe may call herself Annabelle,\nbut that is only for your sake,\nbecause she knows your daughter is in her\nand her soul was always yours to take.\nBut I did lose myself in the Clinic that day,\neven though it was just a painting.\nAnd even though it was all in my mind,\nI know it was real and Arial is still waiting.\nShe will wait for you until the end of time,\nuntil the brush is stea\nuntil your face can be painted again\nand until you think she is ready.\n{\"text\":\"\"}\nPostscript.\n\nA collection of poems\nwritten by a famous Adunian poet in the late Anthosian period.\n\nLet her memory be charished by all those who read her delightful words.\n\n-Hanrahan Brae", ""),
