        StaticBook("Life Of Thurak", "ilikefooddude", "The Life Of Thurak'Braduk\n=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=Thurak has only had two times in his life when he was really, undeniable happy. Two meagre, short lived moments to look back on for the rest of his life that he can safely say that he was happy.\nThe first was long ago,\nin his early childhood as a Cub of Raurna and Ghazkull Braduk. For a time, he\nlived without worry; his mother was caring, (in an Orcish way of course) and his father was there for him, training him proudly in the ways of being an\nOrc, and teaching him how to wield his chosen weapon, the hammer. Till the fourth\nyear of his infancy, such was Thurak's life, without worry or need to worry, and the days passed quickly and pleasantly.\nThe ancestors must have looked down\non Thurak, though, the day his happiness was swept away, and decided that he was\nnot destined to have this life, carefree and happy.\nThe air was bitter and cold on that silent desert night. Winds blew the sand\naround into small willy willies which swirled around in endless, untraceable\ncircles.\n\n'STOMP' went the boots when they clashed with the sand.\n'STOMP' went the footwear of the Orc of the Ugluk clan. Looking, searching was one pair, on patrol, was the other. The stomping stopped, as one pair neared\nthe other. A voice came, booming from the mouth of the owner of the loudest\nboots. Quieter, softer, came the voice of the Cub in response. Looking, searchin, was the cub.\n\"Where is mother? Where is father?\"\nIt asked, to be replied by the slide of a sword from its\nsheath.\n\"Follow or die,\" Accompanied the voice of the sword.\nThurak folllowed.\n10 years, Thurak stayed. Positions were reversed. Once his clan, now his enemy. Once his enemy, now his clan. Why\nthey did not come, he knew not. He questioned them, but did not hate them. 10 years of belief being crushed.\nMaybe tommorow, he said before he went to sleep on the dirt floor, next to the\nempty bed of his new father. Maybe next\nweek, he soon was saying. Maybe\nnever. His hope was crushed. Happiness, now such a sad memory to behold.\nArriving at the gate, another traveller.\nThurak thought nothing of the darks skinned Uruk as he entered the gates of his unwilling home, paying no more attention to him than any other of the passing by Uruks. This one was far from home, but close to\nBlood. Unrecognised, he went, by his enemies, and by his friends, until alone he was, with Thurak.\n\"What clan are you?\" He asked.\n\"Braduk,\" came the response. A name Thurak had not heard for too long.\nHope came then,\nrushed in, the flood gates of his heart now open. \"Take me back,\" he pleaded, answered by a yes. It sounded strange, a word of hope and willingness where the young cub had thought there was none.\n\"No welcoming party for me.\" Ignored,\nhe should have expected. It hurt more now, hope of happiness is the easiest crushed. Time rushed by, though not like before. Now he strove for attention,\nwhere once he had an endless supply.\nOne Cub, two Cubs, three Cubs. Thurak was forgotton in the crowd, truly more alone than when he was alone.\nAt 18, Thurak was tired of holding it in. His youngest brother, Gor, was\ntroubling his Rhino, swinging his axe down on its toe. Patiently, Thurak asked\nGor to stop, an axe, flying at him, was the response. Anger, hatred. It filled Thurak, and bloodlust\nCame upon him.\nIt was a good kick; his brother must have gone a few meters.  Again, Gor troubled him, another good kick. The third time, Thurak lost all of his precious inhibitions. His brother, the leech, who stole everything Thurak needed so dearly, had\nDriven Thurak deep into bloodlust. Gor must have seen this, and had retrieved his axe, sending it spinning towards Thurak's neck. It was a good throw.\nJust as Thurak was about to launch another powerful kick at Gor, in came Durakai, pushing Thurak away,\ndepriving him of his vengeance.\nLater that day, a clan meeting was held, over what to do about Thurak. Exiled?\nNo, Thurak himself thought he deserved it, and would have accepted the punishment without complaint. Instead, the second happy\nhappy chapter in his life beginning.\n\nHe finally had a mate, Dari. Soon, along came little Sharn, a beacon of light in his once dark world. ogether, with Dari, he taught her, as his parents had done with him, and she grew to the strong age of four.\nThurak never liked that number.\nHe woke up in his Blarg, stretching and yawning, finding no one else there, not his mate, not his Cub.\nProbably hunting,\nhe though. So he thought, until two days later. No one had seen them, not a trace nor a letter of\nWhere they had gone.\nMaybe tomorrow, he said each night before he went to sleep on the empty bed. Maybe next week, he soon was saying. \nMaybe never.\nHis hope was crushed.\nHappiness, now such a sad memory to behold.\nWith them too, the eldest of his younger brothers had gone. Nazdag. He missed him as much as his mate and child, though he did not know why.\nMaybe because they shared a bloodline, they were both in line for the position of Chieftain.\nTwo years, he went without seeing those three who he held so\ndear. Why they did not come back, Thurak didn't know. He didn't question them, he didn't hate them.\nHe loved them.\nAnother day, another scar; Thurak hobbled around the Uzg, his injury stopping him from walking at his usual pace. The Scabbernak's stinger had plunged into his right ribcage, causing quite a bit of nerve damage.\nA growing infection was hidden underneath the thick Rhino hide bandage, unknown to him and all around him. Once he reached the gate, though, he knew the pain of the walk was worth it. here, stood his brother, bigger, stronger than when he left, but still\nHis brother. \nOne down, two to go.\nHe would restore his family, whatever the cost.\n\n-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-End Of Part One\nPart Two\n=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=The last few years of Thurak's life have been the most bloody and melancholic of them all.\n\n\nFor fifteen years, Thurak searched for a hint, a glimpse, a whisper of advice\nto direct him to what he most wanted; his life mate and cub. For fifteen years, Thurak found nothing but dead ends and false leads. From Oren to Malinor, no news of the two Uruks, mother and daughter. The men in Oren blamed the Elves, just as those\nResiding in Malinor shouted claims of slavery in Oren. From them, Thurak got nothing. From the many he paid off; knights, street urchins, assassins, he heard nothing from. The nobles in their towers knew nothing, and so neither did the\nShady groups delving in dark alleyways, even when beated to an inch of their lives.\n\nThurak killed, questioned and pleaded, all for naught for ten years, before he gave up his dignity and asked the Dwarves.\nHe had expected the result, but was disappointed nonetheless. Riducules and insults were all that awaited him at the Dwarven gates, tempting him to an action he knew he couldn't take.\nFinally, after sixteen years of searching, Thurak would find what he was looking for.\n\nA small trading group, located on the far west side of the desert, was rumoured to be trading in slaves.\nSo close, all along, Thurak though as he approached the small groups of dimly lit tents, the trusty Zpidhammah raised at the ready. Seeing a sentry, Thurak ducked under a dune, narrowly missing the sweeping gaze of the torch wielding human.\nTrying again after the man had done his rounds on his side of the camp; Thurak paced into the camp, and started looking around for any sign of the two feuruks. There, after sixteen years, Thurak caught the first glimpse of his life mate and child,\nLying face down in their blood.\nSixteen years, ten minutes late, was one of the thoughts racing\nthrough his head during the mindless bloodlust that followed. Sentries screamed warnings, and men scattered as they realised what was happening.\nNone were prepared for an attack this late at night, and none could ever be prepared for the wrath Thurak brought forth that night.\nThurak stood, panting amongst the clearing. Three days after the battle was ended, no remains of the slaving camp remained, except for the two pillars marking the remains of those he so loved. Hands splintered and bruised, Thurak sat beside the graves,\nSighing.\n\nNow he could rest.", ""),
        StaticBook("Light Illusion", "Corvo", "Illusion from what we know is the use of the void to create images into one’s mind. How this is done is simple infact, First one must connect to the endless realm that is the void, A realm of nothingness and at the same time the Void is everything\nwe so wish it to be. Now let us continue, With the use of the Void we as Illusionists are able to draw an image within it, Whether it be a small petal or that of a gigantic demon.  Once an Illusionist has done so they must simply connect to the mind\nof their opponent and place the image that they have made into their mind. Allowing them to see what you infact have seen.  However one must note that whatever you are to create you must have seen or experienced in your life,\nYou can simply create that of a demon however what is the point of you do not know how it sounds or feels.  One is of course able to make simple images from what they can think of in their own mind\nas well it is possible to simply add a feeling or sense to said image. Now in order to start learning such a power that is Illusion people tend to start the same as most Mages, Connection to the Void.\nTo do this one must simply empty their mind of all thoughts thus creating a link to the Void, this is mainly done through the simple means of mediation. However some are unlucky in their attempts to connect as some might be rejected from it.\nWhen one is rejected by the Void they may feel a small headache occur or even be knocked out from intense pain. Now once one has connected to the Void they simply draw an image in it and thus try to place that image into another entity's mind.\nSome at this stage tend to try and place the image into the mind’s of animals as an animal's mind is weak and far too stupid to even figure out that what they are seeing or feeling is an Illusion.\nOnce the simple sense of images has been completed Illusionists begin to head towards the Illusions of senses, Pain and touch. This is where some Illusionist find things to be tricky, As an Illusionist must feel and how what they are creating feels,\nthat intends one must be able to survive intense pain if they are so wishing to create it. To perform these sensations. it is simple they must simply draw the image of pain inside the Void and place it into that of their target.\nSimilar to how creating images of Light work. After the Illusionist has played with senses they may now head towards their end, The Illusion of emotions. This is quite hard to do in fact. To perform it they do the same as before,\nhowever it is much more difficult to perform as if one feels happy than angry the next moment it won’t make logical sense as they will know it is an Illusion and it shall break.  The simple way to do this is to ease the Illusion in silently,\nHow this works is if one was happy one second, it is quite easy to cause them to feel the emotion of love the next. This works quite well however from what we know emotions can not be forced upon the target only eased in.\nAs such we come to an end and now I leave the rest to you, The holder of this book. \n\n((For the Light Illusion Subtype))", ""),
        StaticBook("Lileaf Coupon", "Llywellyn Oakleaf", "*&*\n           Lileaf\n         Smithing\n          -*&&*-\n\n\nCongradulations!\n\n      It appears that a Lileaf Smithing store has opened in your area! We are presenting you with a\none time use coupon for any item in our shop! This is worth 10 mina at our shop, effectively deducting 10 minae from anything you wish to purchase!\n\n   Thank you!\n\n    -Llywellyn Oakleaf", ""),
        StaticBook("Lilian Dela", "Kale le Prevost", "Caliphate Clinic\n\nHead Doctor Kale\n\nPatients Name: Lilian Delany\n\nAge: 5\n\nRace: Half-Elf\n\nGender: Female\nCondition/Injury: The child was helped to a bed by a man, found with a broken leg (clean break). The leg was broken due to a fall of of a roof at the docks.\n\nTreatment Plan: To do a basic crack-and-back to pop the bone back into its correct place, and\n- and then place a stick to allign it, then wrap it with a bandage. \nDoctor who treated the patient: Head Doctor Kale\n\nCost for Treatment: 5 minas\n\nDate: 13th of the Second Sun 1523\nEnd Result:\n\nThe child is to stay off of the leg for a weeks time before walking. She was given a crutch for if needed to get around. Her pain level was down once she left the clinic.\n*Doctor Kale's signature would be here\n\n~", ""),
        StaticBook("Logs", "Sarrion", "\"A Sellsword Company\"\nLogs Book Chapter One\n-Sarrion\n\nCreated-\n2nd of the Grand Harvest\n1513\n-Sarrion\n\nJoined-\n4th of the Grand Harvest\n1513\nBjael Alrikson:SK=Lumber\n-Sarrion\nJoined-\n6th of The Grand Harvest\n1513\nCole deGray:SK=Farm/Mine\n-Sarrion\n\nJoined-\n7th of The Grand Harvest\n1513", ""),
        StaticBook("Lonely Fisherman", "Arthur Caulfield", "The Lonely\n         Fisherman\n\n   A short literary\n        Piece\n\"Now I don't know a whole lot about fishing,\" he confesses. \"For twenty long years been trying. They don't even notice when I cast my line into the water.\" \n    The fisherman sat alone on a boat, in the middle of a dark blue ocean that said nothing back.\n\"My bait must be cheap,\" he muttered. \"Must be me bait.\" His face scrunched up in thought. \n   His hair had started to grey, his wrinkles had become more defined. Each day it became harder to get out of bed, though he did not blame this on age.\nAfter a while the water began to ripple. Soon it gently rocked the side of his boat in waves. \n   Looking up the fisherman found another, just like him. He waved to the man in a friendly manner but was ignored.\n   Curious, the fisherman watched.\nThe man in the other boat threw his line in after a few minutes of preperation, and within seconds had caught a fish. \n   \"How unfair,\" the old man mumbled. \"Been fishing in these ponds for years and ain't caught a damn thing.\"", ""),
        StaticBook("Love Study: I", "Hileia'elr", "Love Study: I\n----------------\n\nStudies on Love and Affection, Volume 1:\n\nIntroduction to Love\n\n-Hileia'elr\nI assume you picked up this book because you wanted to learn about the capacity for care, love, and affection that our minds have. Writing a book on the topic is easier said than done, of course.\nUnlike other sciences and studies, love is something that isn't entirely observable to our eyes. Love's roots run much deeper than a material, observable, object.\nFirst, I myself write romance novels for a living. You may have read my novels before, or you may very well not have. Either way, I write about the connection between two people because I believe it's our strongest emotion.\nDefining Love:\n\nBefore we investigate what's possibly our deepest emotion, we do have to understand what it is. Really, this should be quite easy to do, yet some people overthink it.\nLove is simply the attraction that one individual has to another, and the other may or may not return the feelings. \n\nNow, keep in mind that I'm talking about all types of love.\nWhen you hear, 'Love,' you most likely think of the romantic kind. We have to realize through this series that love comes in different shapes and forms, and doesn't have to be romantic. Love can just be affection and care.\nHowever, during this series, I will be focusing primarily on romantic love, as that is the most globally recognized form of, 'Love.' Although, we will take a glance at the other varieties of love as well, which will be covered in a later volume.\nSo, what is the definition of love? While most think that this should be a complicated answer, really, it's just the special bond between two people with a positive connection.\nTo define love itself is easy. However, what is complicated is defining the purpose, or the need to love someone or something. It's like life, we can easily describe what it is, but not why it is. So, we'll take a look at that now.\nWhy do we love:\n\nThis is the harder of two questions to answer. We cannot pinpoint the exact reason that we love, other than that we want to love. As living creatures, it is instinct.\nLove is programmed into our genetic code, and it forces us to carry out that code, causing us to seek out another individual who will both accept and return that feeling. So one answer to our question is because; We're designed to.\nAlthough, with that being said, it cannot be the only reason. Other studies have proven that, someone who is raised with food, water, shelter, and other necesities for life, still can't survive without some type of love.\nSo physically, our bodies need love to go on, to survive. Love itself is a need for survival. One cannot live without it, and will die if they do not receive some type of affection or care. To recap, we need love for several reasons.\nThe first reason we love is because we want to love, and because we need to love. We love because that's what we're meant to do, and we love because if we don't, we'll suffer without it.\nSo if we can define love, get at least an idea of love's purpose, and why we love in the first place, what else is there to ask? In reality, that's the base of it, but we can go much further than that...\nThe rest of your questions we'll look into are mostly about physical needs and the standards set for dating, love, and marriage in different societies.\nWhat age is appropriate to start \"Loving\" someone:\n\nReally, this all depends on your opinion of when someone should start, 'dating.' However, there are set guidlines in some cultures.\nFor instance, an older teenager who's about to become an adult in a few years might consider dating, with their parent's permission. They may not be an adult, but they could be old enough to date.\nAlthough, in other societies, it's expected that you get involved in romantic activities AFTER adulthood hits. For instance, in High Elven culture, where 50 is considered to be an 'adult,' it is not encouraged to date before that age.\nBut whatever the case, older or younger than adulthood, it seems that becoming an adult in all societies is also the line which you cross into the romantic world. In short, the answer to this question, is when you're an adult.\nOf course, that's quite a short and incomplete answer to give. Other variables have to be considered when factoring in 'Adulthood.' The definition of adulthood itself varies, too.\nDo you want to consider someone an adult when they reach the valid age, or consider them an adult when their attitude and personality has matured as well? So really, it's a combonation of age and maturity.\nThis is the end of volume one. In volume two, we'll be classifying different types of love, or different ways love can be expressed among other people.\nI do hope whoever is reading this book has enjoyed it, as I plan to conduct a full study on the subject, and also plan to publish every research paper I create.\nUntil next volume, loyal reader!\n\n~ Hileia'elr", ""),
        StaticBook("Love Study: II", "Hileia'elr", "Love Study: II\n----------------Studies on Love and Affection, Volume 2:\n\nClassifying different forms of Love\n\n-Hileia'elr\nIn this volume, we will expand on the idea that there is not only one, but many ways you can classify love. As mentioned in the previous volume, we will be focusing mainly on romantic love.\nIn this volume, we will stray away from exploring 'Romance,' and rather dive deeper into the idea that love can be something other than such. So, we'll start by defining romantic love, then move on to some other subjects.\nRomantic Love:\n\nRomantic love is most likely what anyone thinks when talking about 'love' in general. Love is often linked to marriage, dating, and so on. Love can refer to any form of love, but is often thought of as romance.\nThe simplest way to think of romantic love, is simply the positive bond and relationship between two people, most commonly a man and a woman. Acts of romantic love often involves dating and kissing eachother.\nIf the two individuals feel so strongly of eachother, they can participate in marriage, or whatever the equivalent in a selected society. Marriage is a physical representation of two people's romantic bond.\nMore often than not a ceremony, marriage will show that both persons are willing to spend the rest of their existance together, and believe that they are compatible enough to exchange acts of love for years to come.\nParental Love:\n\nParental love is also what could be commonly thought of as 'love' in general. Parental love is the bond between the two individuals who gave birth to a child, and the child themself.\nAs I mentioned in the previous volume, our bodies and minds are designed in a way that they actually want to feel affection for others. The same concept can be applied to the connection between a parent and child.\nThe child will recognize each parent as someone who is automatically someone they should look up to, trust, rely on, and love. The same is true for the parents, as they have a special bond with their child.\nAs a parent, giving birth to a child means that the parent has participated in the creation and existance of their child, and does often feel responsible for them. Simply knowing that you've helped continue the\ncycle of life, and that you've helped continue the existance and expand the population of your race, is a feeling strong enough to believe that you are, again, fully responsible for the organism that you've brought into this world.\nSibling Love:\n\nOften reffered to as something along the lines of 'Brotherly Love,' love between siblings is something similar to parental love, seeing as both parents and the offspring are part of a primary kinship.\nThe bond between either a brother or sister is strong, as you both come from the same origin. The same two people created you, and brought you into existance.\nJust as your parents should feel responsible for you, a sibling should feel responsible for their brother or sister, and vice versa. All these relationships have existed since birth, and is, again, designed into our own minds.\nHowever, love between siblings isn't always pretty. In most families, siblings will grow up under the same roof at a young age, and will most likely, at one point or another, grow tired of eachother. Fights and arguments will break loose.\nThe most common battles between siblings are competitive, usually to prove to either friends or family which one of them is 'better.' Siblings also fight over territory. When you live in such a small house, it can get cramped.\nThat's why two brothers, two sisters, or a brother and sister will argue over who claims what in the house or family. Or, maybe one is simply annoyed by the actions and habits of the other.\nNone the less, the love between the two is designed to overcome any differences they might have. That is what's special about the relationship between siblings, the ability to forgive eachother so easily.\nThe ability to easily forgive after a fight or argument is something no other form of love has. Of course, anyone has the capacity for forgivness, but between siblings, it's especially easy.\nLove among extended family members:\n\nYour extended family includes people such as your grandparents, grandchildren, cousins and so forth. You may or may not be as close to them as your parents, siblings, or spouse.\nHowever, there is still always a bond that exists between you and every family member you have. If you live with your cousin, for instance, your relationship is likely to be much stronger than if you were apart.\nBut if that cousin does live further away, and you only see them every so often, your relationship is likely to be much weaker. Reguardless, there is still something that is triggered in our minds that tell us that this is someone close to us.\nEven if it's your first time ever meeting, when you're told that you two are related, another mechanism is instantly activated within the mind. A connection, right then and there, is formed, another bond that can't be broken.\nOvertime, you may not enjoy the company of that relative, yes, but the bond is still there, weak or strong. This bond can never be broken, and is, again, designed to exist until death.\nRelationships with friends (Or pets):\n\nI wouldn't exactly call the relationship with your friends 'Love,' but there is no doubt that a strong link exists between two people who enjoy talking and being with eachother.\nOften called a 'Friendship' for obvious reasons, the conenction between two friends can still be as strong as that with a family member- Or stronger. The idea of having a friend is that the certain individual you're friends with\nis always there to protect, compfort, give advice to, assist, and in general have fun with you. Of course, with friends, there is no everlasting bond. The chain can easily be cut off in most situations.\nHowever, that friendship could also be very strong, and hard to cut off, but it all depends on the two and their effort to maintain the relationship. Just as our need for love is designed into our minds, so is the need for friends.\nThe difference between a relationship in your family, and the relationship with your friends, is that there is no bond, as stated before, and you both have to put in equal effort to maintain that relationship.\nIf your friend runs errands for you, or any work for you at all, and you do nothing in return, that friendship is more likely to be cut off. Remember, while there is a bond between friends, there is no permanant bond.\nThe same can easily go for your pet, an animal companion. While the bond isn't identical, a bond between an 'intelligent' being and an animal can still have the same concepts of friendship applied.\nSuch as friendships, there is no permanant bond, and can be broken. The concept of returning the favor can also be put into place. Usually, a dog will protect their 'Master' in exchange for food, water, and shelter.\nThis is the end of volume two. I recieved some feedback from several people whom read volume one, and I believe that I'm using terms that not everyone would fully understand.\nIn future volumes, I will attempt to either use more simple terminology, or explain myself with many details so that the reader may understand what I'm reffering to.\nIn volume three, we'll be returning to romantic love, and research how love at first sight works, and how the mind decides what physical features we're attracted to. Until next time, loyal reader.\n\n~Hileia'elr", ""),
        StaticBook("Love Study: III", "Hileia'elr", "Love Study: III\n----------------Studies on Love and Affection, Volume 3:\n\nPhysical attraction\n\n-Hileia'elr\nAs we dig deeper into researching this emotion, tucked away in the corners of our minds, it had crossed my mind- Why do we feel a passion for people with different physical features, or certain personality traits?\nIs there some mechanism in our minds that's triggered when we see someone with features we like? Or is there a set pattern, to seek out the individuals with our preffered physical details?\nInvestigating this feeling even more, we will discover what exactly makes us 'tick.' What is it that captivates us so much, that we're lured into attempting to keep another person for our own?\nThere are so many questions to ask, and so many answers that can be provided. What interests me the most is why not every individual is attracted to the same exact features that everyone else is...\nDifferences in attraction:\n\nFor whatever the reason, not everyone is attracted to the same details or features that others of the same species, gender, and age posess.\nFor example, there could be a 50 year old, Highlander man that is attracted to farfolk women, and another 50 year old Highlander man who's into elves.\nThese are two men that are the same age, gender, race, and come from extremely similar origins- Yet their taste in women is awfully different. Should they not fancy the same type of lady?\nEven with these two men living extremely similar lives, there are small signals within our mind that are triggered, switches that are flipped, that are caused by small, trivial events within each person's life.\nReturning to the two Highlanders example- Perhaps when our first Highlander was younger, he witnessed a beautiful farfolk girl simply walking down the street near his home. He hadn't thought anything of it, he just saw her.\nNow, on the other side of that street lives our other example. At the exact same time, he sees a wonderful, majestic High Elven girl, just passing through. He's paying too much attention to her to even notice the farfolk girl nearby.\nNeither men thought anything of these encounters, as they hadn't even talked to either girl at all, and they would never see them again in their lives- But just because of how beautiful they were, that's enough to begin\ntriggering and creating a brand new signal within each of the Highlander's minds. The situation doesn't have to be exactly as the one I gave, maybe not even remotely close. But it shows how something so small and trivial can have a large impact.\nA whole other possibility is that family members influence what an individual is attracted to. Certain people will find compfort in being with someone similar to their normal surroundings when creating such a strong bond.\nIt's entirely possible, that, a man would be attracted to the same features that runs in his bloodline. If he comes from a family of mostly brown hair, even if he himself doesn't have brown hair, he may find it easier to date a girl with brown hair.\nBut then, of course, all these theories could just not apply in some cases, as a man or woman is impacted by the society they've grown up in, and are taught to be attracted to a specific group of people, with specific traits.\nSo really, the reason we're all attracted to different features is a combonation of trivial events from when we were younger, who we feel safer around, and what the enviroment around us has taught us to be attracted to.\nCommon 'Attractive' Features:\n\nAs they say, \"Beauty is in the eye of the beholder.\" However, there are still features that are commonly thought of as, 'Beautiful.'\nWhen asked, there seemed to be a very steady pattern, based off of gender and race. Men seemed to be attracted to girls with a smaller figure, longer hair, and big eyes.\nFemales seemed to generally be attracted to stronger, taller men, a nicely chisled chin, and in some cases, a well groomed beard was a must. Of course, as mentioned before, you must account for many factors.\nWith absoluetly every person you ask, there will be at least one difference in their taste for a lifemate. Two people could be attracted to the same person, however there's always a difference in people's 'Ideal partner.'\nLove at first Sight:\n\nTo some, love at first sight could make for a heart warming, romantic story. Certain people believe love at first sight to be the most pure and true sign of love.\nHowever, there are others who would be much happier thinking that this, 'Love at first sight' is ridiculous and impossible. All parties have valid evidence to support what it means to love at your first glance of another individual.\nFor instance, some would be excused to think that real life would turn out such as that in a story book. It would be nice to think that you could determine your one true love just by looking at them.\nUnfortunatley, this is not the case at all. As mentioned in previous volumes, 'Love' is defined as the bond between two people. This bond cannot be instant, and must be constructed overtime, using the effort between the two partners.\nWith this 'Love at first sight,' no chains or bonds can be established between the two individuals. There is a false connection linking both beings.\nAlthough, that's not to say that two people can build up from this false link, transforming it into that of a real relationship. One of the two can experience attraction at first sight, in which case\nthe false bond mentioned before will be established. This is entirely possible, to think that someone's physical features are ideal at your first glance. Attraction at first sight and love at first sight are quite different.\nTo answer the question, \"Is love at first sight possible,\" no, it is not. There are many ways that one could believe that they are in love, however it should be impossible for our minds to create a bond that quickly.\nBeing attracted, of course, is possible. This is because our minds will still recognize another person as 'Beautiful,' even if we haven't formed the chain, or recieved the chance to know the person.\nThis is where we will conclude volume three. In volume four, we'll be researching the reason why love is the most mysterious of all our other emotions. Until next time.\n\n~Hileia'elr", ""),
        StaticBook("Love's Ending", "Franswaar Kellian", "The day was starting to fade as the sun slowly crept across the sky towards the ever nearing horizon.  I left my home in a hurry wearing the most formal clothes I could manage to find laying around in my home. Nervous and slightly, I finally arrived at my\ndestination. She was more beautiful then ever. Her long, flowing auburn hair, striking hazel eyes, and smile were enough to make a man collaspe. The indigo dress swayed in the wind as we walked towards the ballroom.\nOur hands intertwined, I, walking a\nslightly faster pace. \nInside was full of elegant music and exquisite decor. The chandelier lit up room from above. As we began to dance together, our eyes were inseperable. Though the ballroom was filled to the point where moving was nearly impossible,\nI could only see her. Every second see smiled my stomach fluttered with astounding joy. As the night came to a close, I strolled with her until we could see the sun begin to tower over the mountains. We sat in the meadow watching the sun rise, her head on\nmy shoulder. At that moment, I knew I was in love. Months passed and seasons changed. Every spare second I had I spent it with her. She was the only I seemed to care about. Time spent apart seemed as if it were a life time. Though the day came where I was\nneeded on a cargo ship to sail across the oceans to sell goods. \n3 years were spent on the water exporting, though it felt more like 30 years. When I arrived home, all I wanted to do was see her. When I exited the boat I ran to her home. Excitement\ncoursing through, and adrenaline pumping. I arrived to be greated by a baby crawling on the lawn. Tears began to rush down my face as I learned I became a father. I knelt down to the baby, only to be screamed at by the mother, not realizing who I was.\nI stood up a looked her in the eyes, only to she her change from angry to shock. Together we began to sob as we ran into eachother's arms. We spent the rest of the day conversing about my travels and where I've been. I then asked why she didn't tell me\nshe was pregant when I left. Her reply was as if a dagger was stabbed into my heart, then was proceeded to be ripped and forced into it again. The baby was not mine. I learned that in my absence the woman I spent every moment with, the woman that I\ndevoted my life for, married another man and bore him a child. I lied as best I could fighting the tears congulating her on the new born. She began to explain that I was gone for 3 years and that she began to fall in love with another man.\nI left her home with full intent of drowning myself in the harbor. I stood on the edge of the pier, looking out into the now black water, for night has fallen, and prayed that my soul be given mercy. I shut my eyes and began to lean forward.\nHalf way into my fall or the pier, a hand ripped me away. I struggled to free myself, but I was restrained against the cold, damp floor. My 'Savior' began to scream at me, though he was tuned out as I began to rethink what I should do.", ""),
        StaticBook("Magic Dictionary", "PiercingDarkness", "The Magic Dictonary:\n\nYir'sari - True Wizard, powerful magic masters.\n\nAscended - The demi-god children of the aenguls.\n\nUndead - The evil fists of Iblees, they destroyed Aegis.\nArcane Transportation Pad - \n\nStaff - a tool used to conduct mana, as well as aid you in Void connections.\n\nWand - Akin to the staff, doesn't work as well.\nCleric - Powerful magicians based mostly in healing, they are fueled by aenguls.\n\nNecromancer - Users of Necromany, a deadly magic having to do with life drain.\n\nPyromancer - Fire evocationist, magician of fire.\nElectromancer - Users of electric magic, mostly evocationist of electricity, they are common in Anthos.\n\nEvocation - The use of summong elements from the Void.\nFire-Evocation - The summoning of mana from the Void to create fire.\n\nWater-Evocation - The summoning of mana from the Void to create water or other water related things.\nElectric-Evocation - The evoking of mana from the Void to form electricity.\n\nWind-Evocation - The use of void mana to create wind and it's similiar elements.\nEarth-Evocation - The evocation of mana to form earthen elements.\n\nShamanism - Magic having to do with the use of spirits.\n\nElemental control - A shaman magic having to do with spirits.\nMagic(k) - The use of mana to conjure things, and even to control things.\n\nMana - Life force and energies around us, used to form complex spells.\n\nNecromancy - The use of life drain to get mana to form complex\nspells.\n\nShade-Magic - Fueled by a gem, it's a shadow magic having to do with the art of tendrils, and other shadow elements.\nIllusion - A arcane magic, the use of mana to form false images and feelings, and project them upon others, it's impossible to illusion yourself.\n\nConjuration - The conjuring of living beings.\nWizard - Common term name for the Yir'sari, true wizards.\n\nArch-Mage - Ruler of the mage's guild.\n\nMage - Practicer of magic.\n\nMagician - Mage is it shortened.\nAscended Magic - Magic fueled by a patron aenguel, it is a light magic, and holy, it is akin to cleric.\n\nUndead-Magic - Fueled by Iblees, the Undead used their necromancy to begin their conquest of Aegis.\nThe Void - A place where mages draw power from to fuel their spells.\n\nDragon - A beast of huge size and intelligence, they roam Northern Anthos, hunting.\nYellow Stone Material ((Endstone))\n\nA heavily weird material, it spreads corruption of many kinds.\n\nDruidism - Magic over nature, has to do with the spirits known as the Aspects.\nAlteration - Magic that alters the properties, of real world things.\n\n-------------------\n\nMagic sayings:\n\n\"Magic seeks those who don't seek it.\"\n\"Magic is the path of ths scholar, not a insolent warrior of heavy brains and no mindset.\" \n\n\"Magic is the path of destruction, insolent mage!\" - Gavin the Druid\n-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-\n\n\"Good and evil doesn't decide how magic works, only the acts of the user.\"\n\n- The Arcane Mage\n\n\n\n-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-\nWritten by, Mythras Ardere.\n\nSources:\n\n- history of magic\n- The Clerical Order\n- The Mage's Guild\n- Salamandra's spirit\n- Many assorted books on magic, and knowledge from other mages.", ""),
        StaticBook("Magic dictionary", "Archrim Elmoran", "Magic dictionary:\n\nYir'sari- True wizard\nMasters of magic\nAcended- Demi god\nchildren of the aenguls\nundead- the evil\nfist of Iblees, they destroyed aegis\nArcane tranportation pad-\nstaff- a tool\nused to conduct mana and help with your connection to the void\nWand- akin to the staff but smaller and doesnt work as well\nCleric- mages who devote themselves to the learning and use of healing magic and are fueled by aenguls\nNecromancer- a user of necromancy which is a form of magic devoted to reanimation and the thievery of life\npyromancer- a mage who uses magic that has to do with fire and or smoke.\nelectromancer- a mage who is devoted to the use of electrical magic\nEvocation- the summoning of elements from the void\nfire evocation- the summoning of fire from the void\nwater evocation- the summoning of water and water realated things from the void\nElectric Evocation- the summoning of electricity from the void\nwind evocation- the summoning of wind or air fomr the void\nEarth evocation- the use of mana to summon earthen elements\nShamanism- A form of magic that requires the use of spirits\nmagic(k)- the use of mana and a connection to the void to achive great feats that can not normaly be done\nmana- the life force and energies around us and is used to form spells of any kind\nShade magic- A form a magic that is devoted to the use of shadows and or darkness\nIllusion magic- a branch of magic that is able to produce false images and feelings and project them on others\nconjuration- the summoning of living creatures\nWizard- common name for Yir'sari\nArchmage- ruler of the mages guild\nmage- one who learns and uses magic\nmagician- another name for mages and or Wizards\nAcended magic-  magic fueled by a patron aengul and is considerd holy, and akin to clerics\nundead magic- the reanimation and use of corpses, in short it is necromancy\nThe void- a earthreal place that mages call upon to help fuel their spells\nDragon- a very large and very inteligent creature who mainly roam the north, while hunting\nyello stone (endstone)- a strange stone that seems to spread corruption of different kinds\nDruidism- a form of magic that uses nature, and spirits know as the aspects\nakin to shamanism\nAlteration- a form of magic that is used to alter real world things", ""),
        StaticBook("Magic Notes I", "Vaeria Ossaura", "Sal' told me there are many types of 'Voidal' Magics, those being- Alteration, Arcanism, Evocation, Illusion, Mental and Conjuration.\n\nShe knows Arcanism (both disciplines) and has mastered it. This must mean she knows Arcane Shielding and Arcane-\n'Blasting'. She also knows Eletrical Evocation and Mental Magic- she's obviously very well versed with magic to know all about this kind of stuff. I'll ask her to teach me how to 'connect' to the Void, if I really suck at it.\nThe correct term isn't Arcane 'Blasting'- it's Arcane Evocation. She evokes raw Arcane energy, and uses it against people (in the form of missiles and such).\n\nMaybe magic is why her face is faint scared. I feel faintly bad for her.\nBasic concept of Elemental Evocation- The user draws energy from the Void, 'forming' the voidal energies into an element. They then use that element in certain ways.\n\nAparantly this is a small summary of Elemental Evocation.\nBasic concept of Arcane Evocation- The user draws energy from the void, but they do not form the energy into an element. Instead, they keep it in the raw form, evoking it in certain ways.\nArcane Evocation is much harder then Elemental Evocation (due to the raw power it possesses) but is seemingly not much more powerful. \n\nThis is confusing to me- shouldn't the raw form be more powerful then the 'crafted' form?\nThe reasoning behind this- in Sal's own words- is that; 'it's not as destructive as fire or lighting, nor as sturdy as earth, nor as quick as air'. It's 'flexible'.\n\nArcane Evocation is considered the 'jack-of-all-trades'.\nIt's okay at everything, master-\nat none. Arcane Evocation is Elemental Evocation but without the elemental properties- it adapts to the whims of the caster. In my opinion, this seems so much better. \n\nAnd like always- I am wrong. Elemental Evocation isn't as wild as I thought.\nIt seems dependable, much more then I first thought. Fire and Eletrical Evocation can actually start fires!", ""),
        StaticBook("Magic Notes II", "Vaeria Ossaura", "Well, atleast this time I didn't have a nosebleed. My body is feeling almost bruised and my head is racing from a migraine. I've failed- again. \n\nBut, failure means practise. And practise makes perfect.\nAdniel gave me this tome, and I'm going to learn from it. However, having a tome doesn't make you a practitioner of magic.\n\nConnecting is alot more... locked away, then I thought it would be.\nWhy is it so difficult to enter (or tap into) a realm that feels so close? The Void is locked within the fabric of our own realm- so why can't I just reach out and grasp it?\n\nI hope Sal' will help me learn. Without proper help, I'll be doomed to failure.\nMaybe, just maybe, if I keep torturing myself with this damn connecting, I'll succeed. Probably not, but I can try.\n\nMight not be magic related, but the fire is sort of helping with my focus. Hearing the faint crackling of the timber is relaxing.\nReally relaxing. And the softness of the mat doesn't hurt my knees, so- good thinking on my part, right?\n\n\nMaybe focusing on other things around me is the key to connecting? I'll ask Sal' and Adniel\nabout their way of connecting, and try to really get a grasp for it. I'll write it down here. Sal's Recommendations- Use incense and try your best to block it out. Ignoring the odor aparantly helped her first connection- so I'll have to try it.\nShe also said that connecting feels like 'nothing'. That's insane! Besides the faintness, how will I even handle it? Nothing is intense, when you think about it...\n\nShe also said to conquer the 'unknown' (the unknown being the fear on connecting).", ""),
        StaticBook("Malin", "Signature Trading Card", "One of the four brothers from the start of time, Malin was the father of the Elves. While blessed with a long life, his descendents suffer the curse of limited fertility.", ""),
        StaticBook("Mariana Illianos", "Kale le Prevost", "Caliphate Clinic\n\nHead Doctor Kale\n\nPatient's Name: Mariana Ilianos.\n\nAge: 29\n\nRace: Human\n\nGender: Female\nCondition/Injury: Broken bone in the upper arm. Made terrible cracking noises when moving.\n\nTreatment Plan: Arm was put in a splint and sling until further surgery is performed. \nDoctor who treated the patient: Doctor Daniel Ward\nCost: 25\n\nDate: 15th of Snow's Maiden, 1529\n*Doctor Daniel Ward's Signature appears here*\n\n\n~ Doctor Daniel Ward", ""),
        StaticBook("Mark Birch", "Kale le Prevost", "Caliphate Clinic\n\nHead Doctor Kale\n\nPatient's Name: Mark Birch\n\nAge: 14\n\nRace: Human\n\nGender: Male\nCondition/Injury: A gash remains on his side, leftovers from a sword. Had a considerable amount of blood- Not a terribly large amount, but enough to stain his shirt.\nTreatment plan: Medicine was given to stop the heavy blood flow, then the wound was stitched up with a curved needle. Will recover in one of the patient rooms for a few days.\nDoctor who treated the patient: Kiveck and Katari\n\nCost: 60 minas\n\nDate: 15th - 17th of The Grand Harvest, 1522\nEnd result: Patient left fully recovered. No real issues, the stab wound will take about a month to heal, but has improved quite drastically.\n*Both Katari and Kiveck's signatures would be here*\n\n- Kiveck Irelia", ""),
        StaticBook("Marked Magic", "Adeon of Rhoswen", "\"Though they are not wizards that employ  powerful curses, most Witchmen can cast simple spells that can prove effective when used properly. Witchmen usually use them to fight monsters, though they also have non combat applications.\"\n-An Observer\nMarked Men are encouraged to use weaponry light enough to allow them to more easily cast simple spells with their free hand yet heavy enough so that they may deliver considerable damage to the thick hides of monsters.\nIt is suggested that a Marked Man refrain from using magic if any of these three illnesses affect him:\n\n-Injury\n-Fatigue\n-Loss of lucidity.\nIf a Marked Man is injured it is unlikely he will be able to evoke anything more than a few harmless sparks, this entirely depends on the magical skill of the Marked Man, however, most Marked Men sacrifice some of their magical ability for skill with a\nblade. Using magic while injured is risky as it may cause further injury or worsen bleeding due to increased blood flow when casting.\nLoss of lucidity will impact a Marked Man's judgement, intelligence and concentration- the nature of a Marked Man's job will always contain potential threats to his lucidity such as illusions or traumatising events.\nIt is suggested that if a Marked Man senses he is no longer fully lucid that he immediately ceases the casting of any spells due to the risk of causing harm to himself or another Marked Man.\nMagic causes the caster to become fatigued, no matter how powerful the caster is, eventually fatigue will catch up with them.\n\nA Marked Man should always maintain a high level of stamina while on the job as being fatigued can cause\nboth other illnesses.\n\nFatigue is the leading cause of death amongst monster hunters.\nA Marked Man's spell is most effective when cast with an element of surprise and proximity followed by the strike of a blade. It is always encouraged that a Marked Man keeps close watch on their sense of fatigue and do not spend all of their mana in one\nspell unless absolutely necessary.", ""),
        StaticBook("Mathic Code", "Rosso", "From a note written by a friend in code, of many pages, she had no memory of writing it, or what it did mean.\nI decided to try and decipher it as if it were a secret note between armies, and I came with results. The code is copied first, then the meaning of the code is told, then the translation.\nCode\n{t, ƒ:ªº£ ñ.t:c .ø,}\n\n[t.£ ûe:ç :º e, ñ.ô, ñ.nü| ƒ'ª t, u':ô .nô t, ,ñö£:n,ºº d,i'û t, u':ô û,ª, e:º £' c'ññ.nô]\n[t,ü û,ª, ô\"º£ .nô e, û.º .º . º\"nd,.ñ| ô,ƒ:n:nø .£ i.º£ t,:ª ê.ö,]\n[.º û.º d,ƒ'ª,| t.£ ûe:ç :º e, ñ.ô, .ø.:n| û:t t, º'ªª'û 'ƒ º\"ni:øe£ ª,ñ.:n:nø \"n£ª,.£,ô .nô \"nû,ic'ñ, £' t, ô,,ö c\"ªª,n£º 'ƒ t, º,.]\n[º\"ç ô:ª,c£:'ni,ººn,ºº :º .n.t,ñ. £' e:ñ| ûe' :º i:n,.ª]\n[t,º, .ª, £' d, E:º £ª:\"ñöeº .nô E:º ƒ.:i:nøº| ñ.nü £:ñ,º 'u,ª]\n{t, º,c‘nô ñ.t:c .ø,}\n\n[t, ƒª.c£:‘nº ‘ƒ t.£ ûe:ç :º e, .ª, d.ªª,ô ƒª‘ñ ,n£ªü]\n[t, ‘t,ªº e.u, t,:ª ô,u:c,º ‘ƒ û.ª .nô ô:º“n:‘n| ô,ƒ:n:æ t,ñ .º ûe.£ t,ü .iû.üº e.u, d,,n| t, c.ª,£.*,ªº ‘ƒ t,:ª ƒ.£,]\n[t,ü ê‘“£ .nô öi,.ô .nô ‘ƒƒ,ª t,:ª u‘:c,º £‘ t, e,.u,nº| d“£ t,ü .ª, .º t, ô,,ö c.u,º d,øø:æ ƒ‘ª º“ni:øe£]\n[t, û.ü :º ê“£]\n{t, t:ªô ñ.t:c .ø,}\n\n[. ‘n, £,.ªº ƒª,, t, di:nôƒ‘iô ‘ƒ t, .düºº ƒª‘ñ t, ,ü,º ‘ƒ t, ö.ª£º]\n[:£ :º .º :£ û.º]\n[t, i:øe£ .nô i:øe£º .nô i:øe£:æ ‘ƒ t,:ª êª:n,º ƒª,, t,:ª º:øe£| t.£ t,ü ñ.ü di:nô ‘t,ªº]\n[û.ª .nô ô:º“n:‘n .ø.:n º“øø,º£º :£º,iƒ| .nô t, i:n, :º ñ.ô, .º .n “nc‘“n£.di, º,ª:,º ‘ƒ ö‘:n£º| .ii :n c‘nƒi:c£ û:t t,:ª ƒ,ii‘ûº]\n[.ii û:,iô:æ t,:ª i.û]\n{t, ƒ‘“ªt ñ.t:c .ø,}\n\n[t,:ª i.û :º t, ö‘û,ª ‘ƒ t, ñ.nü]\n[ºi‘ûiü t, i:øe£º .ª, ,æª.u,ô û:t t, ºüñd‘iº ‘ƒ t, ,nº,ñdi,º ‘ƒ t, ñ.nü]\n[t, º:øe£º ‘ƒ n,û ç.:nº .ª, ƒ.ñ:i:.ª]\n{t, ƒ:ƒt ñ.t:c .ø,}\n\n[t, ƒª.øñ,n£º .ª, ñ.ô, .º £‘ø,t,ª .º û.º d,ƒ‘ª,| t.£ ûe:ç :º e,]\n[t, ñ.nü ç‘‘º, t,:ª ö.t| £‘ t, ª,ö,£:£:‘n .º t, º“n ª:º,º .nô ƒ.iiº ‘ª £‘ t, dª,.*:æ ‘ƒ :£| t.£ t, º“n ê.ii º£.nô º£:ii]\n{t, º:xt ñ.t:c .ø,}\n\n[t, ñ.nü d,c‘ñ, .º ‘n, .º û.º t.£ ûe:ç :º e,]\n{t, º,u,nt ñ.t:c .ø,}\n\n[.º û.º d,ƒ‘ª,| t.£ ûe:ç :º ñ.nü ñ.ô, .ø.:n]\nMeaning\n. - a\n, - e\n: - i\n‘ - o\n“ - u\n\nt - th\nƒ - f\nª - r\nº - s\n£ - t\nñ - m\nc - c\nø - g\nû - w\ne - h\nç - ch\nô - d\nn - n\nü - y\nu - v\nö - p\nd - b\ni - l\nê - sh\n* - k\næ - ng\n\n| - ,\n\n[] - sentance\n{} - title\nTranslation\nThe first mathic age.\n\nThat which is He made many, for the void and the emptiness below the void were His to command.\nThey were dust and He was as a sunbeam, defining at last their shape.\nAs was before, that which is He made again, with the sorrow of sunlight remaining untreated and unwelcome to the deep currents of the sea.\nSuch directionless is anathema to Him, who is linear.\nThese are to be His triumphs and His failings, many times over.\nThe second Mathic age\n\nThe fractions of that which is he are barred from entry.\nThe others have their devices of war and disunion, defining them as what they always have been, the caretakers of their fate.\nThey shout and plead and offer their voices to the heavens, but they are as the deep caves begging for sunlight.\nThe way is shut.\nThe third Mathic age\n\nA one tears free the blindfold of the abyss from the eyes of the parts.\nIt is as it was.\nThe light and lights and lighting of their shrines free their sight, that they may blind others.\nWar and disunion again suggests itself, and the line is made as an uncountable series of points, all in conflict with their fellows.\nAll wielding their law.\nThe Fourth Mathic Age\n\nTheir law is the power of the many.\nSlowly the lights are engraved with the symbols of the ensembles of the many.\nThe sights of new chains are familiar.\nThe Fifth Mathic Age\n\nThe fragments are made as together as was before, that which is he\nThe many choose their path, to the repetition as the sun rises and falls or to the breaking of it, the sun shall stand still.\nThe Sixth Mathic Age\n\nThe many become as one as was that which is he.\nThe Seventh Mathic Age\n\nAs was before, that which is he many made again.\nMathic Code\n\nCoppied and Decoded by Rosso\nVaerHaven", ""),
        StaticBook("Maturity", "Azoth Hawksong", "Goodbye.\nIt's time-\nto be on your own.\nLost in new life,\nA long way from home.\n\nCherish your past of\njuvenile leisure-\nWhere the conflicts\nYou faced\nwere but scorn\nof a teacher.\nNow you're grown up\nand are shown\njust how far,\nYour prepareness\ncan take you-\na blessing or a scar.\n\nYour record\nwon't shape you,\nbut the risks\nthat you take:\nThe harships we face\nand the lives\nthat we change.\n\nBy the end\nof this journey\nyou'll be so more\nfree.\nJust a feeling,\nit is, but\nits purpose, you see:\n\nWas to simulate life\nand to usher you in\nTo our final\nlong venture,\nwhich ends\nwhen we end.\n\n- Azoth Hawksong", ""),
        StaticBook("Mead mead mead!", "Ser Romane Niwarsay II", "(A Drinking Song)\nMead, mead, mead!\nWonderfully wet, sinfully sweet!\nHeft your mead horns and hold your mugs high,\nWe want to keep drinking until we do die!\nMead, mead, mead!\nIt's what a body needs!\nIt makes us strong and wise and brave,\nThere's nothing better, it's what we crave!\nMead, mead, mead!\nI drink it morning, noon, and eve.\nI like it spiced and sweet and powerful,\nI'll soon be drunk and thats quite probable,\nMead, mead, mead!", ""),
        StaticBook("Meditations", "Dauth Sundavar", "A Compendium of\n            Questions\n\n\n\n\n         Written by\n      Dauth Sundavar\nAs soon as I stepped into the wilderness, all of time slipped from my grasp. The days and nights melded into one intaginable idea, defined only by the changes in the temperature and the prevalence of creatures within the respective landscape.\nI followed the direction of the winds, the brightest star in the cosmos, the natural pathways within the environment that didn't particularly lead me anywhere. \n\nIt was during my wanderings that I realised the enormity of nature. It must be amazing to be\na Druid; to be connected with every aspect of nature and have a profound understanding of the mechanics behind our very existence. \n\nBut we laymen are ignorant to the true nature of the world around us. We do not know the songs of the land, the stories\nthat are interwoven into the very fabric of the land; the collection of lives that have been, and have seeped into into the very land itself.\n\nI lost sight of myself during my journeys. I lost sight of who I know myself to be, but also who I present\nmyself to be. \n\nI was alone. I spoke to no one. The sound of silence seeped into my very being and began to unravel the knots that filled empty space within my mind and soul. I was being pulled in so many different directions, my mind catching on the\ncracks and crevices that filled my mind. \n\nI thought I was going mad; losing my mind just as any elder of any race does in due time. Multitudes of questions spiralled within the tangle that was my consciousness, drowning each other out and threatening to\nconsume me. \n\nAnd then there was silence. And darkness. A shroud of shadow unbeknown to any man or woman. \n\nAnd I slept. \n\nI don't know how long for.\nAfter I awoke, my mind was, although fractured in some places, whole. The voices had abated, and my heart no longer threatened to leap out of my chest.\n\nIt was nighttime when I awoke. Laying beneath a shroud of leaves, I peered through the\nfoliage into the clear night sky, adorned with millions of stars that flickered at their own accord. \n\nAt first, I studied them all; creating constellations of my own fashion as I basked within the tranquility of the night.\nI saw a stag bounding across the veil of pitch; a soldier garbed with a belt forged from the most brilliant of stars; a wolf with his teeth bared.\n\nI named him the Dread Wolf.\n\nAnd then I began to question whether the stars had their own\nthoughts just as the other aspects of nature did. Can the Druids hear their calls from the heavens, just as they do with the isles of oak that feature so frequently?\n\nOr do the stars exist in another plane of existence? Is the cosmos where the\nAenguls reside when they are absent from the world?\n\nI thought about death a lot. Is death just another plane of existence? \n\nThe Druids say that the best of their Order reside with the Aspects in their own dimension\nwhen their time has passed. What happens to those not of the Order?\n\nI see it this way. We die. Within due time, we turn to dust. The earth then claims our remains and sews us into the very fabric of the land. This is where the voices that the\nDruids hear come from.\n\nIt is nice to think that we serve a purpose after we die. \n\nUnless a Blight happens.\n\nI don't want that to happen.\nI sang to the earth sometimes. I didn't speak to it though. I just sang. I sang words that meant nothing but meant everything at the same time. There is no language for it; only emotion.\n\nI am a very emotional person. Sometimes I let my emotions cloud my\njudgement and I say things that I don't mean to say. But it wasn't me saying them; it was my emotions.\n\nBut at the same time, it was still me.\n\nI want to apologise to Marcy for what I said to her. But maybe she won't accept it, and I'll get angry again.\nThat would be bad. I would probably say hurtful things again.\n\nPerhaps I should stay silent. That way I can't hurt anyone with my words. I want to heal, to help; not hurt.\n\nThere is a song that resides within us all. I believe it defines our very natures.\nI want to find mine.\n\nMaybe if I learn my song, I won't be a slave to my emotions any more.\n\nWhich begs the question.\n\nWho am I?\n\nI need to find out.", ""),
        StaticBook("Mental Barriers", "Elindor", "Recognising the backgrounds of\nMental Barriers\n\n1: Birthright\n\n2: Learnt: Emotional\n\n3: Learnt: Intellect\n\n4: Self Aware\nWhether conscious of the fact or not, all who are of mental capacity for independant thought operate with mental barriers. An avoidance of certain foods, sentimentalities for or against an individual based on previous experience, a shudder at rain dropped\nupon the bridge of the nose. Whether they serve a purpose or not is beside the point, but all with functioning minds also have functioning mental barriers.\nGranted, some are more useful than others, but none should be ignored.\nConsideration 1:\nWhile minds are capable of being moulded, as is the case of a particular experiment wherein a disadvantaged goblin child is given the intellectual upbringing of a noble elven child (for scientific purposes only) and it is observed\nthat the matter of its circumstance of birth played little consequence in its mental abilities after the experiment's completion; however there is equal quantity of proof to suggest the opposite: that particulars of birth can suggest better or\nworse mental ability. So if all with basic mental function posess mental barriers at some level, then it can also be agreed upon that some individuals may have naturally more intentional barriers resulting from the particulars of their birth.\nConsideration 2:\nEmotional experiences are an inevitable by-product of experiencing the company of others. What is not certain is whether the usefulness of such can be realised.\nIt has been observed through various trials\nin which test subjects experienced the same situation that arised a similar emotional outcome in each subject. The results were conclusive that the female subjects were more capable of becoming emotionally guarded against a similar circumstance\nwhen the same tests were repeated (and with more difinitive results in the older subjects). The outcome of said experiments showed that aged women are more capable of producing emotional based mental barriers than are young men.\nOf course this does not mean that it is only the aged woman's game to create emotional barriers, but that their past experiences before commencing tests rendered them more prepared to react in an emotionally defensive manner.\nBut whether young or old, and\nposesssing either of the genitalia, emotionally driven mental barriers prove to be an easily formed barrier that many people demonstrate. It is the experience that heat means danger, or that a wanting look from the opposite sex means trouble. And\nsuch learning becomes the basis of these most simple barriers with the intent to better our future course from further danger.\nConsideration 3:\nLearning needs by neccessity of adapted barriers (the terminology \"adapted\" used to refer to any barriers that are reactive in nature) is not limited to that of emotional education, though it is certainly the easier and more likely to\noccur in individuals who have no conscious recognition of mental barriers than those of the next type: intellectual.\nMentally capable individuals, typically of a higher learning, are more aware of themselves and the commencement and termination of their\nown thoughts, such that they might be able to deduce that the outcome of something will be of ill consequence, and thus establish an intellectual barrier against it without requiring the emotional experience of it to draw such a conclusion.\nAnyone with such ability would be concidered of a more valuable intellectual worth than those who remain unconscious to their circumstance until they experience it first hand, because they demonstrate a conscious control over adapted barriers, knowing\nthe beginnings of danger before the danger is present. As is the results of tests among children in which one observes another experiencing pain and assumes that the outcome of the test implement used will result in pain, without having to experience it\nfor themselves.\nIndividuals with intellectual adapted barriers can become conscious of a problem before the problem manifests, and thus one who intends to expose them to mental danger will find their defense against it pre-emptive from the first.\nConsideration 4:\nThe last is difinitively the most complicated. Tests on such subjects still remain inconclusive as the data is of too broad an array to speculate commonalities; supposing that great caution be taken in anticipating this adaptive and\nindividualistic reaction to provocation.\nIf the proposed individual be of such mental self awareness as to acknowledge their own thoughts and recognise the thoughts of others within their own murmurings, then any mental intrusion will be detected from the\nfirst by these clever adaptive barriers that prevent further intrusion of outside stimuli.\nUnderstanding such mental barriers can only be done on an individual basis, for without foreknowledge of the mental, emotional and experiential past of\nthe individual, one has no way of interpreting or supposing their reaction. And no one route will have conclusive success in all cases.\nThe boldest conclusion:\nIf, in any of the previous cases and most cunning in the latter one, an individual were to acknowledge their own conscious existence with relation to a broader knowledge of the universe and/or deity(s), and at the\nsame time comprehends themselves in reference to The Void, then their mental resoluteness may account for intentional barriers of not just emotional and intellectual stimuli, but of all mental stimuli. Such an individual is capable of constructing a\ndeliberate blockade that utilises their own erperience(s), and acknowledging their experiential or anticipated reaction, mould it into a barrier that will require a specifically, and equally intentional, attempt to remove it.\nOf anticipating and overcoming any of the barriers that the source of which has been suggested in the previous pages, this book will do no work towards. For the efforts of removing a barrier is to discredit the worth of it, while the intent of this book\nhas been to acknowledge and more clearly define the circumstance from which mental barriers may have been constructed.", ""),
        StaticBook("Mental Sense", "Elindor", "Mental Sensitivity\n\nA short depiction of such things and how one may endeavour to experience it.\n\nFeaturing an example.\nOur senses are really only the beginning of perception. The simpleton perceives mostly with vision and uses little else of their other senses, and few actually recognise a higher perceptive nature within themselves. But be not fooled by the majority, for\nthe mind shall see far more than what comes before the eyes, if one can but forfeit their pre-conceived ideas that only the seen and experienced can exist, as well as pinpoint their place within an almighty expanse of universes in which knowledge is\nbut a fraction of the truth, and then dive into the opportunities for them whose mind is self actualised, and whose existence is both infinitely small, and omnipotently all.\nLet us embark upon this journey outside of the material things. Let us learn to apprecciate the placement of all things within the universe and begin perceiving without the senses.\nLet us discover ourselves amidst a world that exists just beyond our eyes.\nTo begin with. Ignore your senses. Block out the information that your eyes, ears and skin are attempting to bombard you with at all times. Discover within yourself that small safe place in which dwells just your thoughts, and your sense of self.\nSome people find this a confronting experience as the immediate outcome is an objective view of the being from which you spend all your time trapped within and, in being so, are unable to adequately examine. You may experience a sense of resentment\ninadequacy towards faults. This is not a bad thing, for it means that you have discovered your true nature without the inflated pride that usually skews this view. This is the first most important step towards understanding the world around from\na liberated consciousness. In trying to discover this place, do not allow yourself to slip into such blankness of thought that you discover The Void. Immunity from the sensory overload that is our waking existence is required for locating both\nones self and The Void that sits in wait behind our reality, yet that much emptiness of mind is not required. Thoughts are a good thing in this circumstance. Your thoughts are what will guide you to view yourself from without your eye sockets.\nNow, assuming that you have indeed come to realise yourself objectively; seen your existential being as it were, you may already havcommenced viewing our world from a new viewpoint. Let us take some time before continuing onwards to apprecciate all the\nwonders that surround us that we so eagerly allow to slip by under the weight of our own self importance. Let us aclimatise to this expanding of the mind before we carry on.\nHave we acclimatised?\n\nGood.\n\nNow. Again, block out all the sensory garbage. Cease seeing, feeling, smelling, hearing and tasting your surroundings and again return to the place of just you and your mind in the quiet.\nObviously you cannot also be reading this. So read this first, and then do it. Have you read the rest?\n\nOk, let us continue.\n\nFrom this place of mental freedom, now look beyond yourself. It may be of value to envision an\nappropriate illustration to better comprehend the surrounding reality. For the sake of clarity an example will be used to more accurately define what one is attempting to do.\nOnce you have discovered your place in the universe, allow the universe to\nreveal its place to you. This isn't supernatural druid bollocks but simply the easiest way to explain that you must oepn your mind enough to discover your surroundings for what they are and no longer what they look, feel and seem to be.\nThe Illustration Begins\nYou exist alone. There is nothing. But your eyes are closed, so how can you know that there is nothing? Breath. Allow your mind to drift, it knows what it is doing.\nNow open your mind's eye. See.\nWhat you beleived to be darkness is quite the opposite. For you exist surrounded by endless white. There is no distinctive end. You cannot perceive a difference between land or sky. You know yourself to be sitting, yet there is no surface below.\nNow examine more closely. What is it that takes shape? There is indeed a surface but it is not solid. You can feel it beneath you, perhaps even see it as your eyes focus. There are stones. Smooth stones that fit nicely in the palm. You might pick one up\nso that you can better examine. It seems too perfect to be real. You glance around and see that they are all too perfect. And with each returned glance there are more. Thousands. Countless. The endless white is as an ocean of small white stones.\nYou should look more closely now at the one in your hand, for a detail you missed before now seems more obvious. It is not perfectly smooth afterall, there is one small part that is slightly flatter. You gather another stone, there is an imperfection to\nits colouring. And another, with a small indentation like a dimple. Even as you look about at the stones far in the distance you become aware that they are not all alike. They are each their own, each in its right place, all together creating an expanse\nof white that stretches out beyond the recognition of sight. Further, even, beyond recognition of the mind. You hold tightly the first stone, with its flat side against the skin of your hand. An unexpected feeling as the awakening from a deep slumber\nbeckons you outward. You know not how to give in to it, so you clench your eyes and grip onto the small stone.\n\nBack in the room where you were meditating. All is still. But you feel an odd attraction to a candle upon the desk (or\nsome other inanimate object as is appropriate to your situation). Perhaps you reach out to touch it, though it does already feel like you have it within your grasp, and as your hand rises the thing appears to wobble. For a single instant. So easy to miss.\nConcluding Remarks Such is the beginning. Humble, as most are. \n\nThere is more in store, but self discovery is the only way towards progress from here. Understanding where and how one object exists can bloom into a working appreciation\nfor the placement of all things around. And an ability to realise when something is not in the way that it should. When a stone is overturned, misplaced, or unsettled by something new.\nThere are other sensitivites that are not just mental based. Sensing auras and magical disturbances have proven highly useful, as was the case with an old mage who had lost use of his sight and was able to perceive as though with eyes from sensing\nthe proximity of things emitting aura. \nNoticing magical disturbances is a bit more common, particularly for Void Mages in sensing when something has majorly shifted in the balance of Void energy.\nThe druids too have some connection to the right placement\nof nature from their communion with the aspects, which, in all relativity, is highly likely to operate similarly to the mental sensitivity suggested in the former pages.\nI will write no more upon the uses of such a thing, as any who struggle to see the usefulness therein are blinded by a love of their sight.\nIn fact, I will write no more at this time upon this subject at all.\n\n    ~ - | END | - ~", ""),
        StaticBook("Music Vol. 1", "Evelynn Shards", "-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-\n \nThe  Sound  Of  Music\n\n-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-\n \nSix songs each book.\nSelf made songs and there will be more soon!\n-------------------Written by Evelynn Shards\n-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-Page 3: The Sun Parade\nPage 4: Into Nature's Beauty\nPage 5: Lights Will Guide Us\nPage 6: All Of Us \nPage 7: Praise Our Emperor\nPage 8: Fire\n-------------------\n-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-The Sun Parade\n\nIt shall start soon. Our king will praise our sister, the sun...\nAnd when the light shall raise, the sword will light up. Few people to hold back. Only one Elven day shall this happen. I-its the Sun.\n-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-Into Nature's Beauty\n\nIf you would walk into the wild and open forest. No human to see. Wide open space full of trees, animals everywhere to be seen. A small rabbit would run in the grass. An owl to fly over. Just too much..\n-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=- Lights Will Guide Us\n\nWhen night falls over the town. Lights will raise from upon the sky. Thou shall form one long line. Just for us to follow. They shall guide us home, and to a better place. Keep on searching to there...\n-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-All Of Us\n\nImagine if our God. Would be down here with us. Nobody to know anything. He built cities all over the world. Nobody to notice anything. Though everybody was happy. All of us were...\n-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-Praise The Emperor\n\nOh, misty forest, filled with ghosts and monsters. The plains ruled by our Emperor. May our Emperor be praised. Oh, praise the Emperor (x2) He makes us happy, gives us good advice. Who doesnt honour him?\n-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-            Fire\n\nI see fire. In the trees going over to the houses. Licking the air like a cat. Burning down towns and forests. The fire needs to be destroyed. It needs to stay small, and keep us warm..", ""),
        StaticBook("Mystery of Flame", "Annette", "The Mystery\n          of Flame\n\n             (\n            ( ,)\n          ).  ( )\n         (, )'   (.\n       \\WWWWWWWW/\n          '--..--'\n\n             by\n      Annette Elendil\nIn nature, there are very few beings that are capable of handling fire. One might even go as far to say that civilization had begun with flame, and that the histories of various ethnic groups is based on the development and history of the flame.\nWe use flame for cooking our foods, and it is the same flame that saves us from cold and darkness. And most importantly, it is because of the flame that we are able to be active at night which allows us to get a lot more done within a given day...\nIn our everyday lives, we share our food with others through camp sharing and it is in such settings that we learn to share ourselves spiritually with other people.\nSuch activities are the very essence of what fuels the energy of our lives and is the process in which we come to accept that we are each a part of this world..\n\nRather beautiful, that.\n\nBut sad, too..\nOn the other hand, flame has always been seen to have mystical and powerful forces. That is probably because flames are at the very source of creation, while at the same time, at the crux of utter destruction.\nWe are fortunate enough to be around flames quite frequently through campfires and such. It is one of the most seemingly basic, yet mythological practices as well.\nEven we ourselves are compared to flame. I am often told that the flame is with us,\neven in the moment of our death.\nWhen a person dies, a flame goes out.\nAnd a body that's lost its flame.\nLoses its light\nas well as warmth\nand in the end, remain as nothing more than dirt in the earth...\n\nThe flame is the heart,\nbecause it is the heart that is at the center of our lifeline,\nand the moment our heart goes out,\nthe flame goes out with it...", ""),
        StaticBook("Nature's Rule", "[Teen] Dedicant Yaehahn Signus", "The promise of life\nbring a few to this dense, mysterious forest.\nFor those that are unaware, Nature is the ruler of these lands.\nGentle, it will share its wisdom with the few that choose to listen.\nForgiving, it will bring back life to the dead\nand forgotten as the seasons cycle in their eternal dance.\nProtected, it will guard its inhabitants from those that only seek chaos and destruction. A fair warning, however, to those that wish to wander into Nature's welcoming arms,\ngive back the same\nwhich is given.\nBe gentle to the budding flower as it shyly awakens from its slumber.\nBe forgiving to the hungry birds that may take what they need from your hand.\nBe protective, for Nature can be vulnerable when its least expects it.\nChoose to listen,\nhear the rustling whispers of the trees as they laugh in the wind. \nAnd the silent deer moving into the distance, \nin search of a place to rest its head for another night.\nBare trees seem to rise towards the\nclouds,\nbathing in misted morning light.\nAnimals stalk silently in their territory, wary of both friend and foe.", ""),
        StaticBook("Neo-Druidism", "Tristanm1", "Neo-Druidism\nBy Salmon Druid Petyr\nWith some modern druidic practices wandering astray, it has been necessary for me to scribe another text - that of Neo-Druidism. Throughout my travels and my meditative studies, I have had revelations unknown to others. It is important to understand that\nthere is a special type of balance that druids themselves remember to study - action and non-action. Let me clarify. Action is motion, the motion of restoring balance, and of being the hand of Nature. Non-action is meditation and silence; invisible\nmotion. You see, this is the only Way to truly understand Nature, the Universe, the \"It\". For, through non-action you may feel \"It\", and through action you may be \"It\".  Call this \"It\" what you want; most druids will call it Nature. What is important is\nto understand what Nature truly is, and how it relates to you. To reveal this is the goal of this tome.\nSection 1 - The Unity of Nature\nThe first fault in thinking is to make distinctions - those between you and Nature, between life and death, between good and evil.  Everything is Nature, despite what any given piece believes. A druid does not use nature\ndruid is merely a piece who realizes the profound truth. Through non-action, one may truly understand this concept and feel it course through them. However, while meditating in non-action, one also separates themselves from the flow; the Way; the path\nthat Nature chooses for its pieces. To rejoin with Nature, one must practice action.\nSection 2 - The Way of Nature\nNature does what it will, and while we realize this while meditating, sometimes we forget in action. We are merely moving along the river of life, the tides of balance. While we believe that we are restoring the balance, i\nis balance restoring itself in an everlasting self-conflict. Thus, through action, we are the hands of Nature, of balance, of the Way. One must not think of \"using\" Nature while playing their part, but of \"being\" Nature. This is a common mistake as well\namong young druids and non-druids. They think to restore the world by planting lots of trees... but that is not balance, nor is it even the way to achieve balance. Action alone is not the Way, for one forgets the purpose of action. Only with the mix of\naction and non-action can one achieve the goal: to have your cake and eat it, too.\nSection 3 - The Druid\nSo as we can now see, the Druid is simply the most important hand, the piece Nature uses to govern itself. To truly try to understand is a task that lies within meditation and non-action. However, there is a limit to the mental re\nof the pieces, for as no one truly knows all of themselves, Nature does not know all of itself. This is a fairly important observation: that even though Nature is unified, the unification is marked by threadlines of dubiousness and doubt. The Neo-Druid\nshould not fret, however. There are uneven surfaces in every river. No tree has identical leaves. Just so, nothing, no \"It\", can be the symmetrical perfection one would like to see. Randomness and uniqueness are inherent in Nature, as defining as the\nunity itself. Paradoxical though it may seem, through balance of action and non-action, it shall be revealed to you, and only then will you understand the phrase...\n\n\n\"All is One\"", ""),
        StaticBook("Nether Star", "Cloven", "Nether Star\n    Research notes\n~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~      Written by Elvi\n        Terryal\n\n *A detailed drawing of the object is on this             page*\nWhat is the object?\n~~~~~~~~~~~~~~\nThe object appears to be a type of gem, rather purplish and resembling much like a heart. According to a few people, this gem is found within a foul creature wielding three heads, most likely one of Iblee's servants or\npart of his undead army. Could this possibly be the heart of this creature?\nFrom observance, the gem appears to be fairly normal, aside from resembling a little bit of a heart. However, I do feel a strange yet eerie aura radicating from it. Could there\nbe something within the gem or is it something used to store the creature's energy in? This energy, or perhaps the gem itself, could be used within Alchemy, magic and perhaps further. Hence why I am studying this object.\nResearch 1#\n~~~~~~~~~\nFrom what I know, this gem is from a creature with three heads, mostly known as a 'Wither', a dark creature that is said to be in league with Iblees. This gem comes from the Wither whenever someone would defeat it.\nHowever, it is unsure on what this item is used for. Could it be used as an Alchemy symbol (then what would it represent? What element would it be?)? Would this be used as part of magic, either light or dark magic? Or does it dive much deeper into\nsomething that perhaps we do not understand. That is something I am hoping to find out during my time researching it.\nFor the time been, I shall refer to the gem as the 'NetherStar' as it would be likely that the Wither is from the Nether and the gem does\nvagely resembles a star from some angles.\n\n\n-Written by Elvi Terryal\n\nDate: 12th of Sun's smile 1484\nTheory number one\n~~~~~~~~~~~~~~\nThe energy theory\n~~~~~~~~~~~~~~\nOne of the few theories I have came up with about this gem is about a potental energy hidden within.\nNow, think about the Wither. This gem can supposedly be its heart, what keeps it\nalive. That energy within can be a type of life source, something that keeps the wither alive. If we can somehow extract this energy from this gem, then we may be able to use this energy for either good or bad intentions, depending on who wants to use it\nand why. I believe a way of extracting this supposed energy can be through the use of magic, or someone who can extract energy from things and possibly can transfer the energy from the gem into something else.\nHowever, judging that the energy is from a\nwither, I cannot guarentee that this energy can be used for good purposes as it is from a very dark creature.\nI am planning on asking a mage of some sorts about this theory. However, I just need to know where one is and ask. Hopefully they will  explain\nabout the process and whenever or not it can be possible. I suppose only time will know.\n\n- Written by Elvi Terryal\n\nDate: 11th of Grand Harvest 1485", ""),
        StaticBook("Nethers wrath", "Vlad Shade", "Nethers wrath\nMandragora\nserpents\ndwarf pumpkin\ndrake tail\nflame tongue\ncrimson vase\nMolten cure\nMandragora\nserpents stalk\ndwarf pumpkin\ndrakes tail\ncrimson vase", ""),
        StaticBook("No Dreams", "Zyvia Haeviel", "[!] You pick up 2 pieces of leather held together by uneven, crudely made string. It's a book! Dirty and rugged, but still a book. The only writing in it is a song, written in neat, slanted cursive.\nSometimes I look at them and I look into their eyes,\nI notice the way they think about the dead with a smile,\nCurved lips they just can't disguise.\nBut they think it's the living making their life worthwhile.\nWhy is it so hard for them to decide which they love more?\nThe Living or... the dead?\nThey like to use words like 'chance' and 'regret'. They like to use words about the living.\nBut when they stop their talking,\nTheir mind turns straight to the dead.\nThey hate the lonely and the weak. But they just think back to the dead,\nAnd they're happy once again.", ""),
        StaticBook("Northern Documen", "Toxzero", "http://i.imgur.com/CghA6Oc.png\n\n((The link is the Document))", ""),
        StaticBook("Notes on Blayism", "Yhorm Aldyhr", ". . .after a successful combination of his three artifacts, we have managed to successfully construct Illius’ guardian. . . oh won’t she be proud of us? A home for the weary once more, that they might find a light to illuminate their dreary souls? Oswald\nmay see us as worms to crush beneath his icey footsteps, yet now we may escape his once inevitable carnage. . . a test, or two, and his strength seems to be expanding with every swing of his fist. Oh how proud you must be, I can already envision\nthe smiles across their hollowed faces, can’t you?", ""),
        StaticBook("Novice Booklet", "Beltran", "[!]A novice script, the leather bound journal seems to belong to a novice monk.\n\"I have fled my teachers, the plague has been wrought upon them but an even dangerous creature lurks in the crowd of those undead! It slithers, the scales it clashes against the cobblestones. I fear I am alone in my endevaour. My peers have gone blind to-\nmy cries. My stomach grumbles but not for food but for escape, for food is what the eye wants from me.\"\n\n\n\n\n1570 -Frair Towl\n[!]Scratch marks are present on the page. Several pages are ripped apart. Blood is splattered upon the sheets in a careful manner, resembling an eye.\nThe eye The eye The eye The eye The eye The eye The eye The eye The eye The eye The eye The eye The eye The eye The eye The eye The eye The eye The eye The eye The eye The eye The eye The eye The eye The eye The eye The eye The eye The eye The eye\nThe eye The eye The eye The eye The eye The eye The eye The eye The eye The eye The eye The eye The eye The eye The eye The eye The eye The eye The eye The eye The eye The eye The eye The eye The eye The eye The eye The eye The eye The eye The eye\nThe eye The eye The eye The eye The eye The eye The eye The eye The eye The eye The eye The eye The eye The eye The eye The eye The eye The eye The eye The eye The eye The eye The eye The eye The eye The eye The eye The eye The eye The eye The eye\nThe eye The eye The eye The eye The eye The eye The eye The eye The eye The eye The eye The eye The eye The eye The eye The eye The eye The eye The eye The eye The eye The eye The eye The eye The eye The eye The eye The eye The eye The eye The eye\nThe eye The eye The eye The eye The eye The eye The eye The eye The eye The eye The eye The eye The eye The eye The eye The eye The eye The eye The eye The eye The eye The eye The eye The eye The eye The eye The eye The eye The eye The eye The eye\n[!]The rest of the pages are crusted, as if they have been soaked in water.", ""),
        StaticBook("Observations", "[Dedicant] Thickfeet", "Observations\n            on\n        Enchantments\n\n      \n\n\n\n\nWritten by\nLethius Thickfeet\nAs my learnings during my druid dedicancy continue, it has come to my attention the unnatural tendencies of the magical realm.\n\nIt its known that the source of most arcane magics is the great endless Void.\nIt is also believed by some the damaged cause by such usage is severe and slowly affects the greater realms that we live in.\n\nI was tasked to witness this in person. \n\nI spent a few days looking to the locals of Kal'Archon for\na regular magic user. Unforunately I found noone in the safety of the newly built city.\n\nOf course within the Druid Grove itself that kind of search would be futile.\nSurprisingly my search led me right under my nose.\n\nI was sifting through old belongings and came across a small journal I had purchased decades before.\n\nAmong my collection stood a very unique\nitem. A mage's journal had described the usage and discoveries she had made involving Enchantments.\n\nI glanced through the pages where a short collection of glyphs and its attributes were described in detail.\nIt was clear to me that such magical imbuement must have come from the same source as most Arcane magics.\n\nWhat I found was not wrong, and what found me was unnatural in every way.\nI began by searching the city markets for the magical reagents needed to perform enchantments.\n\nAfter collecting a few cheap tools and swords, the natural emerald stone which takes most precidence in the profession, and a mixture of other\npowders and artifacts required, I set out into the mountains to find a secluded and safe place to examine and study.\n\nThe first few days of attempts yielded nothing of value. \n\nMost attempts ended with absolutely no\nchange to the item in question. Some attempts the enchantments didn't hold but I had started to gain progress.\n\nFor most of those days, deep meditation and rebalance of the area is how I kept my focus. For every single attempt I\nmeditated to find signs to stop my continued attempts. It seemed to me that nature itself approved of me finding out the dangerous truth of the Arcane.\n\nI also repaid my attempts with various planting of flowers and herbs. Thankfully my work in gathering\nleft me with the stores to do so.\n\nEventually my labor bore fruit. I managed to enchant an old sword with a magical sharpness. It was almost as if the enchantment itself took precidence to the edge of the old blade. Ahead of the blade\nwas a magical barrier that cut through most things much easier than the obvious dull blade would have ever done alone. \n\nI rested that night after planting a healthy sapling nearby.\nThat night was some of the worst sleep I could have imagined. I dreamed of a darkness swallowing all around me. I couldn't outrun it, and eventually it over came me as I ran for my life.\n\nI awoke in the middle of the night\nknowing that something dark was coming due to the actions I had taken in those mountains. \n\nThe sounds of the area were too silent. The life of the area had fled and I could feel an unnatural emittion from the sword.\nI picked up the sword with both hands and felt the great weight atop the used hilt.\n\nI turned to see that a group of undead abominations had found me.  Running up the hill I felt the sweat and pain in my legs as I gained hieight but not distance.\nTaking a few wild swings I was able to stop a few of them as I ran. \n\nThe feeling of futility overcame me as the more I used the sword, the more monsters I had seemed to see. \n\nWhat had I unleashed? This was obviously\nno intent behind the enchantment, but I was starting to believe the author of that journal might have known exactly what they were doing when writing down a 'Sharpness' enchantment.\n\nI had been tricked and I was paying the price.\nI began slashing more wildly at everything in sight.  If I could wear out the blade and break it, I knew the enchantment would cease.  \n\nWhile this included some of the monsters around, I also slashed at the stoney earth.\nThe clangs of metal and stone probably agitated more of these monsters and the pain seared in my arms with every swing.\n\nI thought of death and how I had truly deserved it by naively jumping into a magic I didn't understand.\nA piercing wail filled my ears and a satisfying relaxation overcame me as the weight was lifted from my hands.\n\nThe enchantment had ended and the old sword was scrap at my feet.\n\nAs I stood watching the creatures come\nfor me, I decided to earn my fate. \n\nMy pulse filled my hearing, my body shook with fright, and I realized my folly.\n\nI closed my eyes and asked the aspects for forgiveness as the footsteps came closer and closer...\nA sudden silence filled my ears as powerful as a dragon's roar.\n\nIt felt like an age passed before I gained the courage to open my eyes. \n\nI sat on my knees completely astonished. As swiftly as the creatures appeared,\nthey had suddenly dispersed into the darkness.\n\nI spent a few days longer within the trees just thinking of the events. I hid the remainder of the items I purchased around the area hoping they would be lost with time.\nOnce I returned to the Grove I immediately penned what you have just read.  I hope that my story can help in those that come after me. \n\nDo not delve into what we know least about without guidance and carefulness.\nThe void contains a power that we are simply unable to wield elegently and it is not to be toyed with.\n\nI pray that my actions do not reflect poorly on the order, nor did I do too much damage to nature itself.\nDedicant\nLethius Thickfeet\n\n13th of the Grand Harvest.\n\n1457", ""),
        StaticBook("Oh My Friend", "Saeed Ibn Malek", "____Oh My Friend____\n-------------------A poem by Saeed Ibn Malek\n-------------------Like A Frolicsome Twilight, \n you Hang around the hallways of depature...\n Like a wounded sparrowhawk,\n You travel with no peace...\nAssassinate all forum of connection..\n And thunder with reminiscence. Oh My Friend...\n Every time i called you,\n The cry weeped for hearing me crying...\nAnd every time i looked at you,\n The memories of destroyed my entire entity...\n And the nostalgia made a shortcut straight to my heart...\n And the trailer of memory Returned to tear the cuts of my aches..\n Tell Me My Friend,\nwhat if tears streamed down on my cheeks\n Who would clear those tears out?\n Tell me, What if the phantom of breakdown chased me\n behind the hallways of loss?\n Where shall i find shelter and where the end will be?\nTell me,What if the birds left my sky...\n heading to the skyline of ruins...\n who would bring back the singing of bulbuls...\n How can i draw a way of eternal bestowal...\n without a hand that supports my hand...\nif it ever stumbled with demise?\ntell me who would draw me on the details of his features;\n For me to see the truth after the vanishing of mirage?\n Who whould bring me back from prudence after being foolish\n For My Sake,\n Listen to my speech,My friend, As it comes to one tone\na symphoney of remembrance that I made you own...\n And dont ask me if i miss you and miss being with you instead,\n Look between the lines of your hands,\n you'll find me as a prisoner, Fighting death back and forth\n Breathe Deeply!\nYou'll find me a very little atom fighting \n its paralles to find its way to you\n Close your eyes\n My Phatom won't vanish from your fantasy\n And smile to the drop of rain, when it streams down your forehead & cheeks...\nGreet it and wipe it away softly...\n Just the way you used to pat my shoulder\n When i run away from the world's nonsense to you\n Touch the air,you'd definitely feel my presence..\nAs i's part of your entity\n I'm some of you..\n I'm entirely you, My Friend\n I'm you, Oh you the page of the whiteness\n Gather my dispersion, and sprinkle it on the layers of substantial pain..If you decided to leave.\nLight up the night lamps in the daylight hours \n as the day wont look like a normal day no longer...\n Cover me with the garment of remembrance,\n as the cold will then breach the breath of warmth..\nAnd break up the ribs of nostalgia..\n And drink from the antidote of the past,\n with the heat of my breaths and feelings...\n Leave if you want to leave...but..let your departure be to the lands of reunion \n not to the lands of neglect and loss...\nbe a home to me\n as im an orphan with no home..\n Homeless..Driszzle with pain\n Im a wound..  With human features so,\n Be to me..\n the way of being home feels like...\n-------------------By: Saeed Ibn Malek\n   (ibraheemc2000)", ""),
        StaticBook("Old AdunianVolII", "SortedJarhead", "Ancient Adunian \n           Vol II    \n           \n       Written  by\n         Monk Argyll\n\n-- -- -- -- -- -- --\n           OOC:\n\nThis is part two of a n IC knowledge book. This is only useful if you've read the first one IC.\nAll rules from the first book apply. After your character studies this for 1-2 IC years, you will know all Ancient Adunian. (2-4 weeks IRL MINIMUM)", ""),
        StaticBook("Old Elven Part 1", "Anthron", "Table of contents\n------------------\nAffixes: 1-13\n-------------------Seed words: 14-part 2\n-------------------\nContent provided by\nSporadic:\nhttps://www.lordofthecraft.net/topic/19254-recovering-the-ancient-tongue-of-the-elves/\nAFFIXES\nAffixes  served a very important purpose in the Elven language. You are recommended to learn of them and their purpose before anything else,  for many words are formed by the addition of affixes to the so- called 'seed words', which are listed\nbelow.\nWe further discuss our findings on affixes in the grammatical section of this volume. Furthermore, words deserving extra explanation will be handled in the 'curious words' section.\nNote: The dash indicates the location of the seed word to the affix\nac- (aca-) Honorific for an accursed.\n-------------------ah- (ahe-): Honorific for reverence. Often used in front of names to denote sainthood.\n-------------------ael-: Vile, unnatural, beastly.\n--------------------al: -Designates the completion\nof an action.\n------------------\n-an: many, full of -Mostly designates plurality.\n------------------\nan*h- (a/o/i/e/u): To direct (up/down/left/right/rotate) (more on this in 'curious words').\n-------------------\nav-: wandering.\n-------------------ay: for.\n-------------------\n-ante (-nte): Last.\n-------------------div-: Empty, devoid of (antecendent to -an).\n--------------------ento (-nto): next.\n-------------------\n-ehya: and - used at the beginning of a sentence for 'but...'\n-------------------\nel- (ele-): - Changes a noun into a pronoun, making it a title of someone, or possibly something.\n-------------------\nfi-: new, anew - Often used as prefix, but not exclusively.\n-------------------heya- (hey-): what, how, who.\n-------------------\nil- (ilu-): with\n--------------------ii: little Designated the diminutive form. Plural: iian.\n-------------------\n-ir: - Designates the purpose of someone.\n-------------------iyl-: this usually a prefix, though not exclusively.\n-------------------iyul-: that usually a prefix, though not exclusively.\n--------------------leh: -Designates affiliation.\n------------------\nma- (mar-): very -Intensifier. More on this in 'curious words'\n--------------------n: - Sometimes put behind an adjective or verb to create a noun.\n--------------------ne (-ane): not - A suffix used to indicate the negative.\n-------------------\n-onn: from, born, kin - Often a suffix, but not always. More on this in 'curious words'.\n-------------------par- (pra-): before, pre-\n-------------------res- (re-): wayward, capricious, unpredictable.\n-------------------\n-sae: Spreading, development management. - More on this in 'curious words'\n-------------------tal- (tali-): Part (of) - More on this in 'curious words'\n-------------------u-: or - Always a prefix and always used with apostrophe.\n-------------------\n-yem (-hyem): again -Can be used to make a 're-' family of verbs\n-------------------vul- (vule-): clever, intuitive, deceptive.\n-------------------\nSEED WORDS\nThese words make up the brunt of the Elven language, and may be modified by various affixes to create new words.\nacal: valuable, golden, cursed - From ac-al: Completion of the word\nac: Cursed. Beware of this double meaning. Because of this double meaning,  Elves of old may give those they wish ill a nugget of gold to declare their hostility.\nThe gesture was often wasted on other races, who considered the gold a tribute.\n-------------------acalin: shop\n-------------------acaln: gold\n-------------------\n(a)chikr: (without the a:) ***** - denotes a woman who murders her children (or neglects them to death), which is considered a horrible crime due to the value of fertility and the rarity of Elf children. Without the 'a', the word degenerates\ninto a grave insult. Know that intentional cursing in Elven attracts ill-willing aspects.\n-------------------(a)dil: needy, desiring, wanton - When one forfeits the 'a' it becomes as of a vice.\n-------------------\n(a)dont: requesting, awaiting - Without the 'a' it becomes 'demanding', -er makes verb as always.\n-------------------adri: evil, immoral, hate.\n-------------------(a)elor: abomination, faithless, demon. -Denotes any non-humanoid of intelligence.\nWithout the 'a' the word becomes an insult to call someone disfigured. Know that intentional cursing in Elven attracts ill-willing aspects.\n-------------------Aengul: Aengul - race of semi-divine beings affiliated with light.\n-------------------aetah: age\n-------------------aeth: aether\n-------------------aher: to bless.\n-------------------ahern: blessing.\n-------------------akal: valuable, golden - less ominous.\n-------------------akaleh: wealth, money, valuable.\n-------------------akalin: shop\n-------------------\nakaln: gold - less ominous way to speak of gold.\n-------------------allim: sin  - allimar: to sin; alliman: sinful.\n-------------------andria: hope\n-------------------annyer: to dance\n-------------------Asul: Sun\n------------------\nata: ugly, tainted\n-------------------athri: serpent, snake\n-------------------a)iler: Wizard, conjurer, heretic -Any wizard or spiritual humanoid that's not a druid. When one forfeits the 'a' in the word it becomes an insult.\nIt should be noted that the word 'Ailer' is not used for your everyday magic user, but reserved for a powerful, exclusive brand of spellcasters.\n-------------------alor: salmon -aloronn/alonn: salty\n-------------------ame: forest\n-------------------\nannil: guiding -anniler: to guide, direct\n-------------------ante: Last\n-------------------arche: infrequent, sporadic, rare.\n-------------------(a)rhil: just fair (without the a:) vengeful, scorned.\n-------------------\nasimulum: sanctuary, glade, asylum, dedicated place (esp. one of nature).\n-------------------aver: to wander, to roam around aimlessly, to be confused.\n-------------------avern: wanderer, confused person.\n-------------------ayal: yellow\n------------------\nay(i)lu: liked, likable - may(i)lu: loved. The 'i' is optional, but may not be used if the object is not a person.\n-------------------ayla: good\n-------------------barbu: dark, black, hedgehog\n-------------------bilok: to create, to make.\n----\nBortu: dwarf\n-------------------(a)caele: sky. - The plural, caelan, is used to reference to the Seven skies and with that, God. The lack of an 'a' in front makes the word disrespectful or insulting.\n-------------------celia: star\n-------------------\nceru: strong, mighty\n-------------------chirr: wolf\n-------------------chul: to must\n-------------------cihi: city\n-------------------cinh: to stop, to cease\n-------------------ciw: to sleep\n-------------------\ncrua: hurting, suffering - which makes cruan: pain\n-------------------Daemon: Daemon - race of semi-divines affiliated with smoke and fire.\n-------------------dion: long - dionne: short\n-------------------divu: empty\n------------------\ndrui: Druid\n-------------------draoi: Evil/corrupted druid. Warlock. Sorcerer.\n-------------------ehier: to find\n-------------------enaer: guest\n-------------------ento: next\n-------------------ern: to can, to be able to\n-------------------eth: end, to end, to die -word can be a noun, passive verb and active verb depending on usage.\n-------------------evar: to protect, preserve\n-------------------eyr: to use, apply\n-------------------faesu: horse\n------------------\nfer: tool - This word creates a wide array of words for various tools, such as torches and pickaxes. They are discussed further in 'curious words'.\n-------------------fenn: ice\n-------------------feta: cold\n-------------------fih: to eat, to feed\n--------\nfin: region\n-------------------fiyem: reborn, renewed, revitalized\n-------------------fyu: full\n-------------------hae: her\n-------------------haelun: mother\n-------------------haler: to promise, to pledge\n------------------\nharriel: square\n-------------------heial: circle, council\n-------------------hier: near\n-------------------hileia: Peace. - Has no plural\n-------------------hiuw: to twitch, to move\n-------------------hiylu: Healthy / spirited\n------------------\nibar: white, owl\n-------------------igne: flame, fire - igne'sae: destruction, war\n-------------------iheiuh: wind\n-------------------iheiuhii: voice, breath\n-------------------ihn: stick\n-------------------ihnsil: spear\n------------------\nikur: wrought, cold, gray\n-------------------ikurn: iron\n-------------------ikru: foolish\n-------------------iller: to give, to grand\n-------------------ilum: glowing - when something only reflects light. ilumr: to glow.\n------------------\ninder: to read\n-------------------indor: book\n-------------------irrin: glass\n-------------------ito: to, here, within - Can also be a suffix. More on this in 'curious words'\n-------------------iyat: thought\n-------------------iyath: to think\n-------------------iylanh: to place\n-------------------kae: me\n-------------------kalem: Malin's year, - Roughly 26 Anthos years, denotes the length of a High Prince's reign.\n-------------------kaliri: Event, party\n-------------------\nkarim: Fortday, - The measure of time from one Dwarven sleeping cycle to the next, roughly 24 Anthos days.\n------------------\nkarin(ah): sunrise, - Also used to speak of day\n-------------------kathir: foe\n-------------------\nker: dark, sunset - Also used to speak of night\n-------------------khel: darkness - Strictly a noun\n-------------------kina: murdered, killed, death, in mortal danger-a noun used for someone who is marked for death by someone or something.\n---------------\nkrafeh: corpse\n-------------------krey: single. kreyn - pair.\n-------------------lae: him\n-------------------lar: sturdy, steadfast.\n-------------------lareh: boulder, mountain, rock\n-------------------larebil: anvil\n------------------\nlarueth: netherrack\n-------------------laureh: chosen, favored, noble\n-------------------laurir: Noble\n-------------------len: song\n-------------------lenaern: inn\n-------------------lenniel: melody\n-------------------lent: to (over)grow\n-------------------\nlenti: growth, moss.\n-------------------lentos: brown\n-------------------leyu: graceful, beautiful.\n-------------------liewyn: smile - liewynh: to smile, liewyn'saer: to entertain, to amuse; maliewynh: to laugh\n-------------------\nlle: kindness, good intention\n-------------------lie: dishonest, untrue, false, deceitful -lien: lie , liear: to lie; maliear: to betray (malieir: traitor)\n-------------------llir: friend\n-------------------lin: home, place -linan'sae: town\n--------------\nllum: sorrowful, mourning - llum'saer: to sadden\n-------------------llyt: aggressive, intimidating, -llyth: to strike\n-------------------lom: to fragment, to splinter\n-------------------lomi: fragment, splinter, pebble\n------------------\nlomal: flint\n-------------------luci: grape - lucionn: sour\n-------------------luer:  to imprison, to cage \n-------------------luerlent: flowerpot\n-------------------lye: we - Used to speak of elves, a people, or even the world as a whole. Often misused.\n-------------------lyu: silent - leads to the words lyun: silence; lyuer: to silence and lyu'saer: to censor \n-------------------maehr: wisdom, knowledge\n-------------------maelu: to know\n-------------------mahnih: wall\n-------------------\nMali: elf/elven - Word is both singular and plural, noun and adjective.\n-------------------maln: father\n-------------------marn: more\n-------------------marenne: less\n-------------------masuleh: anvil\n-------------------medi: helping, helpful\n-------------------meracahe: balance\n-------------------miar: lava miar'sae: griefing, harming, spiting\n-------------------mira: warm\n-------------------miruel: red\n-------------------myumier: to carry, to bring\n------------------", ""),
        StaticBook("Old Elven Part 3", "Anthron", "Curious words as a continuation from part 2. 1 - 4\n-------------------Elven Grammar 5 - 41\n------------------\nElven counting 42 - 45\n-------------------more on part 3\n-------------------\nAgain, more thanks to Sporadic for making the post!\nan*h- (a/o/i/e/u):\n\nBy adding one these five vowels into the place of the asterisk the prefix takes on a new usage. This prefix gains many new meanings when combined with other affixes and relies heavily on context.\nSentence order is especially important when using this prefix multiple times.\nElan*h [a/o/i/e]: North, South, East and West respectively.\n\nanh'sae: expanding, stretching - replacing the apostrophe with a vowel specifies in which direction.\nan*h il'an*h: - refers to a diagonal path, defined by the vowel used.\n-------------------\nElven Grammar.\n-------------------Sentence Order: The common order of elvish sentences is Subject-object-verb.\n\nPlural nouns: indicated by the suffix -an.\nThe apostrophe: Used in certain cases and possibly dictates a certain pronunciation.\nCurrent known uses are:\n\n*Denoting certain honorifics (such as sainted)\n*Between a modifier and a verb\n*Between a word and its suffixes (more on this below).\n*With the word 'eyha' (and) to connect sentences and and in terms of a list.\nUse of affixes: This has shown to be a very important factor in Elven language. Elves, tend to use multiple affixes to further define their nouns and verbs, rather than using separate words. For these, the following rules exist:\n*Most affixes seems to have two forms, depending on wether or not the word it relates to ends or begins with a vowel\n*Suffixes often get added with an apostrophe between it and the word.\n*If a word has multiple suffixes, there will only be an apostrophe between the full word and its last suffix. The priority in ordering the suffixes remains unknown.\n*If a suffix is seperated by the apostrophe, it inflects as if the last letter of the word it relates to was a consonant.\n\nRecombination of words:\nWhen one understands the heavy use of affixes and their combining to forge words,\nit is not surprising that the elves relied on a dictionary where this recombination was prevalent in creating new words. \nTheir core words were little, but their abundant use of affixes and linking words still allowed them to describe nearly anything\nunder the sun.\nExamples of this are:\nnarn'vallei: travel'water -> swim\nnarn'acaele: travel'sky -> fly\nathrinarn'acaele: serpenttraveling'sky -> Dragon.\npar'karinah: Before the sunrise -> Dawn\npar'ker: Before the sunset -> Dusk.\nuvul'igne: Firepowder -> Ash\nuvul'thrurn: Explosive powder -> Gunpowder\n\nWe also see this occuring a lot when the word tali is involved.\nMany more examples may be discovered by the studious scholar soon. Take heed, however, that any particular combination, while perhaps comprehensible, may not be correct. The language of nature seems very particular on what words may coexist in harmony.\n-------------------Affiliation: The suffix 'leh (note the apostrophe) designates affiliation, the possession of the object by the subject.\nNote: If no object is present, the suffix is implied to mean something similar to 'lives' or 'spirit'. However,\nit is not placed in translation\nEx: part of a common farewell 'kaean'leh evareh', means 'may (it) protect our spirit' literally, but is translated to 'may (it) protect us'.\n-------------------\nKnown inflections of the elven verbs:\n\nIn this section, the dash indicates the stem of the word.\n\n*-e: First person singular\n\n*-a: Second person singular\n*-e: Third person singular\n*-ae: First person plural\n*-an: second person plural\n*-eyae: Third person plural\n*-eh: Optative singular\n*-erih: Optative plural The optative inflection changes slightly depending on the ending letters of a\nverb. Most verbs end in -er, but if one ends in -ar the plural inflection of the optative becomes -arih ( the -ar- syllable is not repeated!). A verb ending in -h will have the -h- replaced by the full inflection of the optative (thus eth becoming eteh\nand eterih. Other irregular verbs have the inflection simply added to the end.\n\n*The stem of a verb functions as a singular imperative.\n*The stem of a verb used as a suffix to a noun (apostrophe where required) is a modifier\n*The optative serves as a wish ('may' + verb) in most cases. However, adding the negative (-ne) affix after the verb indicates the lack of permission.\n-------------------Pronouns as a subject:\nA pronoun is usually not used to denote the subject of a word, as the inflection of the verb itself would usually denote in which person the verb is denoted. However, on occasion a rather critical ambiguity may arise, like in the following example:\name ignere: The forest burns.\nShould we 'assume' a pronoun here, we can suggest that this sentence may also be translated as 'I burn the forest' or even 'he burns the forest'. This ambiguity is too serious to ignore, and therefore we are expected\nto place another pronoun here as the subject if we wish to denote an action like such:\n\nkae ame ignere: I burn the forest.\nKnow that a pronoun used as a subject alone never gains the suffix 'leh' (this is possible when it is an object, making the implied translation \"spirit\".)\n-------------------Infinitive:\nAn infinitive can be made by tying two verbs together with an apostrophe and adding an 'o' ('or-') prefix to the second verb (the infinitive). The first verb is usually the assisting verb (possibilities include can, must, will) and needs to be the only\nstem.\nThe infinitive verb receives the inflections similar to the way to a simple, single verbed sentence would.\nBy Example:\nito ame ern'onarna: You can travel to the woods\nAn infinitve can also occur without an assisting verb. In this case, you shall add an o prefix to the verb, but no inflection. An example of this:\nito ame onarn: to travel to the woods.\nTake note that the infinitive must not be used to express a wish, as only the optative form may serve this purpose.\nPast and future tense:\n\nTo create a past or future tense, we first need to take notice to the time of day. The words an elf uses for tenses depends on whether it's day or night at the time of speaking.\nFor a past or future tense, we add one of these four words at the very end of a sentence:\nkarin'ento: next sunrise\nkarin'ante: last sunrise\nker'ento: next sunset\nker'ante: last sunset\nThough elves have taken to shortening this to karinto, karinte, kento and kente in more casual speech.\nIf it is night, we use ker'ante for past, and karin'ento for future tense.\nIf it is day, we use karin'ante for past, and ker'ento for future tense.\n\nThis remains true even if the time period one wishes to denote is more than one sunset or sunrise from the time of speaking.\n-------------------\nThe verbs 'to be' and 'to have'\nBefore reading this, you might find it beneficial to have read about the word 'ito' in the 'CURIOUS WORDS' section.\nNote that:\nito kae: To him / on him\nito kae'leh: To his spirit / on his spirit ('spirit' being implied)\nNow, should we place a noun in front of this:\nmali ito kae: An elf to him. -In elvish, this is the equivalent of 'He has an elf'.\nmali ito kae'leh: An elf to his spirit. The equivalent of 'He is an elf'.\nIt becomes easy to mix these meanings up. After all, kae'leh is the possessive form and seems the logical candidate for the verb 'to have'.\nHowever, the implied addition of 'spirit' is very important here. Elves speak of their spirit, or essence, as\nsomething deeply intimate. When something belongs to you as a person, you have it. But when something belongs to your essence, you are it.\n-------------------\nCAPITALIZATION\n\nElves do not capitalize the word at the beginning of a sentence the way we do in common. Instead, only on a select few occasions are words capitalized, namely:\n\n*Names (of persons, places, titles)\n*Races (Mali, Valah, etc.)\n*Objects of worship (Usul, Velulaei), even when the word's not used to denote the divine being tied to the object\n*When denoting aspects of nature (such as Vuln being the aspect fox.)\n*Words of significant concepts are sometimes capitalized, such as 'lye', 'talar', 'acaele' and 'tuva'\n-------------------\nELVEN COUNTING\n\nElves, while their numerical system remains a subject of discussion, were able to write numbers 'in full' like we are. That is to say, they could name numbers of any size in regular conversation.\nAn Elven number, when expressed in full, consists of multiple words. Each word represents an order of magnitude. They were written in descending order, meaning the largest part of the number came first.\nElves had words for the numbers one to ten, and words for a hundred, a thousand, and so on. To denote multiples of hundred,\nhey would take a number from one to ten, and put it in front of a word denoting an order of magnitude, linking the two with an\naprostrophe\nDiscovered words for elven numbers are as follows:\n1: oem\n2: niut\n3: hael\n4: vailu\n5: kulin\n6: banih\n7: laier\n8: esun\n9: moiel\n10: telu\n100: matelu [ma-(mar-) a preffix for 'very']\n1000: marmatelu\n-------------------CONVENTIONS OF THE ELVEN LANGUAGE \n-continues to part 4-", ""),
        StaticBook("Old Elven Part 4", "Anthron", "Continuarion of part 3!\n-------------------CONVENTIONS OF THE ELVEN LANGUAGE 1 - 7\n-------------------\nThis is the last book, so final thanks to Sporadic for making these books possible on the forums!\nThe elven tongue was almost exclusively spoken. Evidence for this is found in the ruins of Inwe, where the writings of early Dark Elves were in an ancient dialect of the common tongue. The most often used elven words in writing are names\nsuch as Mali'nor (now Malinor) and Velulaei.\nWhatever caused the language to change so drastically through the ages is not known, but is especially remarkable due to the long lives of elves, which would presumably make evolution of a language go much slower.\nIt is suggested that a taboo on elvish was enforced, though why and by who remains unknown.\nIt has been rumored that elvish was also the language of nature, which the elves either learned through study or was taught to them by certain deities.\nThey then had the power to converse with nature directly, allowing\nthem to guide the growth of trees and migration of animals as if by magic. If this were true, elvish, assuming someone was capable of mastering the long-forgotten cadence of the words, would be a very potent language indeed.", ""),
        StaticBook("On Ancestors", "Sarrion", "On Ancestors\n-------------->\n\n  The following is a\nscripture on the Maliker relgious ways on thier preservence of ancestors in their kin. The following was written by Dak'ir Des'nox. Re-scriptured by Sarrion Zytiaear.\nThe Ancestors\n~                              ~\nAn in-depth look at the dark elven belief system\nThe Ancestral Plane\n----------------->\nThe fabled afterlife of the mali’ker. The Ancestral Plane is where honourable dark elves pass on and begin their eternal watch over the living. It is here, through their vigilant eyes, that the Ancestors judge\nthe mali’ker that still breathe and deem whether or not their actions are worthy of allowing them to pass onto an eternal paradise amongst their kin. Those that are judged as dishonourable and unworthy and condemned\nto an eternal darkness, blinded and alone. For to a people who thrive amongst family, there can be no worse hell than being away from those you love.\nShrines And Offering\n--------------->\nThere are three ways a dark elf may honour their Ancestors: through the creation of shrines in their honour, offerings, and how one carries themselves.\nShrines are where a mali'ker would go to place offerings and offer words to those above. When constructing a shrine, a dark elf must keep in mind which type of shrine they're making. There are three types of shrines: general shrines,\nusually found in public places and not dedicated to any specific ancestor. These shrines can be made from any material and in any sort of fashion. Clan shrines, which can be found in any common household to honour any fallen of that clan.\nA clan shrine is usually constructed out of materials that reflect that clan. For example, a clan whose history is rooted in the way of hunting may build  a shrine from bones of animals or a clan known for their jewel work may make a\nshrine out of elaborately carved stone, inlaid with precious gems.  Lastly, Greater Ancestral shrines. These shrines are always fashioned in a manner specific to the Ancestor it devotes. A shrine that devotes Khel The Zealous may\nfind itself carved with various imagery of great battles or adorned with weapons.\n\nUpon each and every shrine one would find a stone or metal bowl, these bowls are for the offerings a mali’ker would place to their Ancestors.\nOfferings of food, water, and carved idols being the most common. However, the same as with shrines, offerings must have some form of importance when being dedicated to clan’s fallen\nor a Greater Ancestor. As said previously, an offering upon a clan shrine depends on the clan’s origin and history while a Greater Ancestor’s offering depends upon who they’re bestowing their offer to.\nWhen one may honour Kaldo The Father, wine may be a common item. However, one would not offer wine to Delilah The Mother due to her distaste for it in life. After one presents their offering,\nusually words of praise are spoken or simply the reason why the offering was being presented. After which, the offerings are burnt within the offering bowl. Flames being place either directly into the bowl or below it to boil any liquid that was offered.\nIt’s believe that when these offerings are burnt, the smoke and steam from these offerings rise up into the Ancestral Plane and into the hands of those that have passed on.\nVeneration\n------------>\nThe third method in honouring the Ancestors is how one venerates the fallen. Veneration is relies on how one presents and carries themselves. Dressing like a harlot, killing your kin or others\nwithout good reason, even what you say. The Ancestors observe all. \n\nFor personal presentation, lacking covering for carnal reasons or dying your hair an outlandish colour would be considered dishonourable.\nHowever, revealing part of one's body to display scars or clan markings (tattoos, body paints, etc) would be acceptable provided it’s within reason. Even dying hair can be fine when taking clan traditions into account.\nSuch as Uuthlini and their trademark blue hair or Oussana and their tattoos. \n\nWhen it comes to combat, the dark elves are no stranger to conflict. But there’s a time and place for everything.\nWhen conflict arises amongst kin honour duels are quite common as they provide an outlet for their anger and sometimes improve relationships. During such duels a few things are expected, such as not striking at your opponent's\nback and the use of magic or trickery. Honour duels between mali’ker are meant to settle disputes, not instigate them further. These same rules apply when dueling those outside of the race as well. For a long time the world has seen\nthe dark elves as nothing more than thieves, cultists, and bandits and we must work to to change that. Knowing when to spare a life or when to even engage in conflict is paramount as well. For jumping into a frenzy at the lightest insult does no\nservice to your own honour and especially not of your Ancestors. During war time skirmishes, it is not uncommon to hear mali’ker call the names of particular Ancestors, Khel being the most common.\nEven choice of magic reflects upon the Ancestors. While magic is widely known amongst the dark elves, there are those that delve into it’s darker side. While all dark magic is widely disapproved of necromancy is above all the most dishonourable.\nFor it defiles the dead and bends them to the will of their user when they should be laid to rest.\nFunerals\n--------->\nThe passing of a loved one is always a saddening time. For dark elves, funerals must be attended to in a particular fashion. It’s believe that the Ancestral Plane resides in the skies above, so in order to release the soul\nof the fallen mali’ker cremation is how dark elves are sent into the afterlife. Through this method it is believed that the fallen’s soul is released. Burying a dark elf is considered sacrilegious as it denies their soul\nfrom ascending into the Ancestral Plane. If a body is lost, whether it be at sea or the person has gone missing and is believed dead. Then burning something that was dear to them is thought to work as well as if the item was close\nenough to them that it be considered part of that person.\nGreater Ancestors\n----------------->\nThese status of Greater Ancestors is bestowed upon those that have done great deeds in life or left a lasting impression on their people. The current Ancestral Pantheon are: Khel the Zealous,\nKaldo the Father, Delilah the Mother, Lenden the Joyful, Arganos the Festive, and Jiub the Strong. Each of these dark elves once walked among us and after their deaths were raised to a higher state of honour.\nThese Ancestors are given particular praise, shrines in their honour, even celebrations held in their name. However, honouring them is not the sole purpose of raising them to this honour.\nOne would also look them them for guidance, for their actions in life are not only to be praised but also to serve as teachers to the mali’ker.\n-End->\n\nThis scripture was written by Dak'ir Des'nox and re-written by Sarrion Zytiaear in tome form.", ""),
        StaticBook("On Augurs", "Revion Tathron", "On Augurs\n\nBy: Revion Tathron\nThe information presented in this book was acquired through two interviews with two different Augurs. As they answered the same questions with the same answers, one can reasonably assume this data pertains to all Augurs.\nAugurs share many characteristics with Spectres, and as such many be categorized as a type of them. They have yellow bodies consisting of a golden fluid-like substance with the viscosity of a thick liquid, and armored plates that appear fused to them.\nThey float roughly 1.5 feet off the ground, although an Augur was able to demonstrate floating 3 feet above the ground, albeit for an incredibly limited amount of time, akin to a \"jump\" for groundbound creatures. Augurs are created when a person is truly\ndevoted to Yeu Rhthulu, and her Thirty Strictures. However, the exact process for becoming an Augur was not revealed to the author of this tome. Augurs are able to switch back to their previous forms at will, and the process for doing so is similiar.\nThe 'normal', Descendant form of an Augur has some differences from those who are not Augurs. Firstly, they're completely sterile. Secondly, they appear to be totally incapable of feeling anger. Augurs whilst in their spectral form cannot be harmed by\nfire or electricity. It is unknown if they have a weakness to gold as many otherwordly creatures do: as the Augurs interviewed declined to be stabbed. (All in the name of science, of course.) Augurs do not require sustenance in the mortal sense: it is\nhypothesized that they subsist on Yeu Rhthulu's power alone.\nIn conclusion, Augurs seem an amiable, lively sort of race, which is quite uncommon for spectrals.\n\nEND.", ""),
        StaticBook("On Bryophites", "Toodles", "On Bryophites\nBy: Revion M. Tathron\nAt first site, Bryophites may appear incredibly strange-- entirely green figures, with various plants growing on their 'skin'. However, Bryophites were once normal people-- they were merely transformed by a parasitic plant dubbed 'Verdigris Moss', which\nreplaces the biological material of a person with the biological material of a plant. This, naturally, induces many changes in a person. These changes include:\n-An increased vulnerability to fire and cold.\n-The ability to subconsciously create a sedative that numbs a significant amount of pain.\n-Plants now grow in abundance on the skin, more bountiful than those found naturally.\n-Now experience the drawbacks of voidal magic more quickly\n-Cannot use dark magic without becoming physically ill.\n-They now photosynthesize, with a lack of sunlight inducing various negative effects until death.\n-A small, cordoned-off section of their mind is produced, which can be spotted by a\nmental mage, which is presumably the area of the brain which the parasite controls to maintain the plantlike functions of the Bryophite.\nThere are no behavioral changes in Bryophites-- they are not more inclined towards insanity, as said in other accounts. The Bryophite that the author spoke with seemed completely normal, in terms of psychological and behavioral traits.\nFor further information, 'Vertigris Moss' in the Plants and Farming section of the Library of Dragur is recommended, as it provides a more in-depth list of the physical changes in Bryophites.", ""),
        StaticBook("On Darkstalkers", "Toodles", "On Darkstalkers\nSecond Edition\nBy: Revion M. Tathron\nDarkstalkers, at first sight, are merely men in armor. This is a dangerous mistake to make, as they are abnormally fast and skilled undead warriors who do not feel pain from conventional sources. Under their armor, Darkstalkers, like Liches,\nare skeletons. Darkstalkers run off of life-force, and must drain it to keep from going insane and then dying. They accomplish this by manifesting some sort of life-force-draining black mist. Darkstalkers are created by Necromancers;\nspecfically, more than one. Darkstalkers have something known as a phylactery, which 'holds their life'. If this phylactery were to be destroyed, so too would, presumably, the Darkstalker. Darkstalkers, being undead, immortal creatures, are weak to Aurum\nand holy magics; indeed, these are the only means to inflict pain in one. However, Darkstalkers may be slain by more conventional weaponry, such as Ferrum, due to them being corporeal. Darkstalkers, unlike most Ghouls, are\nquite intelligent, and are capable of speech and reason. Certain Darkstalkers have been known to limit their draining to plants and animals in an effort to save lives. However, this makes them feel weary and aggravated. When killed, Darkstalkers leave\nnaught behind but empty armor.", ""),
        StaticBook("On Geomancy", "Sarrion", "\"Geomancy\"\nEarth Evocation\n--------------->\nBy Sarrion Zytiaear\n\nThe following was transcribed through an interview with the Geomancer known as Coltaine Wick.\nWhat it is:\n\nEarth Evocation, in its most basic form is the drawing forth earth in its various forms, from the void. It differ greatly from the other forms of evocation in that, there is a plethora of  things you can evoke,\nbe it granite stone, sand, gravel, shards of quartz. You may create a massive boulder to flatten your enemy or create a whirling torrent of sand with sharp shards of quartz interspersed\nto scour the flesh from your enemies bones. It is a rather open ended magic, the limit being your own creativity.\nHow to Cast:\n\nIn casting, you must be in a state of concentration, like most other magic, to be interrupted is to lose your connection to the void. You must concentrate on that vast plain of potential known as the void and there create that\nwhich you seek to draw into the world, Earth  Evocation is different in, at least for me, you can't create boulders 7 feet in diameter right out the gate, you start small, and either grow or subtract from that.\nSuch as a pebble, you may use that pebble as a sort of bridge to draw forth more, expanding it exponentially. Sand one would evoke in large enough quantities\nto pressure through the void into our own world. One must be careful, all evocations have their dangers but earth more so, it is an easy thing to accidentally crush one of your own limbs, practice makes perfect.\nRed Lines: \n\nLike all evocations you may not evoke defined intricate forms, no statues, no flying stone birds, that would require conjuration, a whole different skill entirely . You cannot create mud or quicksand without the aid of water evocation.\nThe general limit in size of what one can evoke is a boulder around 7 feet in diameter give or take, it depends on the individual's skill and mana pool. To try and go beyond those boundaries risks harm to yourself and possibly death.", ""),
        StaticBook("On Ghouls", "Toodles", "On Ghouls\nBy: Revion M. Tathron\nGhouls, the lowest of all undead creatures I have yet encountered. Slavering, mindless beings, these rotten corpses are only driven by the need for life-force.  While Ghouls may be killed, as for all undead, their death is merely temporary. They will\nrise again, despite the efforts of common or holy men. When they do rise, they will be hungrier and more angry than ever before. The reason that Ghouls do not die is because they have soul shadows, rather than souls. Soul shadows can be compared\nto souls as embers are to flame. However, there a few Ghouls capable of mastering their Soul Shadows and retaining some semblance of humanity, but this is uncommon.\nGhouls are normally created through the machinations of Necromancy, but the author has been told by an unverifiable source that a Ghoul will inevitably rise from any dead body as time passes.\nGhouls are vulnerable to Aurum and holy magics, as all undead are. Their cravings of life-force can be assuaged by a Necromancer to cease their attack.", ""),
        StaticBook("On Pigmen", "Tristanm1", "A Brief History of Pigmen: Aegis - Kalos\n\nBy Sunshine Druid Verden\nThe appearance of Zombie Pigmen was first documented during the Second Iblees Invasion, several hundred years ago. These unnatural monsters were used by the Fallen Daemon as powerful soldiers during his war of conquest against the mortal races of Aegis,\nwhere their group mentality and shear ferocity was crippling to the defenders.\n\nUnlike other lesser Undead, the Zombie Pigmen possessed an intellect far superior to any common Zombie or Skeleton. They would always be seen in possession of\njagged, golden swords and very rarely were encountered alone. Their speed was almost on par with that of the living, a trait that allowed few the luxury of escaping.\n\nAfter the invasion was ended and the Dark One had been sealed away for good, many\nstrange things were observed by the population of Aegis. While the mindless Zombies and Skeletons continued to attack anyone on sight, the Pigmen did not. They had become passive, almost completely indifferent to the world around them. Instead of\nmindlessly killing, they began to aimlessly roam the world, staring blindly at the things around them as if they weren't truly there.\n\nAfter Aegis had crumbled and sank into the sea, many thought they would never see the creatures again. And indeed, they\ndidn't. For a time. But, again, the Pigmen emerged from the unknown, and they were furious. For many years the Pigmen were found roaming the darkest corners of Asulon and attacked anyone foolish enough to get close. The reason for this is not clear, and\nmany theories have arisen to explain. However, none have been proven.\n\nYet another strange event was observed on the island of Elysium. A large volcano near the temporary Elven settlement erupted and spewed lava over the Malinor farms,\nand the Pigmen rode it down the slope. They were, thankfully, peaceful this time; but they appeared in numbers never seen before. Hundreds of them began to blindly wander from their lair, spilling over the border into the Orcish deserts. There they were\ndestroyed not by men, nor by beast, but by cacti. The creatures tore themselves to shreds over the next few months as they walked through the cacti farms, and they probably would still if it wasn't for the piles of their dead brethren impeding their\nmovement.\n\nSince arriving in Anthos there has only been one reported case of Pigmen. This was a very small issue, where only three or four Pigmen would wander around a border wall in the Wilds. A magical fountain was placed\nat this site, and all has been quiet since then.\n\nIf the Pigmen are here on Anthos, they are hiding very well.", ""),
        StaticBook("On the Void", "miningtilldeath", "Index\n\nChapter I What is the void\n\nChapter II How we can use the void\n\nChapter III Connecting to the void\nChapter I\n\nWhat is the void? No doubt you as the reader know it to be the source of arcane magic (which includes the art of evocation, illusion and alteration). But what actually /is/ it? The void is a plane of existance that contains absoloutely\nnothing. Yet at the same time, it contains everything. Strange and complex as this may sound, it is in fact relatively simple. You see, the void is a place of /potential/. Thoughts, feelings, elements, everything really exists within the void yet puerly\nas potential, waiting to be shaped and formed. Such is the role of an arcane mage\nChapter II\n\nHow can we use the void? As mentioned in the previous chapter, everything that exists in our world exists as potential in the void. But within the void, it is useless to us. Therefore, through trainning a mage can learn to connect to\nthe void and harness its potential in various ways. One example, is evocation, the art of evoking a certain element. An evocationist uses the void to form their element out of the voids potential and then pull it into our physical world. They will have\ncontrol over this evoked element (mind you the level of control depends on their skill) and it will be able to effect the physical plane (evoked flames will burn, evoked winds will blow). Make no mistake however, that which comes from the void does not\nbelong in our physical plane. As such, as soon as a mage looses connection to the void, be it because he is too tired to continue, his concentration is broken or he simply severs the connection, that which came from the void will return there. This\napplies to all arcane magic, not just evocation. You may be wondering, if the void is full of everything, why can't mages /make/ everything. The answer lies with knowledge. You see, creating something from the void is no walk in the park. A mage requires\nabsoloute knowledge of what they are attempting to create before they can create it. For this reason, arcane mages focus on one area of harnessing the void (or more for advanced mages). An evocationist will spend time learning and experiencing all they\ncan about their element, a conjurer will disect creatures so as to understand how they work and an illusionist needs to know how something feels before they can make someone else feel it. So, how is it we can connect to the void?\nChapter III\n\nConnecting to the void itself is relatively simple to learn but takes a lot of time to master. An arcane mage just starting out learning his craft (regardless of what that craft is) will need to spend a lot of time meditating. Why do\nthey meditate? To achieve the state of nothingness needed to connect to the void. A connection to the void requires your mind to be  empty. All thoughts, feelings and emotions must be brushed aside. For a mage just starting out, this will be quite rather\ndifficult and requires a comfortable and secluded area in which they can meditate with extreme focus. To a mage just starting out meditating, it is best if you find somewhere quiet as when you hear a noise your mind will subconsciously focus on it, which\ndisrupts your focus. When meditating for the first time, to not expect to summon anything. When a mage has /truly/ gotten a grasp for this state of nothingness, they will feel a connection to the void for the very first time. Nothing will physically\nhappen but you, the mage will most surely feel the power of the void. I can not say how exactly you will feel it. The result differs from person to person, with some fainting, some feeling immense power surge through them and some having bleeding noses.\nBut you /will/ feel it. After making your first connection it will slowly get easier from there as you progress, now that you know what you need to achieve. You can now start experimenting with your art, assuming you already have extensive\nknowledge on what you intend to create. Bare in mind that pulling something from the void, wether it be a feeling, an element or a creature, requires mana. Large and or longer sustained spells require larger amounts of mana to keep them from returning to\nthe void. It is important that you recognise your limits, for mana has a physicall strain on the body, leaving you fatigued. A mage that does not recognise his limits may end up knocking himself out from his own spell which is quite counter productive. As\na new mage, DO NOT try anything large or long. You will not be able to sustain and provide the mana needed. Start small and work your way up. With lots of meditation and practice, there will come a day when you may connect to the void in an instant.\nThe way of the mage is lifelong. Mastery is simply staying the path. So stay the path\n\n\n\nJonathan Elers, Regent of the Arcane Delvers and Aeromancer\n\nTranscribed 1st of sun's smile 1445", ""),
        StaticBook("Once more", "Glee", "Once more into the fray...\n\nInto the last good fight I'll ever know...\n\nLive and die on this day....\n\nLive and die on this day...", ""),
        StaticBook("Opinion On Truth", "Sofetios", "An Opinion On Truth\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n                        -By Sofetios\nBefore we begin, I just wanted to say that this book will shortly cover a variety of subjects regarding my opinion of fear, doubt, truth, belief, lies, and how I think they correlate with each other. This book is just my opinion.\nYou don’t need to believe it or not believe it. To begin we will start with fear.\n\n                    Fear\n\nWhat is Fear? Fear is when you are afraid of something. But what does it mean to be afraid of something?\nI think fear occurs most often in the absence of knowledge, when you aren’t sure about something. Fear of the unknown is a very common thing, as the unknown can possibly do anything.\nIf you had complete confidence that something will happen a certain way, then you likely will have little fear about it unless it is a bad thing that will occur.\nThough if something goes the wrong way when you are fully confident about something, That may cause the next subject to happen. Doubt.\n\n                   Doubt\n\nDoubt is something that can cause fear.\nAs I explained earlier it ties in with the lack of knowledge in something. Doubt can be a very powerful thing. It changes, or attempts to change your perspective on things, for better or worse, but usually worse.\nTo avoid both fear and doubt, I think it is best to find one of the things that defeats both. Truth.\n\n                  Truth\n\nTruth is a very simple thing, Though it can be very hard, or easy, to obtain.\nIf you know the truth of something, that means you have true knowledge in a certain part or all aspects of it.  This defeats both doubt and fear, because fear is caused mainly by doubt, and doubt requires uncertainty in something.\nThere is, however, something that increases doubt. Most all of us do it. Lie.\n\n                     Lie\n\nLies corrupt the truth, they make things harder to make out.\nIn my perspective I think that the truth is a very simple thing to understand, it is lies that corrupt it and makes it more unnecessarily complicated.  In my opinion, lying causes most of doubt and fear.\nThe next and last subject I like to call absolute truth. \n\n           Absolute Truth\n\nThe thing with how doubt works is it tries to prevent us from getting to the truth.\nLies and doubt have happened so much that sometimes even if we find the absolute truth, we still doubt it, Unless of course we believe it’s the truth. And finally, what to believe is the truth. Well, that’s for you to decide.\n(Please let me know if you like my philosophical writing by telling me in person or sending a bird! ((Sofetios)) )\n\n-Sofetios", ""),
        StaticBook("Order of Nine", "Jack", "The Order of Nine was first established to protect the realm of Athera against any threats that may have came from across the sea, where the Betrayor lived.\n\nFounded by the Nine Heralds, they soon diversified into various chapters\nThe most notable ones being the Medraut the Skybreaker, Borz the Holy, Artorigus the Sacred. \n\nTheir deeds and valor spanned the continent, but as all things they too must have met their end.\nThough they sacrifice themselves to the Seal, their will shall forever remain. The Path of Nine is open to those who wish to protect. \n\n\n-Howard Guiles", ""),
        StaticBook("Overcome Us!", "Saeed Ibn Malek", "No Power Can Overcome Us!\n-------------------A Song By Saeed Ibn Malek\n-------------------There is no power that can overpower us\nMy country won't be shaken and its light no one will erase\nThere is no power that can overpower us\nMy country won't be shaken and its light no one will erase Whoever walks this earth and thinks he is Akagani\nif he tries to use his power here\nWe will break his hand\nWhoever walks this earth and thinks he is Akagani\nif he tries to use his power here\nWe will break his hand Eagles of the sky and lions of the land\nwe are your men Haria!\nOn the Holy book we swore to protect your soil\nEagles of the sky and lions of the land\nwe are your men Haria!\nOn the Holy book we swore to protect your soil When you call out, Before you blink Millions\nwill answer you call\nUntil death we shall fight\nWhen you call out, Before you blink Millions\nwill answer you call\nUntil death we shall fight There is no power that can overpower us\nMy country won't be shaken and its light no one will erase\nIt's difficult for day to become night and for a river to turn to fire\nbut it is even harder for an enemy to step foot on my land Call with a word and belief,We will not accept disgrace\nWhen God created this land he made it part of heaven on earth\nCall with a word and belief,We will not accept disgrace\nWhen God created this land he made it part of heaven on earth\nhere is no power that can overpower us\nMy country won't be shaken and its light no one will erase\nWhoever walks this earth and thinks he is Akagani\nif he tries to use his power here\nWe will break his hand!\n-------------------By Saeed Ibn Malek\n(I did not make this Irl <3 )", ""),
        StaticBook("Oxford", "Kale le Prevost", "Caliphate Clinic\n\nHead Doctor Kale\n\nPatient's Name: Oxford\n\nAge: 5\n\nRace: Human\n\nGender: Male\nCondition/Injury: Three cuts from glass shards, one on the chest, one on the arm, and one on the leg. Very minor cuts.\n\nTreatment plan: Patient was brought into surgery. Easily stitched up. No numbing medicine.\nDoctor who treated the patient: Kiveck\n\nCost: 15 minas\n\nDate: 14th of The Deep Cold, 1522\nEnd result: Patient will come in for a checkup later on. Seemed to be in no real pain, although he puked in the surgical room soon after surgery ended.\n*Kivecks signature would be on this page*\n\n-Kiveck Irelia\nCaliphate Clinic\n\nHead Doctor Kale\n\nCondition/Injury: Came in for a quick checkup.\n\nTreatment plan: N/A, all wounds were healed.\nDoctor who treated the patient: Hileia'elr\n\nCost: 5 mina\n\nDate: 13th of Malin's Welcome, 1523\n\nEnd result: No issues, cuts healed.\n*Hileia's signature would be here*\n\n\n~Hileia'elr", ""),
        StaticBook("Pages.", "Beltran D'avre", "\"Young pages, Black and new.\n\nFolded neatly into a pamphlet. \n\nStaples along a life line, a cover fresh from the print.\n\nLucky to have been torn at first light, creamy white .\n\nBinding not yet dried.\n\"Pencil's mark not yet touched.\n\nThe remains of what once, was priveleged but not yet knowing.\n\nA small braunh upon a tree.\nStanding tall amongst bark thick with green.\"\n\"Endings, dipped in poison.\n\nPurple veins blemishing gnarled foliage.\n\nThe pool of death, drowning cess but not yet.\n\nThoughts of fire, futility of body.\n\nOld, thoughts grows from within.\n\"Parchment damp with age, wrinkled skin painted.\n\nWith designs, experienced and colourful.\n\nBut once was, now aging.\n\nAs books gaze into the fire, endlessly burning. \n\nDespair dances with the shadows.\n\"Perhaps it was better, to have faced the flames blank.", ""),
        StaticBook("Parents and Orcs", "Heruza", "Orcs have a nasty habit of ruthlessly murdering parents. This fact is known, along with how they ruthlessly murder anything except their own kin. Kids whos parents are killed by orcs grow up on the streets to become assassins.", ""),
        StaticBook("Passport", "Razum Dar", "===Heil Vandaloria===\n-------------------Vandalorian Passport\n-------------------\n\nThis Document hereby grants permission to the bearer who has likeness in name and description to live and work within the Kaisereich.\n\n==================\n===Heil Vandaloria===\n\nName: §", ""),
        StaticBook("Paths", "Gwendolyn Powell", "One step after another\nI found myself walking a path, a path laden with roots\nA path breathing and living beneath my foot\nSilant at my touch\n\nAs I wadnered, on and on\nI took a turn\nA turn down a darker, road, and one hidden from the sun\nA path dusty, dry, and lifeless\n\nThis path I waled for many a year\nLost admist a sea of dark\nLost, on a path that turned slowly upwards, and soon became too difficult to walk\nAnd there I sat, pondering my loneliness\nOut of the corner of my eye, I glimpsed the sun\nBrilliant, bountiful and beautiful\n\nI stood, abd began to trudge slowly towards the light\nHearing birdsong, hearing the rustle and movements of leaves\nFeeling warmer and warmer as I went\nThe loneliness seeping from my bones\n\nThe dark shrank away in the light\nThis new path, was one brimming with life\nMuch like the first\nBut now, I began to hear something\nA quiet breathe, tiny beneath the sounds of chirping\nThe hoof beat of a running dear, and the great sway of an oak tree\nSo I listened more closely\nImmersing myself in the slow and steady beat\nOf the true world\nThe growing, giving, loving world\nThat I had once ignored\nAnd had now, finally\n\nFound a place in my heart\nA place, where it began to beat within me\nA place, that I hope to see flourish\nTo see flourish, like the flowers and astoundingly wonderful world around me", ""),
