        StaticBook("Smelly.", "Laethis Izalith", "You stink.", ""),
        StaticBook("Sorcerers", "The Eternal Library", "Sorcerers:\nRecognizing the Various Varieties\n\nby Arthur Versluis\nAmongst those who possess distinctly closer bonds to the void, a few different kinds have been recognized. This short excerpt will attempt to bring the reader through these distinctions, and hopefully provide aid in attaining some small measure of\nenlightenment in the subject matter.\n\n\nThe Common Robed Mage\nSorcer(ess) - Magician - Conjurer - Wizard - Robe\n\nThe typical conjurer often accompanied by a staff and draped in long cloaks, adorned\nin all manner of strange markings, runes and often odd colours, often depicted as an old man with a long, thick beard, and possessing a vast reservoir of knowledge esoteric. There is no doubt that this variety is the most renowned and favoured.\nThe common mage is often most well-versed in the forbidden art of magical combat- with more powerful spells and hexes, and, depending on the proficiency of the caster, all the more perilous. However, with these strengths come weaknesses, for the common\nmage is often, by tradition, frail and lacking in physical prowess, endurance, hardiness and such due to the almost fanatical obsession that such ilk often possess with the endless search and acquisition of more knowledge, and in turn, more of origins\nprofane.\n\nThe Household Mage\nWitch Maid - Hearth Mage\n\nThe common mage's humbler counterpart of lesser renown, the Household Mage could very well be the lowly servant of a small measure too efficient at a crude task of\nsweeping the kitchen floors, or even the unassuming cook who heats the oven furnace a moment too quickly. The subtlety of the magic these beings possess and put into practice makes them quite difficult to discern to those without the knack for spotting\nsuch things. One could, perhaps rightly, assume that such beings might hardly pose any considerable amount of threat, due to their lack of experience and utility in the field of combat. Household Mages can be either shnned members of the secluded Mage's\nGuild or former, disgraced apprentices who, having in the past acknowledged their inferior ability or lack of talents, had decided to follow an entirely different path, seeking a livelihood in different environment. Their spells are most often simplistic\nin nature and effect, and pose very little danger- if any at all. These mages are seen as more benevolent than the common Mage, and physically more robust though such things are quite difficult to confirm, given the elusive and subtle nature of the mages\nin question.\n\nThe Battle Maage\nWarrior-Witch - Spellblade - Witchman - Mystic Knight\n\nThe rarest and least liked of the known types. The Battle Mage is seen as the bane of chivalry and honourable duels, with little\nregard for learning more of this art. The Battle Mage is most often spotted in places where war and conflict are commonplace as frost on a cold winter's morn. He is often the result of a fighter who, through rigorous and excruciating training, had learnt\nboth the way of the blade and the art of the Arcane- applying both theories and practices in harmonious synergy. The existence of such beings suggests existence of some clandestine groups or knightly orders that had handpicked and set aside some select\ncandidates to undertake the rigors required to achieve this balance of spell and blade. The training of a would-be Battle Mage is the stuff of tales and legends- often known to be one of- if not THE most dangerous path to the art of magic.\nApprentices that have been found and deemed fit candidates were often rumoured to have died of various accidents- including, but not limited to: Death by fire and cinders, of spell mishaps and eternal sleep, of ice and water, and of broken shards\nof marble and glass. The fortunate few - if one could even call them as such- that did somehow manage to live through the horrors of their training, and the ire and malice of the commonfolk quite often found themselves unable to resume the lives they had\nonce led, some distant ago, now bent purely on suffering and death as heralds of doom and ruin. Battle Mages are often not as proficient in the art of magic as their more focused counterpart: the common Sorcerer. However, their skill with and mastery of\nthe sword may, at times, more than compensate for this lack of further magical prowess and potency. Hence, it would be most unwise for the regular mage to step within the range of the Battle Mage, neither frail nor unit, could quite easily close the\ndistance, and deal a devastating blow.", ""),
        StaticBook("Sorry.", "Echo Grace", "Being sorry can \nbe very hard when\nWe're overwhelmed with\nour Pride,\n\nBut Sometimes our pride\nMust take\nA fall before\nWe lose out,\n\nAnd when we have to say\nsorry we mean it\nfrom out soul,\nA meaningless sorry\nis like a\n\nBroken\n\nPromise\n\nTo the\n\nHeart.", ""),
        StaticBook("Soul Gems", "Rosso", "Soul Gem\n\nIt is theorized that when a vessel or body is compromised, it’s soul escapes into the Existing Plane (The plane which all life exists) for a brief moment before dissipating into the void.\nIf such a theory is true, then could it not be possible that the soul is captured?\n\nHowever to capture a soul requires a vessel for the soul to remain.\n\nThere arises the theory of Soul Gems.\nSoul Gems are not necessarily gems or precious minerals, but hollow objects made of glass, stone, or gold.\n\nAlthough a simple design, it represents the void- A center of Nothingness surounded by a barrier separating it from the Existing Plane.\nWhen a soul escapes it’s vessel, instead of dissipating into the true void, it enters into the Soul Gem- A representation of such.\n\nAlike a living body, a gem may be compromised. The barrier between the soul and the Existing\nPlane broken.\n\nIt is hypothesized that a Soul Gem alone can do nothing, it remains a simple hollow object, but if it is commanded, perhaps by symbols or markings, the mana of the soul within the Gem may be used to harness the void’s power.\nBut to understand which marking a soul will react to requires the knowledge of the Arcane and the Soul itself.\n\nAs the universe progresses, more will understood of souls and how they react.\nThis theory ends with a final thought: Does a soul’s thoughts remain? The inference is no, as one’s thoughts are developed through life, not the void. \n\n-Tahjeet Mubdee\nSoul Gems\n\nWritten by Tahjeet Mubdee\nCoppied by Rosso\nVaerHaven", ""),
        StaticBook("Soul Resonance", "The Pumpkin Druid", "Soul Resonance Field\nWritten by:\nThe Pumpkin Druid\nI came up with this theory back in Thales however I only recently have begun researching it now in Athera.\nHave you ever had an epiphany? Have your friends ever 'magically' appeared to come save you?\nI believe everyone has experienced this at least once in their life-time. After observing such acts a long time I decided to create a theory on such a phenomenon. I believe that each person has the ability to connect to an invisible field.\nI am led to believe that Soul Resonance is accidentally used in every-day life. For example, people will accidentally use this ability when in danger to contact others and bring their friends to their exact location.\nI call this accessible field: 'Soul Resonance'. Whether a soul is required or not to actually access this field is up for debate as further research is required.\nSoul Resonance allows for at least two sentinent beings to send mental messages to each other, similarly to telepathy. The ability to do so is within every sentinent being that I have studied, descendents included.\n*Further research is needed to see if plants and animals can connect to the Soul Resonance Field.\nI shall call this place a 'Forum'. Much like a real forum people can address their thoughts and concerns for the realm of Athera.\n*More of the 'Forum Theory' in the book 'Forums and Skygods'.", ""),
        StaticBook("Stone Men Riddle", "Cheza", "Stone men... \nSpeak stone words...\nEggs do not hatch.\nFor face without mouth...\nSpeak words that they lack.\nHow do they speak?\nThese stone men with none...\nUnmoving lips, eyes and ears sewn shut...\nWhat of our hands, what of our tools?\nIn a wordless whisper, they do nonetheless move...\nFor such is the ode...\nOf brothers of fish.\nMountains of madness, chisels and wish.\nMountains of stone...\nMountains of words.\nStanding in forrest...\nMessage unheard.", ""),
