        StaticBook("Task From Verden", "Jallentime", "((X -425 / Z -331))\n\n*A simple map has been drawn over the page, leading to a specific point of interest somewhere on the continent*\n\nI would like for you to take Tony, the lakefish, back to his home at Highland lake. The lake has been having troubles recently with an invasive parasite, and Tony here is our solution. Also, please don't eat him.", ""),
        StaticBook("Amoss The Wizard", "§bArthur Caulfield", "Amoss the wizard spoke of death to the prince. \n  Yakov, the target, had not been seen since.\n  Hold up in a castle full of armed guards. \n  Amoss' mind was smashed into shards. \n  Believing he could take on all of the men.\n  Amoss the wizard\n\nrushed into Oren.\n  Then rushing back out, chased by an angry mob.\n  Amoss the wizard looked quite the knob.\n  Surrounded by metal, which could deliver such pain.\n  Amoss the wizard gave up and put in chain.\n\nGrigor, the lead man in a mob of patriotic Orenians. \n  A determined mind that put him above millions. \n  Organised the mob into a killing machine. \n  Each section took the orders ever so keen.\n  To shed blood, for the emperor who runs this place.\n\nAnd oh how they moved, at such a high pace it would seem to be a race to rip off the enemies face.\n  Eraborn. No one saw him, sneak on the rooftop. \n  Only when his eyes fell on to Grigor did he stop.\n  Withdrew a bow, and an arrow made from\n\nthe finest of steel. \n  To make sure that where the head struck, the wound would not heal.\n  I kid you not, it struck his head, but he shrugged it away. \n  Many may tell you different, but I was there on that day.\n\nA day when the guards seized the man on the roof.\n  But using his magic, he escaped their clutches with a poof. \n  While that man is being hunted Amoss is on trial.\n  The treachorous things he shouted were ever so vile.\n\n\"Off\" shouted the crowd, \"Off with his head!\"\n  And after the trial, I am sure Amoss will be dead.", ""),
        StaticBook("The Farmer Pt. 2", "§bWilhelm von Bran", "The Farmer\n            &\n             The Witch\n            OR\nThe Defeat of\n             The\n        Conjuror\nBy,\n   \n   Wilhelm von Bran\n\nPart II - End\n\n“Papa! Papa, I found Mama!” cried Erika in innocent charm, her beauty quite similar to that of her mother’s. An odd silence fell upon the group, a stillness. The gypsies stepped back and simply watched the interaction closely.\n\n“Amelie, how could you! I loved you!” cried out Herr Vertuger in a pitiable rage.\n\n“Ah dear for vhich mein heart did bleed, you are now vhere ze moon is seen! You say you love me vith ze sveetest vords, but come to hug me vith zose swords?”\n\nThe lady responded stepping ever closer, her hands moving as if cursing, and her words forming sick rhymes as if spells.\n\nWorried, the farmer beckoned Erika back to him but she stood still, as if frozen. “Please, Amelie, just let her go! You can come back vith me!”\n\n“Sveet Anders, how I vish I could! But my home is now amidst ze vood! Amidst ze crow, ze goat, ze bitch, for indeed you know I am a witch!” The gypsies, filled with fear dropped their weapons and stood in fright, the witch moving closer every step.\n\nAnders Vertuger stood, confused and faint. “But Amelie, how could you be a vitch? You are my vife, come back to me!”\n“Nein, zis cannot be, my love, come vith me und ascend above. Vasting your life is so tragic. Come vith me und accept ze magic!”\n\nThe farmer turned to his daughter “Come mein dear, your mother ist not ze same,” he then turned to the gypsies “To arms, mein gallant heroes! Zis curse can be qvelled by ze grace auf Gott! Rise you Creationist spirits! Be vith me, Alphonse!”\n\n“You speak auf ze Creator here? Be assured ze lies you bring strike no fear. Indeed, magic is opposed to zis notion, so come to me und stop ze commotion!”\n\nWith this new found inspiration, the gypsies leapt at once before she could cast any spells or react, they subdued her and threw her back to the throne. “Release ze spell Amelie, give mein daughter back to me!”\n\n“Why, oh Anders, do you demand so loud? Do you zhink ve fight above ze clouds? Indeed zhere is no need to yell, you have not yet reached ze gates auf hell!”\n\nWith those words, the gypsies released their hold and turned menacingly back to Anders, their brands lighting beneath their tunics.\n\n“Tell me, my sweet what will break this spell?” He shouted to his wife as the gypsies approached\nInstead of responding, the witch simply remained sitting\n\n“But how, mein vife, did zese men come under your spell, but gut Fazher Alphonse did not?” Anders retreated away, his sabers held high, ready to attack.\n\n“The good father was pure of heart, and these men have done things that he would not. Albeit, you can have a good defense, if you make remove of every sense!”\n\nWith this riddle, the farmer cast one of his sabers out the window and charged through the gypsy zombies to the lady and swung violently, in one swipe her nose was removed.\n\nThe Queen screamed a horrible scream and began to turn homely and plain. The gypsies shook violently and quaked.\n\n“I am sorry, mein love, I know not why, but I am cursed, I cannot fly! I’ll tell you vhat your Gott behooves, zhat each sense be cut; removed!” Wanting only for this tale to end, the farmer plucked the eyes from his fallen wife.\n\n“Vithout mein eyes I cannot see, Oh Erika remember your mother, Amelie! Dear Anders, make zhis curse end qvick, lest our daughter become so sick!”\nCrying, now, the farmer took both her hands and severed them at the wrists.\n\n“Alas, mein hands are cut! Und soon ze door auf life slammed shut, at last mein cursed soul must die. Oh Anders, I bid you: never cry!”\n\nAnders, reluctant reached his hand into the witch’s mouth and pulled her tongue sharply bringing his sword closer, ending her last sense; taste.\n\nIndeed with this shriek, the fog of the valley lifted at once and the Sun showed brightly through the stained glass, the pitiable image of the Lady was even more seen as an ugly and plain, pale and unattractive.\n\nAt once, he held his wife close, but one second more, he was only clutching her white dress. The gypsies laid unconscious and Erika wept in the corner.\n\nAnders however, thrust his sword in the air and spoke with eloquence, before unknown to him, “Ah dear vife I find you gone, but vith me und Erika, you still live on. Und vhile I vanquished, und made you tot, I kneel here before you und pray to Gott.”\n\nTogether, the Waldenian family returned to normalcy in that little town of Ansbluch.\n\nIf you, reader, wonder what the moral of this story might be. Take heed and read more carefully; Take heed and do not take it light, lest you find the Lady White.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n--The End", ""),
        StaticBook("Moonlight Dance", "§bLucius Tython", "The sun submerged itself beneath the dimming salmon mountaintop to the sight of the harmonic moonlight begining its climb from the darkened earth. The air was amongst a thinning moisture that tingled the auromic smell of lingering fern. Perfectly placed\n\nfingers were present amongst the warmth of her grass pressed palm. Her teals eyes pierced the skin to which she glanced, enticing my very being. The charming aura surrounding such a very being pulled me closer to her ever so slightly. Returning the gaze I\n\npulled my lips closer to hers, watching the dimming sight of her illuminated lips under the moonlight as I found my eyes closing furthering my body towards hers. The warming sensation of her lips became that of a snug hearth under a wood burning fire.\n\nMy mind soon became detained in cadence of her lips on mine. A joyful ache overtook me as her arms found home upon my shoulders wrapping themselves around my neck. The slight tug of her arms took me onto the ground alongside her torso. The swing of her\n\naffection brought me amongst a trance of cherishment. The trance was broken by an unevitable pulling from my lips. My adorning eyes opened in a graceful haste to look upon a warming smile within her eyes. Almost as if it was a pleasant virus, I too felt a\n\nsmile manifesting at my mouth. The beating of our breath was only so slightly shadowed by the delicate rushing water of the creek. I could only take a moment of admiration towards her before I felt the forward pulling of her weakened hands among my shirt.\n\nThe scene faded into an Aengulic light as I was pulled against her chest.\n\n-Published on 17th of the Amber Cold, 1472 by Lucius Tython ((Kyle4924))", ""),
        StaticBook("Feyko's Journal", "§bFeyko Drei", "Day 1.\nI have left the city. Leaving uniform and friends behind. I will venture towards Alras, as I have heard that it is there were I may find the information I need to find the creature which has stolen all I have worked for.\n\nDay 2.\nI am currently seated outside of the walls of Alras, looking up to the sky. I have gained no information from Alras, but have from myself. Today I remembered seeing the creature enter the city from the direction of Norithel.\n\nDay 3. \nI have traveled past Haelun'or and to Norithel, stopped by the water which meets its southern walls. Tomorrow I will find a way around the cliffs. I remember on my first scouting mission, I found a path that lead to swamp lands. This may work.\n\nDay 4.\nI have traveled back from Norithel and then through the path. I am writting this while sitting on top of a broken cart on the edge of the swamp. I will make preparations tonight, for my travel tomorrow.\n\n[!] What is written is damp and smudged.\n\nDay 5.\nI hate the swamp.\n\nDay 6.\nI have found the exit to the swamp, with no luck on my quest. I have found a human settlement. I traveled briefly through it, with little issue. I am writting this from inside of a large tower, to shield myself from the rain.\n\nDay 7.\nI have reached water yet again. At our land's edge I have found a monastary and a large, glowing tree. Near me is a dense forest, with what appears to be a lot of spider activity.\nI am preparing to venture there tomorrow.\n\nDay 8.\nToday I found a tree.\nThis tree was not like any tree I have seen before. It was made completely out of metal, with a black something oozing out of the sides. It made the area around it metallic as well. I spent most of my day investigating this.\n\nDay 9.\nI am writing this from on top of a tomb. I have found the ruins of a gravesite. Bodies have been dug up and disturbed. I could here noises and clawing from some coffins. This place smells of death, and it feels as though life is not welcome here.\n\nDay 10. I have begun venturing back towards the city, to report my findings. On my way back, I found a small cave, with skeletons littered around, and a pile of leaves appearing to serve as a bed. I left before whatever made this their home returned.\n\nDay 11. \nI have returned to the city to give the Div'cruan this journal. I am leaving back to further investigate the gravesite and dwelling. I will not return, until I am sure that I have found, 'it'.\n\nI am alright thus far,\n-Feyko", ""),
        StaticBook("You are", "§bArthur Caulfield", "You are a tumour, taking me out of a perfect life. \n\nYou are a fire, forcing me out of my home.\n\nYou are a thief, stealing all of my fun and joy. \n\nYou are a killer, killing the immersion.\n\nYou are a fly, around my food. \n\nYou are a parasite, leeching onto my head.\n \nYou are my only clothes, disgusting and ripped.\n\nYou are a sword through my still beating heart.\n\nYou are an ass, dirty and unwiped. \n\nYou are a spider, driving everyone away.\n\nYou are a current, desperate to pull everyone down to your level.\n\nYou are a guard, closing down my party. \nYou are an aggressive orc, capable of only one emotion.\n\nYou are porridge, bland and dull.\n\nYou are the crayon nobody uses.\n\nYou are a made up language or fictional history. \n\nYou are a lump in a comfortable pillow.\n\nYou are an obscenity shouted at a deaf man.\n\nYou are a greedy beggar at my door all day.\n\nYou are the corpse of a king who demands a temple for a resting place. \n\nYou are a pot with no soil that demands a flower.\n\nYou are the loose string that undoes a shirt.\n\nYou are the thunder I hear while trying to sleep. \n\nYou are the crack in a large expensive window.\n\nYou are the loose screw in a banks vault door.\n\nYou are a spot on a fresh piece of paper. \n\nYou are a creaky step in a silent house.\n\nYou are a moth among butterflies.\n\nYou are water extinguishing a torch.\n\nYou are dried up ink.\n\nYou are a chicken without a head.\n\nYou are wilted soil in a draught.\n\nYou are a church without a religion.\n\nYou are.", ""),
        StaticBook("GUY.CO INSURANCE", "§bAyche Raven'kor", "ARE YOU PAYING TOO MUCH FOR CART INSURANCE?\n\nSEEK OUT GUY. CO FOR PREMIUM INSURANCE PACKAGES.\n\nYOUR CARTS ARE AN INVESTMENT. PROTECT THEM.\n\n15 MINUTES COULD SAVE YOU 15% OR MORE.", ""),
        StaticBook("Asher's Guide", "§bKatari Stirling", "Beginning lessons -\n\nThe first lesson would consist of teaching said student about the void. The void is a place that consists of everything yet is nothing- a definition that has been spoken countless times throughout time. However if you're teaching a -\n\n- magic to a student it's incredibly likely that you're already aware of the void and how it works. Thefore, we'll move on to the more important teachings of actual fire evocation.\n\nA fire evocation teacher's first lesson should be simple. Incredibly simple. They would need to teach their student how to evoke a simple flame, a flame the size of a candle light; if not smaller. If necessary, the teacher could begin with a spark.\n\nThe teacher would of course explain how this process is done. The student would need to focus on their goal and their goal only- creating a small flame. They'd clear their mind of any distractions and imagine a small ember, wavering and acting just as -\n\n- any other flame would. They'd practice this act until they were capable of performing it without much of an issue.\n\nAfter that lesson would be complete, the teacher would have a bit more freedom. They would be allowed to teach their student how to direct flames in certain directions, or they could teach said student how to sustain a larger flame. The process begins -\n\n- the same way, the student creating a small ember in the void and withdrawling it into the mortal plain. If they were being taught how to create and sustain a larger flame, all they'd have to do is simply imagine their ember slowly swelling and growing -\n\n- in size. This would be quite a tiring process for a beginner of course, and they'd have to practice it for a short while just as they had to practice the sustaining of the ember. If they were learning how to direct their ember, it would also take -\n\n- a good amount of practice to complete. They'd simply imagine the movement of the flame, making sure it acts just as any other normal flame within the realworld, otherwise it would diminish into nothingness.\n\nIt would be best if the student becomes proficent in both of these tasks, as it will make the more advanced things much easier. A good size would be things such as a medium sized ball, and being able to toss the flame at a reasonabley fast speed without -\n\n- it diminishing before it reached its destination.\n\nAfter these tasks are complete, the teacher would begin teaching the more advanced parts of fire evocation. Things such as the temperature of their flame, the speed it is thrown at and even how many balls of flame are created at once; or even creating-\n\n-a torrent of flame and controlling it in different ways. There's something I feel I forgot that was important...\n\n[!] A few things are scribbled here and crossed out.\n\nOh, yes! I forgot that there's a step before all of this you have to take, that step being actually teaching your student the properties of fire. You know, things such as the way it acts, the way it reacts to other things, etc. Here, I'll give a few -\n\n- starters:\n\n- Fire is hot. What a surprise.\n-It can't be controlled to a fine degree, since it's.. fire.\n-Fire wavers and flows, it won't stay still and will always rise upward, so don't be silly and try to create a fire under your hand. Unless you're-\n\n-asking to be burnt.\n-I don't think I mentioned this yet, but fire is hot.\n-Fire is hot.\n-Super hot.\n-Fire will burn you.\n-I'm not kidding.\n\nAlso, white fire will probably kill you. Just a heads up. That's assuming-\n\n-you don't pass out first.\n\nTime between teachings and such- \n\nWe'll go off of a sort of tier system. \n\nTier one would last for about 1-2 weeks.\n\nTier two would last for about 3 more weeks until you reach tier three.\n\nTier three would last for about 1-1 1/2 elven months until tier four is achieved.\n\nTier four would last for a long while. Perhaps 2-3 months until the student masters the magic.\n\nWhat to teach at each tier-\n\nTier one- Begin with the basics. Things as simple as how to create and sustain an ember. Perhaps practicing how to grow it to a slightly larger size. Nothing else should really be-\n\n-practiced however at this stage, as that might be a bit too much for the student.\n\nTier two- Begin teaching your student how to continue the growth of their flame, and also how to start controlling its direction. Once again, nothing-\n\n-too fancy should be taught at this point, only more advanced versions of the basics. (If that makes sense.)\n\nTier three- At this tier the student would obviously continue learning how to further the movement and growth of their flame but-\n\n-they should also begin learning how to launch the flame at high velocities, ones the size of a medium sized ball. They could also begin experimenting with things such as multiple balls of flame the size of apples. Two at once, rather.\n\nTier four- At this tier the teacher should once again continue teaching their student how to increase their size and fluent movement of flames, though it becomes less of a priority. They should teach them about things more advanced. Things  such as-\n\n-evoking larger sized balls of flames at once, tossing it at quicker speeds, using fire in different forms; such as torrents and larger explosions.\n\nAt tier five, the student should be able to create their own usage for the magic and have -\n\n- essentially mastered the art.\n\n          -----\n\nThe teacher should also teach their student at about tier three how they're capable of deciding if the flame is explosive or not, though it takes a longer-\n\n- amount of time to evoke.", ""),
        StaticBook("Pine Tree Art", "§bArchdruid Gi'Garun", "*An elaborate painting of a Pine tree is here*\n\n             --\n           -----\n         ------- \n       ----------\n     ------------ \n    -------------\n   --------------\n  ---------------\n -----------------\n            [ ]", ""),
        StaticBook("Journel Part 1", "Blundermore", "*The Journel is Rustic and worn, it smells foul with many stains that are both known and... unknown. The pages are torn and ruined somewhat and the picture of a skull dots the front of the book.*\n\n-A faded and worn date can be seen here-\n\n\"FOOLISH MORTAL. YOU HAVE RECOVERED THE JOURNEL OF BERTDILE! MASTER MAGNUS OF THE NINE NETHERS! COMMANDER OF THE DEAD (and other things) SORCERER SUPREME AND FEARED BY ALL WHO KNOWS MY NAME. But for you mortal. You, can call me Bert.\n\nFear what you may discover in these pages! I'm warning you! Dark evil things that will toy with your very mind lie within! Don't turn that page. I'm warning you. No, don't do it, I'm an evil maniac! ...\n\nY'know what, fine, be that way, I'm going to spare you the torment of reading my journel by ripping it off from the rest of the book. Bwhaha! *You can see the last \"Bwhaha!\" was scribbled out* Sorry, that sometimes happens... But know this mortal! Dare\n\nnot find the rest of my journel! For your mind will go mad indeed for what you may uncover! This is your one and final warning!\n\nFEAR ME\n\\/\\/\\/\\/\\/\\/\\/\\/\\/\\*You can see that the rest of the Journel has been torn from here*", ""),
        StaticBook("Note-Block Organ", "§bThe Musician", "How To Tune A\n  Note-Block Organ\n\nA note-block organ is a simple instrument, actually composed of several complex boxes which produce a single note each. These boxes can be tuned to produce most any note on the scale.\n\nIn order to tune each box, one must turn a crank once to the right for each step up the octave.\n\nFor simplicity's sake, this book only records the C major scale, but their range begins at F# below C, and can be turned 24 times before returning to F#.\n\nIt is interesting to note that the box's tone changes depending on what material they sit upon. Wood produced a deep, resonating sound, while simple dirt and most other materials creates a sound that is the closest to an actual organ or harp.\n\n     C MAJOR SCALE\n\nC: 6 turns\nD: 8 turns\nE: 10 turns\nF: 11 turns\nG: 13 turns\nA: 15 turns\nB 17 turns\nC: 18 turns", ""),
        StaticBook("Not Poetry", "§bOtaehryn", "Death. Death is a very under appreciated part of nature, which some seem to fear. However, death is necessary for the cycle, for life blossoms each time a life is naturally taken. Let's take the deer for example. A deer may be felled by a wolf and on the\n\nsurface this may look like only the wolf is benefiting, but there's more to it than that. The wolf may drag the carcass to her pups, providing sustenance  to a family. The leftovers may be swarmed by ants or other small bugs in the coming days and will\n\nprovide nutrition to the earth. This isn't the end though, for everything that was aided by the death of that deer will go on to serve its own purpose before eventually dying as well. Thus the cycle continues.", ""),
        StaticBook("Old Man's Dream", "§bMerekM", "'There once was a dream,\nIn this man's heart,\nThat life was never sullied,\nInstead was cherished,\nWould not be perished,\nAnd brought to eternal flame.'\n\nA keep they built,\nSo it was felt,\nA burden they all could carry,\nBut darkness fell,\nIt did compel,\nA burden for none to bury.\n\n\n'New burden made,\nSo lightness fade,\nAnd the man was who to blame.'\n\n\n\n\n-Poem rewritten by Merek Moonstrike. Original creator: Gladius Moonstrike.", ""),
        StaticBook("A Diary & Story", "§bLyra", "This is Violetoins Diary!\n  \n\nIt is my real diary.\n\nYou just bought this book!\n\n\nMany copies will follow if this book sells well. Then i shall start writing useful books!\n\nDear Diary, Lyra is trying to kill me! If you find this i'm probably already dead! \n\nSomeone help! Anyone  just dont let Lyra find me!\n\nOh No, she's coming...\nShe's going to k...\n\nHello, this is Lyra. I like following Violetoin all around. This book might be useless. Indeed it is. Still i shall write a story at the next page. Thanks for shopping!\n\n-=-=-=-=--=-=-=-=-= The Evil Bird\n    \n        &\n          \n          The Alchemist\n------------------- Written by Lyra Moonstone\n-------------------Copyright is probably owned by Lyra.\n\nOnce upon a time, in a little, little town a bird was sitting on a tree next a big wheat field. The wheat field was laying next to a few houses and a blacksmith building. Many people were strolling around carrying logs and stone. The people of Astruagan\n\nwhere building the town since they started it last month.\nThe bird sat in the tree on a twig. It was one of the rare species. The Mad Chicken. Its so rare you can only see it in special trees! So a few days later, someone new joined the town.\n\nHis name is Joseph. He was an Alchemist and made potions for everyone in town. Everybody was healthy until the Alchemist went chopping trees to get more logs for houses. When he started chopping the special tree of the bird Magma\n\nMagma jumped out of the tree, pecking on the man's head. It started to bleed heavily and he screamed for help. Everyone ran to him and helped him. He died later. R.I.P. Joseph\nA message to Magma. Bad bird.\n\n-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=  \n\n\n\n\n         THE END\n\n\n\n-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-", ""),
        StaticBook("Wubberz", "§bKitten", "STURY UF WUBBERZ\n------------------\n\nUkie so wubberz iz muy bluck und whitez kitteh -------------------Wubberz iz verh scury, wullz klompinz uf huy nyydz tu.\n-------------------Wubberz iz verh fury", ""),
        StaticBook("Sunshine Letter", "§bGrand Druid Verden", "Dear Arch Druid Leyun,\n\n   Judging by the large knot on the side of my head and the missing leg of my traveling companion; the Black Hand has returned. They're stalking the roads within Human borders and I have reason to believe they may be getting near\n\nRespiren's grove. Be wary along the roads and spread awareness among the Malinor grove so that our brothers might remain safe.\n\n\n\n        Aspects be with you all,\n                                        Verden", ""),
        StaticBook("Da", "§bMitz", "Tha buuk uv da Goboz:\n\n1. Elweys lomp sumwun iv dey klomp latz.\n2. Elweys bi guud frendz tu utter Orcz en Goboz.\n3. Elweyz travul en paks, et iz nub guud tu fite alown\n4. Elweys flat t'e twiggehs!\n\n5. Tri en bi neis tu dah drewidz ur dey wil klomp latz.\n6. Unli mugg peeple en groopz.\n7. Stek tugithur az Goboz.\n8. Nevur bi neis tu enywun whu latz nub knuw. But unli iv latz haz tuu.\n\nDat iz ebery t'ing abowt beyiing a Gobo.\n\n  Latz frend Gobo's,\n           Mitz en Fitz.\n\nP.S Mey duh Gobo bi within!", ""),
        StaticBook("The guard", "§bArthur Caulfield", "A guard on duty\npulled out his sword\nthreatened an innocent man\n\nThe man cried \"what have I done\" but reason with the guard no one can. \n\nThe guard searched his clothes, took his food and hit him on\n\nthe head. \n\nThe guard shouted \"you quiet now, I kill you and you be dead!\"\n\nThis is when the man realised who he was dealing with. \n\nA guard as dumb as this one could only be a myth.\n\n\"How did you pass training? How have you survived?\" \n\nThe guard simply awnsered \"I stole armour, when the soldiers arrived.\"\n\nThe man slapped the sword away, landing on the floor.\n\nLooking to the guard he asked \"what the hell is all this for?\" \n\nThe guard shrugged his shoulders, drool falling from his chin. \n\nAfter a moment the guard tried to pick his nose, his gigantic fingers not going in.\n\nThen he began to sob, tears falling down his face. \n\nBut to the man, everything seemed to be out of place. \n\nHow could such a bufoon have so much strength?\n\nAnd in order to avoid this people we must go to such great lengths.\n\nTo take away their swords. \n\nAnd point them towards..\n\nA place run by a mayor like that\n\nwhere? You would like to know is such a place as that.\n\nA town not far from petrus known as Fustenberg.", ""),
        StaticBook("Sunshine Letter", "§bGrand Druid Verden", "Dear Arch Druid Leyun,\n\nI'm sending this as a confirmation of my mailing address in the event that anything needs brought to my attention. My mail is kept confidential and locked tight, so feel free to let me know of any developments.\n\n                 ->\n\nPO Box 24 Equinox Island Rd.", ""),
        StaticBook("Desolation", "§bGuru Pinya", "~+~+~+~+~+~+~+~+~\n\n       Desolation\n    By Guru Pinya\n\n   Answer me, Lord,\nout of the goodness\n     of your love;\n In your great mercy\n       turn to me.\n   Do not hide your\n   face from your\n         servant;\n\n   Answer me quickly,\n  for I am in trouble.\n  Come near me and\n      rescue me;\n  Deliver me because\n      of my foes.\n You know how I am\n scorned, disgraced\n     and shamed;\n  All my enemies are\n     before you.\nScorn has broken my\n    heart and has\n\n    left me helpless;\n      I looked for\n  sympathy, but there\n      was none,\n    for comforters,\n  but I found none.\nThey take my tongue\n      And silence\n       my words.\n   May their eyes\n    be darkened,\n\n so they cannot see,\n   and their backs\n   be bent forever.\n   Pour out your \n   wrath on them;\n  let your fierce\nanger overtake them.\n   May their place\n     be deserted;\n   Let there be no\n     one to dwell \n    in their cities.\n\n For they persecute\n  those you wound,\n and talk about the\n    pain of those\n       you hurt.\n  Charge them with\n  crime upon crime;\n   do not let them\n    share in your\n       salvation.\n     May they be\n  blotted out of the\n\n   Book of Life and\n  not be listed with\n    the righteous.\n   But as for me,\nafflicted and in pain-\n       may your\n       salvation,\n  God, protect me.\n     I will praise\n God's name in song\n and glorify him with\n     thanksgiving.", ""),
