        StaticBook("Healing Potions", "§bilikefooddude", "Healing Potion\nA Basic Alchemy Guide\n=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=Ingredients Needed:\n\nThree Bottles of clean water\nOne reed of the Serpent's Stalk plant\nOne large sample of Tippin's Root\n\nMethod\n\n1. Finely chop the Serpent's Stalk and add into the three bottles of water; stirring and infusing until the liquid turns a sickly green colour.\n\n2. Crush the Tippin's Root in a Mortar and Pestle and add to the\n\nLiquid, which should turn a brown or greyish colour when mixed.\n\n3. Commence to boil the potion in a brewing stand for about an hour, or until it takes on something like a rocky texture.\n\n4. Filter out the\n\nChunks of Serpent's Stalk and Tippin's Root with a clean cloth, and transfer the potion (Which now should be brighter and clearer than before, with shifting textures of crumbling grey) to another bottle.\n\nForenote\nDo not drink this potion. Apply directly to wounds with a cloth and bandage quickly. Injuries such as cuts and bruises will heal in a matter of hours, though broken bones will still take a week or two to heal. Consumption will cause nausia,\n\nChoking, and, sometimes death.", ""),
        StaticBook("Application", "§bJentos Of Blackwood", "Application\n---------+---------\nName: Jentos Blackwood\n((Jentos))\nMagics you wish to study: Wind Evocation, Lightning Evocation\n\nGuild you wish to be associated to: The Sanctuary", ""),
        StaticBook("Serpentions Note", "§bSerpention", "Your father, Luthar, has said we should not be together. I find it not to be so for you are the beautiful intellegent aengul of my eye but yet it seems not to be. I wish that we could be in a romance but your father will kill me. I might just go to him\n\nanyway...but thank you for leading me astray from a dark path.\n\n~Serpention, the necromancer expertiment to-be\n\nP.S. I still love you.", ""),
        StaticBook("A widow's letter", "§bStephanie", "[!]A flowery exquisite cursive would read[!]§0\n§0\n§0\"Dear Robin,§0\n§0\n§0Your greed has blinded your eyes. Where has the good man I knew gone? Even now, I grieve for the consequences which befalls such avarice.\n\nThough you may forsake my love, I hope this golden music box finds you well, as your love of gold and jewels overcome even my love for you.§0\n§0\n§0See you in the next world, my once glorious husband.§0\n§0\n§0-Stephanie", ""),
        StaticBook("Shades", "§bEin Sarard", "ＳＨＡＤＥＳ\n        ¨¨¨¨¨¨¨¨¨¨¨¨¨ \n\n\n\n\n            ✴\n\n         ✴    ✴\n\n\n\n¨¨¨¨¨¨¨¨¨¨¨¨¨¨¨¨¨¨¨¨¨¨¨¨¨¨¨¨¨¨¨¨¨¨¨¨¨\n\nShade, the lowest point of magic, and the pinnacle of forbidden arts, is known as a curse and a gift. A Shade is not a person, or a type of magic, but a parasitic being born from Iblees, which in turn grants its host a peculiar skill of conjuring tendrils\n\nShades have been documented through times, ever since Aegis. According to stories and rumours, the first caster to appear was Rilath - with unrivaled prowess. It is said that Iblees themself bound the shade upon him, and so the story of Shades began.\n\nA guild for dabblers in the voidal arts, The Mages Guild in the times of Aegis, got a new apprentice by the name of Rilath. The young caster became an apprentice before anyone knew of his true intention, and more importantly, what he had inside him.\n\nHe began learning about the arcane, but he couldn't handle the mental pressure the Shade put on him through its endless pestering of his very mind. So it wasn't long before chaos ensued, for where shades go, so does disorder and turmoul. What was curious,\n\nis that the Guildmaster at the time, didn't kick Rilath out of the guild as word reached his ears. Mithas, the Guildmaster saw it as an opportunity, and soon was succumbed into what the shade had to offer. One after another, masters of the guild\n\ngave in for the thirst of power, without knowing the consequences. Thus, as early as it was, the guild of magi gained its reputation for having problems with people dabbling into black magic. But the balance in the guild wasn't completely ruined, since\n\nevery tree has a few spoiled fruit too. The Archmage of the guild, now a man of legends, Ambros, didn't take well the unnatural occurence of dark arts in his guild, and set out to punish the young apprentice.\nWithout further notice, so he did.\n\nAmbros caught Rilath, and a heated battle took place, which ended up in the Shade getting defeated. The archmage sealed the shade in a pure, clean crystal - removing its influence from Rilath, essentially creating the Shade Gem. A gem that held the power.\n\nRilath, having had a taste of the power, did not simply give up but in fact stole the gem to regain his prowess. \n\nAfter that, shades kept popping up time after time, but the gem disappeared. Famous figureheads were Lucas Black and Salamandra, who ma\n\nsure that chaos was ever present in this world.\n\nThen, a time of silence and secrecy began, and the Mages Guild became more like a secret organization.\nHighly exclusive, and shrouded in mystery.\n\nLittle did the commonfolk know, a lot of changes took place in the guild of magi.\nIt was a coup of sorts, and many were essentially kicked out.\n\nWyvernym Hold,\ncirca 1440, hundred years later of foretold, the new guildmasters\n\nhad somehow gained posession of the Shade Gem. \n\n-------------------          /\\_\n      /\\i  /\\\n      i  /Y   \\\n      i <  ^   i\n      i  ^   ^ i\n      ¨¨¨¨¨¨¨¨¨¨¨¨¨\n[!] A detailed sketch of the gem was on the page.\n\nThe guild was in shambles, and it could only be guessed what happened to it, as new shades no one had ever heard of popped up.\n\nAfter a small uproar, the magical community fell silent again, until word of the gem from which all shades were\n\ndrawing power from had gotten broken.\n\nThe shades became nothing but a rumour.\n\n-------------------\n\nWhat a shade is capable of, is largely of off rumours, but it is said that one shade can conjure around a dozen of pitch black tendrils. Tentacles of darkness to strangle, cut and tear apart nearly anything in their way. To a person unknown of this, it\n\nwould appear as if they were controlling shadows.\n\nHoly magic, as per usual, clashes with the tainted being. Gold is as sharp to it as it is to a ghoul, but other than that, a counter for a shade isn't known.\n\nHow Ambros managed to seal the first shade remains a mystery too.\n\n-------------------\n\nA person cursed with a shade has many problems however. Their grasp on reality begins slipping, as the being constantly attempts to gain a foothold of the body. Being the parasite it is, the shade tries to control its host, until their will finally gives\n\nin. Thus, it is a curse.\nThe shade never sleeps, and that is why the cursed often suffer intense insomnia. The lack of sleep slowly drives them mad, and then the daemon within takes control - with no going back.\n\nNot to mention them not being fully in control even the whole time they spend awake.\n\nIf you find out that someone carries the curse, it is wise to not make them your enemy, and simply disappear from their sight.\n\nHowever, for several decades, shades haven't been sighted and shall remain mostly alive in the stories and rumours of old.\n\nPerhaps one day, they will return.", ""),
        StaticBook("Writable Book", "", "I have decided to begin taking notes concerning my condition. I do not yet have a name for myself, but something derived from the Zephon Duskcrawler may be apt.\n\nMany things have changed drastically within my body. I am wholly carnivorous, feel the impulse to produce more of my kind, and occasionally desire to devour halflings. Ontop of the internal alterations, I have also grown the lower body of a spider, my\n\nteeth have grown sharp, and what remained of my muscles has shrunk, leaving me weak.", ""),
        StaticBook("Writable Book", "", "7th of The First Seed, 1617§0\n§0\n§0Got someone to teach me transfiguration finally. My first task is to heat up a glass of water, sounds simple but I've barely managed to even alter the damn thing. I wonder if it isn't just the heat from my hands rubbing off.\n\n8th of The First Seed, 1617§0\n§0\n§0I made a breakthrough. Heating water is nothing new, it's as mundane as it gets. Of course it's a bit more complicated to do it magically, but what is magic if not a different way to do something?§0\n§0I heated a flask of water-\n\nand took notes of everything. The flame heats the mass of water contained above it, a transfer of energy ocurring in one direction. If I am to replicate this, then I have to do exactly as the flame does. My own mana needs to become the flame.\n\nKnowing this, I was able to understand what I was doing far better. It was a breeze to figure out the rest. I managed to heat up the water. Just tepid, but it's my best result yet. All I needed was knowing all the pieces that make up the physical process-\n\nto then replicate it with transmutation. I weave the required pieces in the void, then bring them forth with a bit of mana. The rest practically works itself out. Can't wait to get into more complex stuff.\n\n14th of The First Seed, 1617§0\n§0\n§0I bought a clump of strange glowing wax from a peddler. He said it was made out of glowstone, but he was so shifty I can't just take his word for it.§0\n§0Regardless, the wax does glow and I intend to find out how.\n\nMaybe I can learn how to make other things glow if I can, that would be useful.§0\n§0\n§0If I compare this to a different light source, like fire, the differences are made obvious.§0\n§0A flame produces a lot of heat and not so much light. Light is merely a byproduct.\n\nIt is the other way around with this wax. It produces light but only a tiny amount of heat. It is much more efficient than fire as a light source. Still it must have some kind of fuel, just like fire. It still emanates energy, like a slow ember.", ""),
        StaticBook("Writable Book", "", "Resident Logbook§0\n§0\n§0Format:§0\n§0\n§0-Name§0\n§0-Race§0\n§0-Age§0\n§0-Additional Notes\n\n-Junidth Walai§0\n§0-Mali'Aheral§0\n§0-Five and a half centuries§0\n§0-No magical skills, but shows interest in evocation and mental. Has a daughter part of a holy order.", ""),
        StaticBook("The Azdrazi", "§bIcarus Adil", "The Azdrazi\n\n        Haelun'or,\n           Axios\n\n     Published 1592\n\n\n\n     by Icarus Adil\n\nThe Azdrazi\n===================   The Azdrazi are an odd creature, draconic in nature, which have recently returned to some prominence in the land of Axios. Upon their head are long, brown horns, and their skin is unmistakably scaly. Their eyes are orange\n\nand serpentine, their pupils slitted, long and thin. They can retract these fearsome features to a certain extent, though not all choose to. At a glance, the Azdrazi are monsters, and will likely strike fear in the hearts of those who see them.\n\nFortunately, this is false.\n   In this work, it is my hope to spread understanding regarding the Azdrazi, for these misgivings not only pose undue risks to their well-being, yet also hamper them in pursuit of their noble task. The Azdrazi should not be\n\nregarded as monstrosities of evil, or as ne'er do wells of any sort. Instead, they should be treated as neutral creatures pursuant of a righteous task, as I shall explain below.\n   To begin, one must look to the history of the Azdrazi, for their existence\n\npredates most of our own. The creatures first emerged during the Great War, borne with the sole intent of stopping the darkness of the Betrayer Iblees. Much of their history has been lost to time or otherwise destroyed, but it is known that the current\n\niteration of Azdrazi stem from the line formed by Azdromoth, prior to his present affliction.\n   The current brood of Azdrazi began with the stirring of one known as Alistair, the child of Azdromoth, and father of the remaining Azdrazi, at least as far\n\nas is currently known. He returned and began to create other Azdrazi, though the method of doing so is still unknown. Unfortunately, it was not until after this began that they learnt of Azdromoth’s descent to existence as a Drakaar, a fate most Dragaar\n\nescape by confining themselves to stone.\n   With this knowledge, the Azdrazi have severed ties with their progenitor, instead pursuing the goals their very existence is predicated upon. They exist to protect man against the forces of darkness, and\n\nhope to one day find a cure for the blight which affects their father and grandfather, Azdromoth the Arch-Drakaar.\n   Though these creatures have found conflict against the Paladins of Xan, as is natural giving their order's aversion to the dragonkin, it\n\nwould seem a truce may be found. The disciples in the Cult of Azdromoth also combat the Azdrazi, as is natural for those who worship the Arch-Drakaar in its present form. Yet beyond this, the Azdrazi hold no conflict with man, and should indeed be treated\n\nin accordance to their intentions: to protect man against the dark.\n   In conclusion, I encourage all who find themselves in the presence of Azdrazi not only to stay any hostility based on assumptions of their aesthetic, yet also to thank them for their\n\nservice to the realm of mortality. It seems that we all owe them our appreciation, and they are yet to find it.\n\nNotes\n===================\n\nThe information for this piece was gathered through the author's interview of Mister Suldaeroth and Mister Favnir, two Azdrazi who freely gave information regarding their existence. Their support of\n\nintellectual pursuit will not go unforgotten, so long as the information they have offered does not prove false. If you see them, thank them for their service both through this tome, yet also against those who promote darkness in our realm.", ""),
        StaticBook("The Obelisks", "§bLeyu Ward", "The Obelisk Theory:\n\nThis is simple, a very simple theory. we're floating in a void, if you've traveled far enough you'll understand what I mean, and everything on each isle looks like it's been pieced together, a Mesa runs straight into a plain,\n\na desert straight into a mountaineous snow region. The areas look pieced together, my theory is that someoen got a little trigger-happy with their magic, or possibly some Daemon or Aengul, but the Obelisks seem to be sparsely placed but most often found\n\nin habited areas, or corners, there's some in corners of the realm, and others in the middle of the land. My theory is that these obelisks are what keep us suspended in this voidal abyss. I'm not sure why I think this, but I do. I know a few people want\n\nto destroy the obelisks, but if they do I'm afraid it may just make the land drop into the void and kill us all. We did get here through some kind of portal, or so I've heard.", "")
    )

